window.AWS_SEARCH_INDEX=[{"title":"Course Roadmap: AWS for DevOps","lead":"A map of the whole course: what you will learn, in what order, and why each piece matters for someone who wants to work as a DevOps engineer on AWS.","sections":[{"h":"Who this course is for","id":"s1","t":"This course teaches AWS (Amazon Web Services) from zero, through the eyes of a DevOps engineer. You do not need any earlier knowledge of AWS or any other cloud provider. Goal: job-ready skills, not a certificate. The focus is on what DevOps engineers actually do every day, not on memorising exam facts. Theory + practice. Most lessons explain the idea first, then walk through a hands-on demo in the AWS console or terminal. Projects and interview questions appear along the way, so you can talk about real work in interviews. DevOps services only. AWS has 200+ services. We skip areas like machine learning and focus on the services a DevOps engineer uses most. Good newsCloud concepts carry over. If you learn AWS well, moving to Azure or Google Cloud later is mostly about learning new names for the same ideas. Interviewers know this, so being strong in one cloud is enough to start."},{"h":"What does a DevOps engineer do on AWS?","id":"s2","t":"A DevOps engineer sits between the people who write code (developers) and the systems that run it. On AWS, the day-to-day work usually looks like this: Control access Create users and groups, give each person only the permissions they need, and keep the main (root) account locked away. Run servers Launch virtual servers, install tools and applications on them (for example Jenkins), and make them reachable. Design the network Build private networks, decide what is public and what is hidden, and add firewalls so only the right traffic gets in. Automate everything Write templates and scripts so infrastructure is created by code, and build pipelines that test and deploy code automatically. Watch and react Monitor CPU, logs and errors, set alarms, and trigger small functions that fix or report problems automatically. Keep it safe and cheap Store passwords securely, check that resources follow company rules, and clean up anything that wastes money. Notice that you rarely \"just deploy an app\". Around every application you also set up access, networking, security, DNS, monitoring and automation. That is why this course covers all of them."},{"h":"The roadmap at a glance","id":"s3","t":"Lessons build on each other. Early lessons give you an account and the basics; later lessons combine them into real projects. The course follows a snake path: foundations first, then services that build on each other, then advanced projects."},{"h":"Stage by stage","id":"s4","t":"Foundations (Lesson 2). What \"the cloud\" is, private vs public cloud, why companies move to AWS, regions, and creating your own AWS account that you will use for the rest of the course. IAM (Lesson 3). The first real service. You learn how to create users, groups and permissions so that nobody shares the all-powerful root login. EC2 (Lessons 4–5). Launch a virtual server, log into it, install Jenkins, and open it from your laptop's browser. Your first deployed application. Networking and security (Lessons 6–8). Private networks (VPC), firewalls (security groups and NACLs) and DNS (Route 53). Many people find this the hardest part, so it is taught in a cloud-neutral way that also makes sense on other clouds. First production-style project (Lesson 9) and interview scenarios (Lesson 10). Combine everything so far, the way a real team would. Storage and tooling (Lessons 11–13). S3 object storage and static websites, the AWS CLI, and Infrastructure as Code with CloudFormation, so you stop clicking and start scripting. CI/CD (Lessons 14–17). Build a full pipeline with AWS's developer tools: source, build, deploy. Monitoring, serverless and delivery (Lessons 18–21). CloudWatch alarms, Lambda functions, an automated cost-saving project, and CloudFront. Containers (Lessons 22–24). Store images in ECR, run them on ECS, and go deeper on Kubernetes with EKS, which most companies use. Going further (Lessons 25–40). Secrets management, Terraform, compliance checks, load balancers, migration strategies, career tips, and bigger end-to-end projects. Don't skip around too earlySome lessons are deliberately linked. For example the CI/CD lessons (14–17) build one pipeline across four lessons, and the monitoring and Lambda lessons feed into the cost project. Skipping one can break the flow of a project. Try it: build your learning path Pick what you want to get out of the course. You'll get a suggested order of lessons (you can still do everything later). Choose a goal above."},{"h":"Prerequisites","id":"s5","t":"For AWS itself: none. Everything AWS-specific is explained from the start. A few general skills make the journey smoother: Skill Why it helps Needed from Basic Linux commands (cd, ls, chmod, sudo, apt) You will log into Linux servers and install software. Lesson 4 Git basics Pipelines start from a code repository. Lesson 14 Container basics (what Docker images are) Needed for ECR, ECS and EKS. A short primer is in Lesson 22. Lesson 16 A little Python Lambda automation examples use Python, the most common DevOps scripting language. Lesson 19 A debit or credit card AWS asks for one when you open an account, to verify you are a real person. Lesson 2"},{"h":"How to get the most out of it","id":"s6","t":"Do every practical yourself. Watching is not the same as clicking through the console and hitting your first error. Stay in the free tier and clean up. Stop or delete what you create after each lesson. A forgotten server is the most common surprise bill (see Lesson 36). Keep notes and turn projects into resume points. The projects (Lessons 9, 16–17, 20, 24, 26, 32–34) are designed to be discussed in interviews. Practise explaining. Interviews often ask \"how would you…\" scenario questions. After each lesson, try explaining the \"why\" out loud."},{"h":"Check yourself","id":"s7","t":"What is the main focus of this course? The course is built around what DevOps engineers do every day, not exam preparation or every possible service. Which service do you learn first after creating your account, and why? Access comes first. Once you have your own IAM user with the right permissions, every other service can be used safely. You know AWS well but a job uses Azure. How big a problem is that? Concepts like virtual servers, private networks, access control and pipelines exist on every cloud. Interviewers usually accept strong experience in one cloud."},{"h":"Key takeaways","id":"s8","t":"The course starts from zero and is aimed at DevOps jobs, not certificates. A DevOps engineer on AWS handles access, servers, networking, automation, monitoring, security and cost, not just \"deploying apps\". The order matters: account → IAM → EC2 → networking → storage/IaC → CI/CD → monitoring/serverless → containers → advanced projects. No AWS knowledge is needed; basic Linux and Git will help a lot. Practise every demo yourself and clean up afterwards to avoid surprise bills."}],"n":1,"slug":"01-course-roadmap"},{"title":"Introduction to AWS & the Cloud","lead":"What \"the cloud\" really is, how public and private clouds differ, why so many companies use AWS, how AWS spreads its data centers around the world, and how to open your own account.","sections":[{"h":"Before the cloud: data centers","id":"s1","t":"Ten to twenty years ago, if a company wanted to run an application, it bought physical servers (powerful computers built to run software 24/7) from vendors like IBM or HP. Those servers lived in a data center: a room or building full of servers, with: lots of network cables and the company's own network setup, cooling to keep the right temperature, backup power so nothing switches off, a team of people to patch, upgrade and guard everything around the clock. There was also a big waste problem. One server might have 100 CPUs and 100 GB of RAM. If you ran a single small application that needed only 1 CPU and 1 GB, the other 99% sat idle. Need another app? Buy another server."},{"h":"Virtualization: one server, many machines","id":"s2","t":"Virtualization fixes that waste. A piece of software called a hypervisor runs on the physical server and splits it into several virtual machines (VMs). Each VM behaves like its own separate computer with its own operating system, CPU share, memory and disk. Virtualization turns one large, mostly idle server into several fully used virtual machines."},{"h":"So what is \"the cloud\"?","id":"s3","t":"Once servers are virtualized, VMs can be created on request and handed out over the network. A developer asks for five servers, an admin creates five VMs and sends back their IP addresses. The developer uses them without knowing (or caring) which building or rack they physically live in. That is the core idea of cloud computing: computing resources (servers, storage, databases and more) delivered on demand over a network, while the physical hardware stays somewhere out of sight, \"in the cloud\". Private, public and hybrid cloud Private cloud A company builds this cloud setup inside its own data center, only for its own staff. Its own team buys, runs and secures everything, often with platforms like OpenStack or VMware. Banks and others with very sensitive data still do this. Public cloud A provider such as AWS, Microsoft Azure or Google Cloud owns huge data centers worldwide and rents resources to anyone with an account. You pay only for what you use (\"pay as you go\"). Hybrid cloud A mix: some systems stay in the company's own data center (for example, a sensitive database), while others run on a public cloud. Common during a slow move to the cloud. On-premises (or \"on-prem\") simply means \"in our own building, on our own hardware\", which is where private clouds live. Think of it like…Owning a car vs. using a taxi app. Owning (private cloud) gives full control, but you pay for fuel, insurance, repairs and parking even when it sits unused. The taxi (public cloud) is there when you need it, someone else does the maintenance, and you pay per ride. \"Isn't public cloud less secure?\"A common worry is that your servers share hardware with other companies. AWS keeps customers isolated from each other, and you can build your own private, walled-off network inside AWS. That is a VPC, covered in Lesson 6."},{"h":"Why public cloud is so popular","id":"s4","t":"Most people assume the main reason is cost. Cost matters, but the first reason is usually maintenance. No maintenance overhead. A 50-person startup would need several people just to run a data center: patching servers, fixing hardware, watching for power loss and attacks, 24/7. On a public cloud you create an account, give your team access, and start building. Cost and pay-as-you-go. No large upfront purchase. Turn servers off at night or during holidays and you stop paying for them. Providers buy hardware at enormous scale, so it's cheaper per unit. Speed and choice. Get a server in minutes instead of waiting weeks for hardware delivery. AWS started with a handful of offerings (virtual servers, storage) and now has more than 200 services (ready-made products such as databases or managed Kubernetes). Is everyone leaving the cloud? You may hear about cloud repatriation: companies moving from public cloud back to their own data centers, usually because of security rules or because they didn't get the savings they expected. It is real but small, a tiny percentage of companies. Startups and mid-size companies especially can't afford to build and staff their own data centers, so cloud adoption keeps growing."},{"h":"Why AWS?","id":"s5","t":"First mover. AWS pioneered public cloud. Many companies started their cloud journey on AWS over a decade ago and stayed. Largest market share, followed by Azure and then Google Cloud. More companies on AWS means more AWS job openings. Skills transfer. The concepts are the same across clouds; names, screens and some details differ. Learning AWS first is a safe bet."},{"h":"Regions and Availability Zones","id":"s6","t":"AWS's data centers are spread across the world, and you choose where your resources live. A Region is a geographic area with AWS data centers, such as Mumbai (ap-south-1), Frankfurt (eu-central-1) or N. Virginia (us-east-1). Regions are independent of each other. An Availability Zone (AZ) is one or more separate data centers inside a region, with its own power and networking. A region has several AZs, named with a letter: us-east-1a, us-east-1b, … One region, several isolated AZs. Running copies of your app in two or more AZs keeps it online if one AZ fails. How to choose a region Law and data rules. A European bank may require that its data never leaves Europe, so you'd pick Frankfurt or Ireland. Latency (the delay for a request to travel to the server and back). Put servers close to your users. A US customer hitting a server in Mumbai will feel the delay. Price and features. Prices differ a little between regions, and the newest services sometimes appear in a few regions first. Why AZs exist: a fire, flood or power cut can take down a data center. If your app runs in only one AZ, it goes down too. Spreading copies across AZs gives you high availability (staying up even when parts fail). TipMost AWS resources belong to one region, and the console shows one region at a time (selector in the top-right). If a server you created \"disappeared\", check that you are looking at the right region. While learning, pick one region near you and keep everything there. Some newer regions are \"opt-in\" and must be enabled before use. Try it: pick a region, survive an outage Part 1. Pick a scenario to see which region fits and why. Choose a scenario. Part 2. Tap the AZs where your app runs, then cause an outage. Outage hits: Your app runs in us-east-1a only."},{"h":"Who is responsible for what?","id":"s7","t":"Moving to the cloud doesn't hand over all the work. AWS calls this the shared responsibility model: AWS secures the cloud Buildings, hardware, power, cooling, the physical network and the virtualization layer. You secure what's in the cloud Who can log in, your data, firewall rules, your server's operating system updates and your application."},{"h":"Create your AWS account","id":"s8","t":"You'll use one account for the whole course. The sign-up screens change from time to time, but the steps are roughly: Go to the AWS website and choose Create an AWS account. Enter an email address and an account name. This email becomes the root user, the account owner with unlimited power (more in Lesson 3). A fresh email just for AWS is a good idea. The account name can be changed later. Verify the email with the code AWS sends you, then set a strong password. Choose Personal (for learning), then enter your name, phone number and address. The address is used for billing and tax. Add a debit or credit card. AWS makes a tiny temporary charge (around 1 USD or the local equivalent) to confirm the card is real, and releases it. This stops people from creating spam accounts. Not every card is accepted; use a proper card from a bank rather than \"virtual card\" workarounds. Some countries ask for tax details (for example a PAN in India). Then verify your phone and pick the free Basic support plan. Watch out: the free tier has limitsAWS's free tier lets you learn at little or no cost, but only within limits (for example, a set number of hours of a small server per month, or a pool of credits on newer accounts). Go beyond them and the charges are billed to your card. Always pick resources marked \"Free tier eligible\", delete what you don't need, and set a budget alert (see Lesson 36). Free tier terms change, so check AWS's current Free Tier page. A quick console tour The AWS Management Console is the website where you click to create and manage resources. Search bar (top): type a service name like \"EC2\" or \"IAM\" to jump straight to it. Region selector (top right): the region you're currently working in. Account menu (top right): account ID, billing, security credentials, sign out. Recently visited and Favorites: shortcuts to services you use often. Clicking around isn't the only way in; later you'll also drive AWS from the terminal with the AWS CLI."},{"h":"Check yourself","id":"s9","t":"What is the main difference between a private and a public cloud? Both use virtualization. The difference is who owns and runs the data centers, and who is allowed to request resources. Your app runs on one server in us-east-1a. That AZ loses power. What happens, and what's the fix? AZs are isolated so one failure doesn't take down the others, but only if your app actually runs in more than one AZ. According to the lesson, what's usually the first reason startups choose public cloud? Cost matters, but avoiding the 24/7 work of buying, patching, cooling and guarding servers is the bigger win."},{"h":"Key takeaways","id":"s10","t":"Virtualization splits one physical server into many VMs; the cloud delivers such resources on demand over a network. Private cloud = your own data center for your own staff; public cloud = a provider rents resources to anyone; hybrid = a mix. Public cloud wins mainly by removing maintenance work, then by pay-as-you-go cost and speed. AWS leads the market (first mover), so it has the most jobs, and the concepts transfer to other clouds. Regions are geographic areas; AZs are isolated data centers inside a region. Pick regions for law and latency, and use several AZs for high availability. Stay inside free-tier limits and clean up: overage charges go to your card."}],"n":2,"slug":"02-intro-to-aws"},{"title":"IAM: Who Can Do What","lead":"IAM (Identity and Access Management) decides who can get into your AWS account and what they are allowed to do once inside. It's the first service every DevOps engineer sets up, and one you'll use constantly.","sections":[{"h":"A bank, two checks","id":"s1","t":"Picture a bank. It has a service desk, an employee area, and a vault full of documents and cash. To keep it safe, the bank runs two separate checks: Can you come in at all? Only known people (customers, staff) may enter the building. Once inside, where can you go and what can you do? A customer can use the service desk. An employee can use their laptop. Only a few senior staff can open the vault. Without these checks, anyone could walk straight into the vault. These two checks have names: Authentication \"Who are you?\" Proving your identity, for example with a username and password. Getting through the front door. Authorization \"What are you allowed to do?\" The permissions you have after you're in. Which rooms you can enter and what you can touch. IAM is the AWS service that handles both for your AWS account."},{"h":"The root user: the master key","id":"s2","t":"When you created your account in Lesson 2, you signed up with an email address. That login is the root user. It can do everything: delete any server, wipe any database, close the account. Now imagine a company with 1,000 employees sharing that one root login. Someone, by mistake or on purpose, opens the database service and deletes production data. The data may be gone for good, and because everyone used the same login, nobody can even tell who did it. Rule #1: protect and don't use the root userNever share root credentials. Turn on MFA for root right away, and use it only for the few tasks that truly need it (such as some billing and account settings). For daily work, create IAM users. While you're learning in your own practice account, using root briefly is tolerable, but build the habit early."},{"h":"The four building blocks","id":"s3","t":"IAM has four main parts. Each solves a specific problem. 1. Users (authentication) An IAM user is an identity for one person (or one tool) inside your account, with its own login. When employee #501 joins and needs AWS access, the DevOps engineer creates an IAM user for them. Now every action can be traced to a specific person. A user with no permissions can sign in but can't do anything useful, like being let into the bank lobby and then stopped at every door. 2. Policies (authorization) A policy is a document that lists permissions: which actions are allowed (or denied) on which resources. You attach policies to users, groups or roles. Example: \"user 501 can read the database but not delete anything from it.\" AWS managed policies: ready-made by AWS, such as AmazonS3ReadOnlyAccess or AmazonS3FullAccess. Great for common cases. Customer managed (custom) policies: written by you when you need something more specific, such as access to one particular storage bucket only. 3. Groups (less repetitive work) People join and leave companies all the time. Attaching the same five policies to every new developer is slow and easy to get wrong. Instead you create groups (for example Developers, QA, DB-Admins, Others), attach policies to the group once, and drop each new user into the right group. Even better: if all developers later need a new permission (say, to list servers), you add it to the group once and every member gets it, instead of editing four, forty or four hundred users. 4. Roles (temporary access for services and outsiders) Sometimes the thing that needs access isn't a person. An application running on a server needs to read files from storage. A tool in another AWS account needs to deploy into yours. For these cases you use a role. A role has policies like a user does, but no permanent password or keys. Someone or something trusted \"assumes\" the role and receives temporary credentials that expire automatically (typically within an hour or a few hours). If those credentials leak, they soon stop working. That's much safer than baking a permanent password into an app. Typical uses: an EC2 server reading from S3, a Lambda function writing logs, a CI/CD pipeline deploying code, or access between two AWS accounts. You'll use roles in practice in the CI/CD lessons (Lesson 17). Think of it like…A user is an employee ID card with your name on it. A role is a visitor badge from the front desk: it grants specific access and stops working at the end of the day. Users join groups, policies attach to groups (or directly to users). Apps and services use roles instead of permanent credentials."},{"h":"What a policy looks like","id":"s4","t":"Policies are written in JSON (a simple text format of keys and values). Here is the core of AWS's managed AmazonS3FullAccess policy, simplified: { \"Version\": \"2012-10-17\", \"Statement\": [ { \"Effect\": \"Allow\", \"Action\": \"s3:*\", \"Resource\": \"*\" } ] } Field Meaning Version The policy language version. Always \"2012-10-17\" (it's a fixed value, not today's date). Statement A list of rules. Each rule has the three fields below. Effect Allow or Deny. Action Which API actions, written service:Action. s3:* means every S3 action; s3:List* means every S3 action starting with \"List\". Resource Which things it applies to. * means all; an ARN (Amazon Resource Name, a unique ID for one resource) narrows it down. A custom policy that only lets someone read files from one bucket might look like this: { \"Version\": \"2012-10-17\", \"Statement\": [ { \"Effect\": \"Allow\", \"Action\": [\"s3:ListBucket\", \"s3:GetObject\"], \"Resource\": [ \"arn:aws:s3:::my-app-bucket\", \"arn:aws:s3:::my-app-bucket/*\" ] } ] } How AWS decides: allow or deny? Everything starts as denied. If no policy says \"Allow\", the answer is no (an \"implicit deny\"). An explicit \"Deny\" always wins. If any attached policy denies the action, it's blocked, even if another policy allows it. Otherwise, an \"Allow\" lets it through. Permissions from the user's own policies and from all their groups add up. Try it: IAM policy evaluator Pick a user and an action. Toggle the extra policies and see how AWS decides. User Action Attach AmazonEC2FullAccess to the Developers group Attach a Deny s3:DeleteBucket policy to dev-501 Result appears here."},{"h":"Hands-on: users, policies and groups","id":"s5","t":"This is the practical flow you'd follow in the console. Do it yourself in your account. Step A: create a user (authentication only) Sign in, search for IAM in the console, open Users → Create user. Name it, e.g. test-user-501. Tick Provide user access to the AWS Management Console and choose I want to create an IAM user. (The other option, IAM Identity Center, is AWS's recommended way to manage many people across many accounts; you can ignore it for now.) Choose an auto-generated password and tick Users must create a new password at next sign-in. You don't invent passwords for people, and they set their own on first login. Skip permissions for now and create the user. AWS automatically attaches one policy, IAMUserChangePassword, so the user can set their new password. Copy or download the .csv with the username, password and the console sign-in URL, which contains your 12-digit account ID: https://<account-id>.signin.aws.amazon.com/console. Sign out, then sign in as an IAM user using the account ID, username and password. Set a new password. Now open S3 and try to list buckets: you get an access denied error saying you need s3:ListAllMyBuckets. Launching an EC2 server fails too. You're authenticated, but not authorized. Step B: add a policy (authorization) Sign back in as an admin, open the user, choose Add permissions → Attach policies directly. Search \"S3\" and pick AmazonS3ReadOnlyAccess (view only) or AmazonS3FullAccess (view, create, delete). Sign in as the test user again. Now listing buckets works, and with full access, so does creating one. (Bucket names must be unique across all of AWS, so pick something unusual.) Step C: use a group instead Go to User groups → Create group, name it development, attach AmazonS3FullAccess. Add your test users to the group. Later, when the team asks for server access, open the group and attach AmazonEC2FullAccess. Every member gets it at once. Clean upWhen you finish practising, delete test users and groups you don't need. Unused logins are a security risk even when they cost nothing."},{"h":"Best practices","id":"s6","t":"Least privilege. Give each person or app only the permissions they need for their job, nothing more. Start small and add when needed. A new junior engineer might not get delete rights at all. Turn on MFA (multi-factor authentication). After the password, the user must also enter a short code from an authenticator app or tap a security key. A stolen password alone is then not enough. Enable it for root first, then for every human user. One identity per person. Never share logins, so you always know who did what. Manage permissions through groups, not user by user. Use roles for applications and AWS services instead of storing long-lived passwords or access keys in code. (Access keys for the command line are covered in Lesson 12.) Interview angle\"Do you use the root user?\" Good answer: \"No. Root is locked down with MFA. Our DevOps team members each have their own IAM identity with admin-level access; developers and QA get IAM users placed in groups with least-privilege policies; applications use IAM roles. That way every action is traceable to a person.\""},{"h":"Check yourself","id":"s7","t":"A new IAM user can sign in but sees \"access denied\" everywhere. What's missing? Signing in proves who they are (authentication). They still need policies, directly or through a group, that allow actions (authorization). An app on an EC2 server needs to read files from S3. What's the best way to give it access? Roles give temporary credentials that rotate automatically, so nothing permanent is stored in the code. A user's group allows s3:*, but a policy on the user says Deny s3:DeleteBucket. Can they delete a bucket? IAM checks for any explicit Deny first. If one matches, the action is blocked no matter how many Allows exist."},{"h":"Key takeaways","id":"s8","t":"Authentication = who you are; authorization = what you may do. IAM handles both. The root user can do everything: protect it with MFA, never share it, don't use it day to day. Users are identities, policies (JSON with Effect / Action / Resource) are permissions, groups save repeated work, roles give temporary access to apps and services. Default is deny; an explicit Deny beats any Allow. Follow least privilege and enable MFA for every human."}],"n":3,"slug":"03-iam"},{"title":"EC2: Virtual Servers","lead":"EC2 lets you rent a virtual server from AWS in minutes. You'll learn what it is, why companies use it, how to pick the right type, and then launch one and deploy Jenkins on it so you can open it from your own browser.","sections":[{"h":"What is EC2?","id":"s1","t":"EC2 stands for Elastic Compute Cloud (two Cs, hence \"EC2\"). Each word means something: Compute A bundle of CPU, memory (RAM) and disk. In other words, a server. Each one you launch is called an instance. Cloud It runs in AWS's data centers, not yours. AWS uses virtualization to carve its big physical servers into many virtual machines and hands you one. Elastic You can scale it up or down: make it bigger, smaller, add disk, or add more instances. AWS uses \"Elastic\" in many service names for this reason (Elastic Load Balancing, Elastic Kubernetes Service, Elastic Beanstalk…). So an EC2 instance is simply a virtual server you rent from AWS, which you can resize when your needs change."},{"h":"Why use EC2 instead of your own servers?","id":"s2","t":"You could buy physical servers, install a hypervisor, and hand out VMs yourself. Creating 1,000 VMs with a script is the easy part. The hard part comes after: keeping the hardware and virtualization layer patched and upgraded, checking for security problems, fixing things when a server dies at 2 a.m. and developers can't work. With EC2, AWS handles the physical hardware and the virtualization layer. Two big wins: Less maintenance. No hardware to look after. Lower cost, pay as you go. AWS buys hardware at huge scale, and you pay only while an instance runs. Nobody using the test servers at night or over the holidays? Stop them and stop paying for compute. NoteAWS manages the hardware, but you still look after what runs inside the instance: operating system updates, installed software and your app. See the shared responsibility model in Lesson 2."},{"h":"Instance types: pick the right shape","id":"s3","t":"Just like buying a physical server, you choose what it's strong at. AWS groups instance types into families: Family Strong at Typical uses Example General purpose A balance of CPU, memory and network Web servers, dev/test, small apps, tools like Jenkins t3.micro, m7i.large Compute optimized More CPU power per GB of memory Gaming servers, batch processing, ML inference, video encoding c7i.xlarge Memory optimized Lots of RAM In-memory databases and caches, real-time big-data analytics r7i.2xlarge Storage optimized Very fast local disks Large databases, data warehouses, log processing i4i.large Accelerated computing GPUs or special chips Training ML models, graphics rendering g5.xlarge, p5.48xlarge Throughout this course you'll use small general purpose instances. The launch steps are identical for every type; only the type you pick (and the price) changes. Bigger and specialized types cost more. AWS adds new types over time, so check the official \"Amazon EC2 instance types\" page when choosing for real. Reading an instance type name A name like m7g.2xlarge breaks down as: m: the family (general purpose) 7: the generation (higher = newer, usually better value) g: optional attributes (here: AWS Graviton, an ARM-based processor) 2xlarge: the size. Roughly, each step up (large → xlarge → 2xlarge → 4xlarge…) doubles CPU and memory. Try it: instance type picker and decoder Pick a workload to see which family fits. Choose a workload. Decode a name. Type any instance type. Result appears here."},{"h":"Where does it run?","id":"s4","t":"Every instance lives in a region (e.g. Mumbai, Frankfurt) and inside one Availability Zone of that region. Pick regions close to your users or required by data rules, and spread important apps across AZs. Regions and Availability Zones are explained in Lesson 2: Introduction to AWS."},{"h":"The pieces you choose at launch","id":"s5","t":"AMI: the starting disk image An AMI (Amazon Machine Image) is a template for the instance's disk: the operating system plus any pre-installed software. AWS offers AMIs for Ubuntu, Amazon Linux, Red Hat, Debian, SUSE, Windows and more, and each comes in several versions. You can also build your own AMI with your app already installed, so new servers start ready to go. Key pair: how you log in By default, Linux instances on AWS have password login turned off. Instead you use a key pair: two matching keys. The public key is placed on the instance by AWS. The private key (a .pem file) is downloaded once to your computer. Only you have it. When you connect, the server checks that your private key matches its public key. Share the public key freely; never share the private key. AWS doesn't keep a copy, so if you lose it you can't download it again. How to actually connect with the key (SSH, MobaXterm, PuTTY, file permissions) is covered in Lesson 5: Connecting to EC2. Public and private IP addresses Public IP: reachable from the internet. You use it to log in and to open your app from your laptop. Private IP: only works inside AWS's network. Servers use it to talk to each other. The automatic public IP changes if you stop and start the instance. (A fixed one, called an Elastic IP, is available if you need it.) Private networks and subnets are explained in Lesson 6: VPC. Free tier and cost Always pick options marked \"Free tier eligible\". Under the classic free tier, that's a small instance (t2.micro or t3.micro, depending on the region) for 750 hours per month, enough to run one instance all month. Two instances running all month use 1,500 hours, so you'd pay for the extra. Newer accounts may get a credit-based free plan instead; check what your account shows. Stop vs terminateStop = switch off. You stop paying for compute, but the disk is kept (and storage is still billed beyond free limits). Terminate = delete the instance for good. When you finish practising, terminate what you don't need."},{"h":"Hands-on: launch an instance and deploy Jenkins","id":"s6","t":"Jenkins is a popular automation server (a Java web application) used for CI/CD. It makes a perfect first app for a DevOps engineer. You log in over SSH (port 22) and open Jenkins in a browser on port 8080. The firewall (security group) must allow both ports. 1. Launch the instance In the console, search for EC2, open Instances, click Launch instance. Name: e.g. my-first-instance. AMI: choose Ubuntu, a free-tier-eligible version. Instance type: the free-tier-eligible micro type. Key pair: Create new key pair, type RSA, format .pem, give it a name like aws-login. The file downloads automatically. Keep it safe. Network settings: leave the defaults for now (they allow SSH). Networking is covered properly in Lessons 6–7. Storage: the default is a small disk (about 8 GB). That's disk space, not RAM, and enough for this demo. Click Launch instance and wait until the state shows Running. Open the instance and copy its Public IPv4 address. 2. Connect to it On Mac or Linux (or Windows PowerShell), from the folder with your key: chmod 400 aws-login.pem ssh -i aws-login.pem ubuntu@<PUBLIC-IP> ubuntu is the default login name on Ubuntu AMIs. If you see \"permissions are too open\", the chmod line fixes it. Windows tools and every error message are explained in Lesson 5. Once in, whoami prints ubuntu. Use sudo for admin commands (or sudo su - to become root on the server). 3. Install Java and Jenkins # refresh the package list (always do this on a new server) sudo apt update # Jenkins needs Java sudo apt install -y fontconfig openjdk-21-jre java -version # add the Jenkins apt repository: copy the current two commands # (download key + add repo) from jenkins.io → Installing Jenkins → Linux → Debian/Ubuntu sudo apt update sudo apt install -y jenkins # check it is running sudo systemctl status jenkins TipJenkins' required Java version and its repository key change over time, so always copy the repository commands from the official Jenkins install page instead of an old blog post. Also, a micro instance has only about 1 GB of RAM, so Jenkins will be slow. That's fine for a demo. 4. Open port 8080 so your browser can reach it Jenkins listens on port 8080. Browse to http://<PUBLIC-IP>:8080 and… nothing loads. AWS blocks incoming traffic by default; only SSH was allowed at launch. Open the instance → Security tab → click its security group (the instance's firewall). Edit inbound rules (inbound = traffic coming in to the instance) → Add rule. Type Custom TCP, port 8080, source My IP. (\"Anywhere IPv4\", 0.0.0.0/0, also works but opens it to the whole internet.) Save. Security groups, inbound vs outbound rules, and NACLs are explained in Lesson 7: Security Groups vs NACLs. 5. Unlock Jenkins Reload http://<PUBLIC-IP>:8080. Jenkins asks for an initial admin password, which it saved on the server: sudo cat /var/lib/jenkins/secrets/initialAdminPassword Paste it in, and the Jenkins setup screen appears. You've just deployed your first application on AWS and reached it from the outside world. When you're done, stop or terminate the instance so it doesn't eat your free hours."},{"h":"Check yourself","id":"s7","t":"Jenkins is running on the instance, but http://IP:8080 won't load in your browser. Most likely cause? Incoming traffic is blocked unless a security group rule allows it. Add an inbound Custom TCP rule for port 8080. You need to run a large in-memory cache. Which family fits best? In-memory caches and databases need lots of RAM, which is what memory optimized types offer. What does an AMI provide? The AMI is the disk template. CPU and RAM come from the instance type; the key pair handles login."},{"h":"Key takeaways","id":"s8","t":"EC2 (Elastic Compute Cloud) gives you resizable virtual servers; AWS handles the hardware, you handle what runs inside. Pick an instance family by workload: general purpose, compute, memory, storage or accelerated. Names read as family + generation + attributes + size. A launch needs a name, an AMI (OS image), an instance type, a key pair, network settings and storage. Log in with your private key over SSH; reach apps on the public IP, but only after a security group rule opens the port. Stay on free-tier types and stop or terminate instances when you're done."}],"n":4,"slug":"04-ec2"},{"title":"Connecting to EC2 from Windows","lead":"Log into your Linux server from a Windows laptop in a few minutes, using MobaXterm, PuTTY or the SSH client built into Windows. You'll also learn how SSH keys work and how to fix the most common connection errors.","sections":[{"h":"How SSH works, in one minute","id":"s1","t":"SSH (Secure Shell) is the standard way to open a remote terminal on a Linux server. Everything you type is encrypted on its way across the internet. By default it uses port 22 (a port is a numbered \"door\" on a server; each service listens on its own). On EC2, SSH uses the key pair you created when launching the instance (Lesson 4): AWS puts the public key on the server, in the file ~/.ssh/authorized_keys of the default user. You keep the private key file on your laptop. When you connect, the server sends a challenge that only the matching private key can answer. The private key itself never leaves your computer. Think of it like…The public key is a padlock you can hand out to anyone. The private key is the only key that opens it. AWS fits your padlock to the server's door; you keep the key in your pocket. Four things must line up: the right private key, the right username, the public IP, and a firewall rule allowing port 22."},{"h":"Before you connect: checklist","id":"s2","t":"Instance is \"Running\" and has a public IPv4 address. At launch, check under Network settings → Edit that \"Auto-assign public IP\" is enabled. SSH is allowed in the instance's security group (inbound rule for port 22, ideally from \"My IP\"). The launch wizard adds this by default. More in Lesson 7. You have the private key file that matches the key pair chosen at launch. It's usually in your Downloads folder. You know the default username, which depends on the AMI (see table below). It's not your AWS login. AMI (operating system) Default username Ubuntu ubuntu Amazon Linux ec2-user Red Hat (RHEL) ec2-user SUSE ec2-user Debian admin"},{"h":".pem or .ppk?","id":"s3","t":"When you create a key pair, AWS asks for a file format: .pem The standard OpenSSH format. Works with MobaXterm, the ssh command on Windows/Mac/Linux, and almost everything else. Pick this if unsure. .ppk PuTTY's own format. Pick it only if you'll use PuTTY. Already have a .pem? Convert it with PuTTYgen (comes with PuTTY)."},{"h":"Option 1: MobaXterm (recommended for Windows)","id":"s4","t":"MobaXterm is a free Windows terminal with a built-in SSH client, saved sessions and a file browser. It's friendlier than PuTTY and uses .pem files directly. Search \"download MobaXterm\", choose the free Home Edition, and download the Installer version (easier than the portable one). The download is a .zip. Right-click → Extract All, open the extracted folder, run the installer, accept and finish. Open MobaXterm → Session → SSH. Remote host: your instance's public IP. Tick Specify username and enter ubuntu (or the right user for your AMI). Open Advanced SSH settings → tick Use private key → browse to your .pem file. Click OK. On the first connection, accept the server's host key. You're in. Try sudo apt update. The session is saved in the left panel, so next time it's one double-click. (If you stop and start the instance, its public IP changes, so edit the session.)"},{"h":"Option 2: PuTTY","id":"s5","t":"Install PuTTY (it includes PuTTYgen). If your key is .pem: open PuTTYgen → Load (set the file filter to \"All files\") → pick the .pem → Save private key as .ppk. In PuTTY, set Host Name to ubuntu@<PUBLIC-IP>, port 22. Go to Connection → SSH → Auth → Credentials and choose your .ppk as the private key file. (Optional) Back in Session, give it a name and Save. Then click Open and accept the host key."},{"h":"Option 3: the ssh command (no install)","id":"s6","t":"Windows 10 and 11 ship with an OpenSSH client, the same ssh command Mac and Linux users have. Open PowerShell in the folder with your key: ssh -i .\\aws-login.pem ubuntu@<PUBLIC-IP> -i means \"identity file\", i.e. which private key to use. Then comes username@address. Key file permissions SSH refuses a private key that other users on your computer could read, with a warning like \"UNPROTECTED PRIVATE KEY FILE\" or \"permissions are too open\". Lock it down so only you can read it. Mac / Linux: chmod 400 aws-login.pem # owner can read; nobody else can do anything Windows PowerShell: icacls.exe .\\aws-login.pem /reset icacls.exe .\\aws-login.pem /grant:r \"$($env:USERNAME):(R)\" icacls.exe .\\aws-login.pem /inheritance:r (chmod 600 also works on Mac/Linux: owner read + write.) MobaXterm and PuTTY handle this for you. Try it: SSH command builder Fill in your details to get the exact commands or settings to use. Your tool AMI Key file Public IP Enter your instance's public IP."},{"h":"When it doesn't work","id":"s7","t":"Message Usual cause Fix Connection timed out Traffic never reaches the server Check the instance is running, you're using the public IP, and the security group allows port 22 from your IP. Permission denied (publickey) Server rejected your login Wrong username for the AMI, or the wrong key file (not the one chosen at launch). UNPROTECTED PRIVATE KEY FILE Key file is readable by others Run chmod 400 (Mac/Linux) or the icacls commands (Windows). Are you sure you want to continue connecting? First time seeing this server Normal. Type yes. The server's fingerprint is saved for next time. Worked yesterday, not today You stopped/started the instance The public IP changed. Copy the new one from the console. Tip: no key handy?For a quick check, the EC2 console's Connect button offers EC2 Instance Connect, a terminal in your browser. It works on supported AMIs (like Ubuntu and Amazon Linux) when port 22 is reachable from AWS's Instance Connect service. Learning a real SSH client is still worth it: you'll use it daily. Watch outTreat the private key like a password. Don't email it, commit it to Git or paste it into chat. If it leaks, create a new key pair and replace the public key on the server."},{"h":"Check yourself","id":"s8","t":"You run ssh -i key.pem ec2-user@1.2.3.4 against an Ubuntu instance and get \"Permission denied (publickey)\". Most likely fix? \"Permission denied\" means you reached the server (so the port is open) but the login was rejected. Ubuntu AMIs use ubuntu. Which key file format does PuTTY need? PuTTY uses its own .ppk format. MobaXterm and the ssh command use .pem directly. The connection just hangs and then says \"Connection timed out\". What should you check first? A timeout means packets aren't reaching the server at all, which is usually a firewall rule or the wrong/missing public IP."},{"h":"Key takeaways","id":"s9","t":"SSH gives you an encrypted remote terminal on port 22; EC2 uses key pairs instead of passwords. The server holds the public key; you keep the private key (.pem for most tools, .ppk for PuTTY). On Windows, use MobaXterm (easiest), PuTTY, or the built-in ssh in PowerShell. The command is ssh -i key.pem user@public-ip, and the user depends on the AMI (ubuntu, ec2-user, admin…). Lock down key permissions; \"timed out\" = network/firewall, \"permission denied\" = wrong user or key."}],"n":5,"slug":"05-connect-to-ec2"},{"title":"VPC: Your Private Network","lead":"A VPC (Virtual Private Cloud) is your own fenced-off network inside AWS. In this lesson you'll learn why it exists, what its parts are (IP ranges, subnets, internet gateway, route tables, NAT gateway), and how a request from the internet finds its way to your server.","sections":[{"h":"Why do we need a VPC?","id":"s1","t":"Picture a big piece of land in a village. A clever builder buys it and offers a deal to people who don't want to build or maintain their own homes: \"Tell me what you need, pay me, and I'll build and look after the house for you.\" Business is good — but to save money the builder puts all the houses right next to each other with no walls between them. If a burglar gets into one house, it's easy to walk into the next one. The careful customers complain, so the builder invents something better: a gated community. Each group of houses gets its own fenced property. There is a single gate where visitors come in. Inside, signposts tell visitors which path leads to which house. At each house a guard checks: \"Are you really allowed to see this person?\" Think of it like…AWS is the builder. Its data centers are the land. In the early days, servers from many different companies could sit side by side with weak separation, so one hacked customer was a risk to others. The VPC is the gated community: a logically isolated network that belongs only to you. The gate is the internet gateway, the signposts are route tables, and the guards are security groups. Unlike the builder story, AWS doesn't design your community for you. AWS gives you the building blocks and documentation; DevOps engineers are the ones who create and configure the VPC for their project. NoteEvery AWS account comes with a default VPC in each region so you can launch an EC2 instance straight away (that's what happened in Lesson 4). Real companies almost always build their own custom VPCs so they control every setting."},{"h":"Sizing a VPC: IP ranges and CIDR","id":"s2","t":"Land is measured in acres. A VPC is measured in IP addresses — every server, load balancer and database inside it needs one. When you create a VPC, AWS asks for an IP address range, written in CIDR notation (Classless Inter-Domain Routing), for example 10.0.0.0/16. An IPv4 address has 32 bits, written as four numbers (10.0.0.0). The number after the slash says how many of those bits are fixed. The rest can change, and each combination is one address. So /16 leaves 16 free bits → 216 = 65,536 addresses. /24 leaves 8 free bits → 28 = 256 addresses. Smaller number after the slash = bigger network. AWS lets a VPC (and a subnet) be anywhere from /16 (largest) to /28 (16 addresses, smallest). Inside every subnet AWS keeps 5 addresses for itself (the network address, the VPC router, DNS, one reserved for future use, and the broadcast address), so a /24 subnet gives you 251 usable IPs. TipPick a size with room to grow. You can add extra ranges to a VPC later, but it's extra work. Private ranges such as 10.x.x.x, 172.16–31.x.x and 192.168.x.x are the usual choices. Try it: CIDR calculator Type a range like 10.0.0.0/16 or 172.16.3.0/24, or tap a preset. Results appear here."},{"h":"Subnets: splitting the VPC","id":"s3","t":"One project usually has several smaller parts — say payments, transactions and reporting. Instead of throwing everything into one big pool, you split the VPC's range into smaller ranges called subnets (short for sub-networks). For example, inside 172.16.0.0/16: Subnet Range Addresses Payments 172.16.1.0/24 256 Transactions 172.16.2.0/24 256 Reporting 172.16.3.0/24 256 Each subnet lives in a single Availability Zone, and you launch EC2 instances into a subnet. Subnets come in two flavours: Public subnet Reachable from the internet. Its route table sends internet traffic to the internet gateway. This is where you put things the outside world should talk to first: load balancers and NAT gateways. Private subnet No direct route from the internet. Your application servers and databases live here, so nobody outside can reach them directly. Best practice: apps go in private subnets."},{"h":"The main building blocks","id":"s4","t":"Internet gateway (IGW) The gate of the gated community. An internet gateway is attached to the VPC and is the only way traffic can move between your VPC and the public internet. No IGW = nobody from outside can get in, and nothing inside can reach out directly. Route tables The signposts. A route table is a list of rules that says \"traffic going to this destination, send it there\". At home your Wi-Fi router does the same job, and on the internet your request hops through many routers. In AWS each subnet is associated with a route table. Every route table has a local route for the VPC's own range, so subnets in the same VPC can talk to each other. A public subnet's table adds 0.0.0.0/0 → igw-… (\"anything else, send to the internet gateway\"). A private subnet's table has no route to the IGW (it may have 0.0.0.0/0 → nat-…, see below). NoteA subnet isn't \"public\" because of a checkbox. It's public because its route table points to an internet gateway. Remove that route and it becomes private. Load balancer Users don't hit your servers directly. A load balancer sits in the public subnet, receives every request and passes it to a healthy server in the private subnet. You tell it which servers to use with a target group. Load balancers get a full lesson later in Lesson 28. Security groups (and NACLs) The guard at the door. Even after a request reaches the right subnet, the server's security group can still refuse it — for example \"only allow port 8080\". There is also a second guard at the subnet level called a NACL. Security groups, NACLs, and how they differ are covered in Lesson 7: Security Groups vs NACLs."},{"h":"How a request travels into a VPC","id":"s5","t":"Let's follow a user who opens your app in a browser. Press Next step to light up each hop on the map. Orange arrows: a user's request coming in. Dashed arrows: the app reaching out to the internet through the NAT gateway. Try it: a packet's journey Press Next step to start. The map above will highlight each hop. In short, an incoming request goes: Internet gateway — the request enters the VPC. Public subnet — its route table connects it to the IGW, so the request reaches the load balancer. Route table — the local route lets the load balancer reach the private subnet. Security group — checks the port and source IP. If allowed… EC2 instance — the application finally gets the request."},{"h":"NAT gateway: going out without being seen","id":"s6","t":"So far traffic came in. But servers in a private subnet often need to go out — to download a package, apply security updates or call another company's API. They have no route to the IGW, so how? Also, it's bad practice to reveal your private servers' IP addresses to the outside world. If the website you call is ever compromised, an attacker would learn where your servers are. The answer is a NAT gateway (Network Address Translation): It lives in the public subnet and has its own public IP (an Elastic IP). The private subnet's route table says 0.0.0.0/0 → nat-…. When a private server sends a request, the NAT gateway swaps the private source IP for its own public IP (this is \"IP masking\"), sends it out through the IGW and passes the reply back. It only allows connections that start inside. Nobody on the internet can start a connection to your private server through it. Think of it like…A company mailroom. Employees hand letters to the mailroom, which sends them out with the company's return address. Replies come back to the mailroom and get delivered inside. Outsiders never learn which desk the letter came from."},{"h":"VPC flow logs","id":"s7","t":"When something doesn't work (\"why can't users reach my app?\"), you want to see the traffic. VPC flow logs record information about the IP traffic going to and from network interfaces in your VPC: who talked to whom, on which port, and whether it was accepted or rejected. They are great for debugging and security audits. Note that flow logs (and NAT gateways) cost money, while the VPC itself, subnets, route tables and the internet gateway are free. Interview angle\"Explain how a request reaches an app in a private subnet\" is a classic. Walk through it in order: internet gateway → public subnet with load balancer → route table → (NACL) → security group → EC2, then add how outbound traffic uses a NAT gateway. You'll build this exact setup in Lesson 9."},{"h":"Check yourself","id":"s8","t":"Your VPC is 10.0.0.0/16. How many IP addresses does it contain? /16 leaves 32 − 16 = 16 free bits, and 216 = 65,536. What makes a subnet \"public\"? Public vs private is decided by routing. A route to the internet gateway makes a subnet public. A server in a private subnet must download security patches, but must not be reachable from the internet. What do you add? A NAT gateway lets private servers start outbound connections while hiding their private IPs, and blocks connections started from outside."},{"h":"Key takeaways","id":"s9","t":"A VPC is your own isolated network in AWS — the \"gated community\" for your servers. Its size is a CIDR range: /16 = 65,536 IPs, /24 = 256. AWS reserves 5 IPs per subnet. Subnets split the range. Public subnets hold load balancers and NAT gateways; private subnets hold your apps. The internet gateway is the door; route tables are the signposts; security groups are the guards. A NAT gateway lets private servers reach the internet while hiding their IPs. VPC flow logs record traffic so you can debug and audit."}],"n":6,"slug":"06-vpc"},{"title":"Security Groups vs NACLs","lead":"AWS gives you two firewalls inside a VPC: security groups guard each server, and network ACLs guard each subnet. You'll learn how each works, how their rules are evaluated, what \"stateful vs stateless\" means, and you'll replay a hands-on experiment that blocks and unblocks a real app.","sections":[{"h":"","id":"","t":"This lesson builds on Lesson 6: VPC — subnets, internet gateways and route tables are explained there."},{"h":"Security is a shared job","id":"s1","t":"Before a company moves to the cloud, its first question is \"Is it secure?\" AWS's answer is that security is a shared responsibility (see Lesson 2): AWS secures the data centers and gives you tools, and you must configure those tools well. A request from a user travels a long way inside your VPC — internet gateway, load balancer, route tables. The last two checkpoints before it reaches your app are: NACL (Network Access Control List) — at the subnet edge. It protects every server in that subnet. Security group — at the instance (EC2) level. It protects one server (or a group of servers you attach it to). Because they're the last line of defense, a mistake here usually means your app is exposed. Two layers of defense. Incoming traffic must pass the NACL first, then the security group."},{"h":"Security groups: the guard at each server","id":"s2","t":"A security group is a virtual firewall attached to an EC2 instance (more precisely, to its network interface). It has two lists of rules: Inbound rules Traffic coming into your instance — e.g. a user opening your website. Default: nothing is allowed. You add rules like \"TCP port 8000 from anywhere\". Outbound rules Traffic going out of your instance — e.g. your shop app calling a payment provider. Default: everything is allowed. You can tighten it if needed. This is why, back in Lesson 4, Jenkins on port 8080 couldn't be reached until you added an inbound rule. When you launch an instance from the console, the wizard creates a security group that allows only SSH on port 22 — otherwise you couldn't even log in. Security group rules can only allow. There is no \"deny\" rule — anything not allowed is blocked. A rule names a protocol (TCP/UDP), a port or range, and a source (an IP range like 203.0.113.0/24, 0.0.0.0/0 for \"anywhere\", or another security group). All rules are checked together — there is no rule order. Changes apply right away to every instance using that security group. Watch outOpening \"All traffic from 0.0.0.0/0\" to make things work quickly is a common mistake. Every unused open port is a door for attackers or junk traffic. Open only the ports your app actually needs. NoteAWS restricts outbound traffic on port 25 (email/SMTP) from EC2 by default, to stop spammers from abusing its IP addresses. You can ask AWS to lift this if you really run a mail server."},{"h":"NACLs: the gate of each subnet","id":"s3","t":"Now imagine a team that, to save time, opens all traffic from everywhere on its 50 instances. As the DevOps or network admin, you can't police every instance. A Network ACL gives you a second layer you control at the subnet level: One NACL applies to every instance in the subnet automatically — 50 or 10,000 of them. NACLs support both ALLOW and DENY rules. If you deny something here, opening it in a security group won't help. You can block by port (\"deny port 8000\") or by IP range (\"deny 3.4.0.0/16\"). The default NACL that comes with a VPC allows all inbound and outbound traffic, so by default it doesn't get in the way. Rule order matters Each NACL rule has a rule number. AWS checks rules from the lowest number up and stops at the first match. The last rule, shown as *, denies everything that didn't match anything else — you can't delete it. Rule # Type Port Source Action 100 All traffic All 0.0.0.0/0 ALLOW 200 Custom TCP 8000 0.0.0.0/0 DENY * All traffic All 0.0.0.0/0 DENY Is port 8000 blocked here? No! Rule 100 (allow all) matches first, so rule 200 is never reached. To block 8000, its deny rule needs a lower number than the allow-all rule, e.g. 110 deny and 200 allow. TipNumber your rules in steps of 10 or 100 (100, 110, 200…) so you can slot new rules in between later."},{"h":"Stateful vs stateless","id":"s4","t":"This is the most-asked difference in interviews. Every connection has two directions: the request and the response. Security group = stateful It remembers connections. If a request was allowed in, the response is automatically allowed out — no outbound rule needed. Same the other way: if your app calls google.com, the reply is let back in automatically. NACL = stateless It has no memory. Every packet is checked against the rules on its own. So a response needs its own rule in the other direction — for example an outbound rule allowing the return traffic. Think of it like…A security group is a guard who remembers faces: \"You came in a minute ago, go ahead and leave.\" A NACL is a turnstile that checks your ticket both ways — entering and leaving. Responses go back to the client on a random high \"ephemeral\" port (typically 1024–65535). With the default NACL that's fine, because it allows everything. If you write a custom NACL, remember an outbound rule for those ports, or replies will be silently dropped."},{"h":"Side by side","id":"s5","t":"Security group NACL Works at Instance level Subnet level Rule types Allow only Allow and deny Rule order All rules checked together Lowest number first; first match wins State Stateful (return traffic automatic) Stateless (return traffic needs a rule) Default Inbound: deny all · Outbound: allow all Default NACL: allow all both ways Typical owner Instance / app owner DevOps / network admins Using both is called defense in depth: if one layer is misconfigured, the other can still protect you. That's how most organizations run."},{"h":"Hands-on: block and unblock an app","id":"s6","t":"Here's the experiment you can repeat in your own account. Create a VPC. In the VPC console choose Create VPC → VPC and more. Name it demo-vpc and keep the defaults. AWS creates public and private subnets in two Availability Zones, route tables and an internet gateway for you. The Resource map tab shows a diagram of it all. Launch an EC2 instance (Ubuntu, t2.micro, your key pair). Under Network settings → Edit, pick demo-vpc, choose a public subnet (only for this demo — real apps go in private subnets), and enable Auto-assign public IP. Let it create a new security group. Start a tiny web app after you SSH in: sudo apt update python3 -m http.server 8000 Try http://<public-ip>:8000 in a browser. It times out. The NACL allows everything, but the security group only allows port 22. Security group → Edit inbound rules → Add rule: Custom TCP, port 8000, source Anywhere-IPv4. Refresh — you see the directory listing (HTTP 200). NACL → Edit inbound rules: replace the allow-all rule with 100 · Custom TCP · 8000 · DENY. Refresh — blocked again, even though the security group allows it. Reorder: set 100 ALLOW all and 200 DENY 8000. It works (rule 100 matches first). Change the deny to 110 and the allow to 200, and it's blocked (rule 110 matches first). Notice how an app owner who \"did everything right\" in the security group can still be blocked by the subnet's NACL. That's the power the NACL gives the network team. Try it: firewall simulator Set up the two firewalls, then send a request to the EC2 instance. Watch where it gets stopped. Try turning off the outbound rules to see stateful vs stateless. Request NACL inbound (subnet) Security group inbound (instance) TCP 22 from 0.0.0.0/0 (SSH) TCP 8000 from 0.0.0.0/0 Return path Security group outbound: allow all NACL outbound: allow all (incl. ports 1024–65535) Press Send request. Interview angle\"How would you enforce strict network control across a VPC?\" — Use NACLs at the subnet level for broad allow/deny policies (block bad IP ranges or forbidden ports for everyone), and tight security groups per instance that open only needed ports. Mention stateful vs stateless and rule order."},{"h":"Check yourself","id":"s7","t":"A NACL has rule 100 ALLOW all traffic and rule 200 DENY TCP 8000. A request arrives on port 8000. What happens? NACL rules are checked from the lowest number up and the first match wins. Rule 100 allows everything, so rule 200 is never reached. You allow inbound port 443 in a security group but have removed every outbound rule. Can users still get responses? Security groups remember allowed connections, so return traffic is let through. A NACL, being stateless, would need an outbound rule. You need to block one country's IP range for every server in a subnet. Which tool fits best? Security groups can't deny. A NACL deny rule applies to the whole subnet at once."},{"h":"Key takeaways","id":"s8","t":"Security groups protect instances; NACLs protect subnets. Traffic passes the NACL first, then the security group. Security groups only allow; new ones block all inbound and allow all outbound. NACLs allow and deny, checked by rule number from lowest up — first match wins, * denies the rest. Security groups are stateful (replies automatic); NACLs are stateless (replies need a rule). Open only the ports you need, and use both layers for defense in depth."}],"n":7,"slug":"07-security-groups-nacl"},{"title":"Route 53: DNS on AWS","lead":"People type names like amazon.com, but computers connect to IP addresses. DNS is the system that translates one into the other, and Route 53 is AWS's DNS service. Here you'll learn how DNS works and the three jobs Route 53 does: registering domains, storing DNS records in hosted zones, and running health checks.","sections":[{"h":"What is DNS?","id":"s1","t":"DNS stands for Domain Name System. Every load balancer or server you create gets an IP address, such as 3.6.10.171. Yet you never type IP addresses to visit Amazon, Flipkart or Instagram — you type a domain name. Something has to turn the name into the IP address. That something is DNS. We say DNS resolves a domain name to an IP address. Think of it like…Your phone's contacts list. You tap \"Mom\", and the phone dials the number stored for that name. You don't need to remember the number, and if Mom changes her number you just update the contact. Why not just share the IP address? Names are easy to remember; numbers aren't. Imagine memorising an IP for every app you use. IP addresses change. Restart a server without a fixed (static) IP, or replace a load balancer, and the address can be different. The name stays the same — you only update the DNS record. In real setups, a domain name usually points to the load balancer in your VPC, not straight to a server."},{"h":"Route 53: DNS as a service","id":"s2","t":"Just as EC2 gives you \"compute as a service\", Route 53 gives you \"DNS as a service\". (The name comes from port 53, the port DNS uses.) Without it, putting an app online the traditional way means: buy a domain from a seller like GoDaddy, find hosting somewhere else, and maintain DNS records yourself. If your app already runs on AWS, Route 53 lets you do it all in one place. In the architecture you've been drawing since Lesson 6, Route 53 sits in front of everything: the user asks Route 53 for the address of example.com, gets the load balancer's IP, and from there the request flows through the internet gateway as before."},{"h":"How a DNS lookup works","id":"s3","t":"A lookup takes a few hops behind the scenes, usually in milliseconds. Step through it: The resolver walks the DNS tree on your behalf: root → top-level domain → the domain's own name servers (Route 53). Try it: resolve shop.example.com Press Next step to type shop.example.com into the browser."},{"h":"Route 53's three main jobs","id":"s4","t":"1. Domain registration First you need to own a domain name. Search for the name you want (myapp.com taken? Try myapp.in or myapp.xyz), then buy it. With Route 53 you can: Buy the domain from AWS. You pay upfront, usually per year — this part is not pay-as-you-go. Route 53 sets up the hosted zone for you. Keep a domain bought elsewhere (e.g. GoDaddy years ago). Create a hosted zone in Route 53, then copy its four name server (NS) records into your registrar's settings so the internet knows Route 53 now answers for your domain. 2. Hosted zones and DNS records A hosted zone is a container for all the DNS records of one domain — the actual \"name → address\" list. When a lookup arrives, Route 53 finds the hosted zone and checks its records. Public hosted zone — answers questions from anywhere on the internet. Private hosted zone — answers only from inside the VPCs you attach it to, handy for internal names like db.internal. The records you'll meet most: Type Points a name to… Example A an IPv4 address app.example.com → 3.6.10.171 AAAA an IPv6 address app.example.com → 2600:1f18::1 CNAME another name (an alias for a name) www.example.com → example.com Alias (Route 53 only) an AWS resource such as a load balancer, CloudFront or S3 website; follows its IPs automatically and works at the root domain example.com → my-alb-123.elb.amazonaws.com MX mail servers for the domain example.com → 10 mail.example.com NS the name servers that answer for the domain ns-123.awsdns-45.com TXT free text, often for proving you own a domain \"google-site-verification=…\" TipPointing a domain at a load balancer? Use an Alias A record. A load balancer's IPs can change over time, and an alias follows them for you. A CNAME also works for subdomains, but can't be used on the bare root domain (example.com). Each record also has a TTL (time to live): how many seconds resolvers may cache the answer. Long TTL = fewer lookups; short TTL = changes spread faster. 3. Health checks Route 53 can regularly send requests to your endpoints (for example every 30 seconds) to see if they're alive. Say your app runs on web servers in two Availability Zones: if one stops answering, Route 53 can stop handing out its address and send everyone to the healthy one. Health checks can also trigger alarms. Routing policies When a record has more than one possible answer, a routing policy decides which answer Route 53 gives. Pick one to see what it does: Try it: routing policy picker Choose a policy. Interview angle\"What happens when you type a URL in a browser?\" starts with DNS. Mention caching, the resolver, root → TLD → authoritative name servers (Route 53), the record type that's returned, and then the request going to the load balancer."},{"h":"Check yourself","id":"s5","t":"What is the main job of DNS? DNS resolves names like example.com to IP addresses. Spreading traffic is a load balancer's job. You bought myapp.com on GoDaddy and want Route 53 to manage its DNS. What do you do? The registrar must point the domain at Route 53's name servers, which serve the records in your hosted zone. Which record type is best for pointing example.com at an AWS load balancer? An Alias record points at AWS resources, tracks their changing IPs and works at the root domain."},{"h":"Key takeaways","id":"s6","t":"DNS resolves easy names into IP addresses, which are hard to remember and can change. Route 53 is AWS's DNS service and sits in front of your load balancer. Its three jobs: domain registration, hosted zones with DNS records, and health checks. Common records: A, AAAA, CNAME, Alias, MX, NS, TXT. Use Alias for AWS resources. Routing policies (simple, weighted, latency, failover, geolocation, multivalue) decide which answer is returned."}],"n":8,"slug":"08-route53"},{"title":"Project: Production-style VPC","lead":"Time to build the setup AWS recommends for running apps securely: servers hidden in private subnets across two Availability Zones, a load balancer in front, NAT gateways for outbound traffic, and a bastion host for admin access. Along the way you'll meet Auto Scaling Groups, launch templates, bastion hosts and Elastic IPs.","sections":[{"h":"","id":"","t":"This project uses ideas from Lesson 6: VPC (subnets, route tables, NAT) and Lesson 7: Security Groups. Review them if anything below feels unfamiliar."},{"h":"What we're building","id":"s1","t":"A VPC with a public and a private subnet in each of two Availability Zones. In the public subnets: an Application Load Balancer, one NAT gateway per AZ, and a bastion host. In the private subnets: app servers launched by an Auto Scaling Group. Users reach the app only through the load balancer. Servers reach the internet only through the NAT gateways. Orange: user traffic. Dashed: admin SSH via the bastion. Dotted: a private server reaching the internet through its NAT gateway. Try it: walk the architecture Pick a story, then step through it on the diagram above. Press Next step."},{"h":"New building blocks","id":"s2","t":"Two Availability Zones In the theory lessons we drew one public and one private subnet. In production you repeat them in two (or more) Availability Zones. If one AZ — one group of AWS data centers — has a problem, the other keeps serving users. That's high availability. Auto Scaling Group (ASG) and launch template Instead of launching each EC2 instance by hand, you tell an Auto Scaling Group: \"Always keep at least 2 copies of this server running, spread across these subnets.\" Desired capacity — how many to run now (e.g. 2). Minimum / maximum — the floor and ceiling. On a festival sale day, when CPU climbs to 80–90%, a scaling policy can grow the group up to the maximum (say 4), then shrink it back later. If an instance dies, the ASG replaces it automatically. An ASG needs a launch template — a saved recipe for the instances it creates: AMI, instance type, key pair, security group, network. It's the same form as launching an EC2 instance, saved for reuse. One template can be shared by several ASGs and acts as a record of exactly what your servers look like. Bastion host (jump server) Servers in private subnets have no public IP, so you can't SSH to them from your laptop. A bastion host is a small, tightly-guarded EC2 instance in the public subnet. You SSH into the bastion first, then \"jump\" from it to the private servers. Only one door to guard instead of many. One place to log and audit who logged in and what commands they ran. You can apply extra rules there (allowed source IPs, MFA, scripts). Linux servers are reached over SSH; Windows servers over RDP. Think of it like…The reception desk of an office building. Visitors can't walk straight to the server room; they sign in at reception, and only from there are they escorted inside. NAT gateway per AZ and Elastic IPs Each private subnet sends its internet traffic to the NAT gateway in the same AZ. One NAT per AZ means losing an AZ doesn't cut the other AZ's servers off from the internet. Each NAT gateway needs an Elastic IP: a public IP address that belongs to your account and stays the same (a static IP). Normal public IPs can change when an instance stops and starts; an Elastic IP doesn't. Load balancer and target group (just enough) The load balancer spreads requests across servers — 100 requests might go 50/50, or 60/40. It sends traffic to a target group (a list of servers plus a port), and only to targets that pass their health check. Full details in Lesson 28."},{"h":"Build it step by step","id":"s3","t":"Step 1 — Create the VPC Open the VPC console → Create VPC → choose VPC and more. AWS draws a preview of everything it will create. Name: aws-prod-example. Keep the IPv4 CIDR 10.0.0.0/16 (65,536 IPs is plenty). No IPv6. Availability Zones: 2. Public subnets: 2. Private subnets: 2. NAT gateways: 1 per AZ. VPC endpoints: None (not needed here). Click Create VPC. AWS creates the subnets, route tables (public → internet gateway, each private → its NAT gateway), the internet gateway, Elastic IPs and NAT gateways. NAT gateways take a few minutes to become active. Watch outIf creation fails with \"maximum number of addresses has been reached\", your account has hit its Elastic IP limit (5 per region by default). Release unused Elastic IPs in EC2 → Elastic IPs, then click Retry. Step 2 — Launch template EC2 console → Auto Scaling Groups → Create Auto Scaling group → Create a launch template. Name it, pick the Ubuntu AMI, a free-tier instance type (t2.micro) and your key pair. Create a new security group in your new VPC with inbound rules: SSH 22 and Custom TCP 8000 (the app port). Open only the ports you need. Step 3 — Auto Scaling Group Name the ASG and select your launch template. Network: choose your VPC and the two private subnets. Load balancing: No load balancer for now — we'll create and attach one separately. Group size: desired 2, min 1–2, max 3–4. Scaling policy: none for this demo. Skip notifications. Create. After a minute, EC2 → Instances shows two instances: one in us-east-1a, one in us-east-1b, both without public IPs. Step 4 — Bastion host Launch a normal EC2 instance named bastion-host (Ubuntu, t2.micro, same key pair). Network settings → Edit: choose the same VPC, a public subnet, and enable auto-assign public IP. Security group must allow SSH (22). Step 5 — Jump into a private server and start the app The bastion needs the key to log into the private servers. The simple (demo) way is to copy the .pem file to it with scp (secure copy): # from your laptop: copy the key to the bastion scp -i aws_login.pem aws_login.pem ubuntu@<bastion-public-ip>:/home/ubuntu # log into the bastion ssh -i aws_login.pem ubuntu@<bastion-public-ip> # from the bastion: hop to a private server using its PRIVATE IP chmod 400 aws_login.pem ssh -i aws_login.pem ubuntu@10.0.140.109 TipCopying private keys onto servers is fine for learning, but avoid it in real life. Use an SSH agent and ProxyJump instead, so the key never leaves your laptop: ssh-add aws_login.pem ssh -J ubuntu@<bastion-public-ip> ubuntu@10.0.140.109 Also restrict the bastion's SSH rule to your IP rather than 0.0.0.0/0. (SSH basics are in Lesson 5.) On one of the private servers, create a page and serve it: cat > index.html <<'EOF' <!DOCTYPE html> <html><body> <h1>My first AWS project</h1> <p>Demo of an app in a private subnet</p> </body></html> EOF python3 -m http.server 8000 We deliberately leave the second server without the app, to watch the load balancer's health checks at work. Step 6 — Target group EC2 → Target groups → Create. Target type: Instances. Protocol HTTP, port 8000 (the port your app listens on — getting this wrong is a common mistake), your VPC. Select both ASG instances → Include as pending below → Create target group. Step 7 — Application Load Balancer EC2 → Load balancers → Create → Application Load Balancer (works with HTTP/HTTPS — \"layer 7\"). Scheme: Internet-facing. VPC: yours. Mappings: both AZs, each with its public subnet. Security group: one that allows HTTP port 80 from anywhere. Listener: HTTP :80 → forward to your target group. Create, and wait for state Active. Watch outIf the console warns \"security groups for your load balancer don't allow traffic on the listener port\", the ALB's security group is missing an inbound rule for port 80. Add HTTP · 80 · Anywhere and wait a moment. Step 8 — Test it Copy the load balancer's DNS name into your browser. You'll see \"My first AWS project\", served from a server that has no public IP. Traffic went: Internet gateway → ALB in the public subnet → target group → EC2 in the private subnet. Refresh many times — you always land on the same server. Why? The target group's health check marks the server without the app as unhealthy, so the ALB only sends traffic to the healthy one. Your assignmentDeploy the page on the second server too, with a different heading (\"My second AWS project\"). Once both targets are healthy, refreshing should alternate between the two pages — load balancing in action. Clean up NAT gateways and Elastic IPs cost money every hour. When you're done, delete in this order: Auto Scaling Group (otherwise it recreates instances), load balancer, target group, bastion, NAT gateways, then release the Elastic IPs and delete the VPC. Interview angle\"Have you built a public/private subnet architecture? How does traffic reach the app?\" Describe this project: two AZs for availability, ASG for scaling and self-healing, ALB in public subnets, apps in private subnets, NAT per AZ for outbound, bastion for admin access, and security groups opening only needed ports."},{"h":"Check yourself","id":"s4","t":"Why can't you SSH from your laptop directly to the app servers in this project? Private-subnet servers have no route from the internet. The bastion in the public subnet is the controlled entry point. What does an Auto Scaling Group need before you can create it? The launch template is the recipe (AMI, type, key pair, security group). A load balancer is optional. Only one of two servers runs the app. The ALB keeps sending you to that one. Why? Load balancers route only to healthy targets. Deploy the app on both and traffic will be shared."},{"h":"Key takeaways","id":"s5","t":"Production layout: public subnets (ALB, NAT, bastion) and private subnets (apps) in two AZs. An Auto Scaling Group keeps the right number of instances running, using a launch template as its recipe. A bastion host is the single, auditable door for SSH into private servers. One NAT gateway per AZ, each with a static Elastic IP, gives private servers safe outbound access. The ALB forwards to a target group and only to healthy targets. Remember to open port 80 on its security group. Clean up NAT gateways and Elastic IPs after practice — they bill by the hour."}],"n":9,"slug":"09-vpc-production-project"},{"title":"Scenario Questions: EC2, IAM, VPC","lead":"Interviewers today rarely ask \"What is EC2?\" — they describe a situation and ask how you'd handle it. Here are 10 scenario questions on everything so far, with model answers and links back to the lessons that explain each idea.","sections":[{"h":"How to answer scenario questions","id":"s1","t":"Break the question into parts. \"Highly available and scalable two-tier app\" is really three questions. Name the AWS building block for each part (AZs for availability, Auto Scaling for scale, subnets for layout…). Say why, in one sentence each. Mention security when it fits. If several answers work, give them, then say which is simplest or best and why. TipTry to answer each question out loud before opening it. Use the buttons below to open or close all answers at once."},{"h":"Networking and VPC","id":"s2","t":"1. Design a VPC for a two-tier application that must be highly available and scalable. Split it into its parts: Layout: a public subnet holding an internet-facing load balancer, and a private subnet holding the application servers. The load balancer must be reachable from the internet, otherwise nobody can reach the app. High availability: repeat the public and private subnets in at least two Availability Zones, so if one AZ goes down the other keeps serving. Scalability: run the app servers in an Auto Scaling Group that adds instances when load rises. This is exactly the build in Lesson 9. (Adding a database tier makes it three-tier.) 2. Your VPC has several subnets. Block outbound internet access for one subnet but keep it for another. You could tighten outbound security group rules on every instance, but the cleanest answer is routing: Find the route table associated with the subnet you want to restrict. Remove the default route 0.0.0.0/0 that points to the internet gateway (or NAT gateway). With no route out, nothing in that subnet can reach the internet. The other subnet keeps its own route table and stays connected. Route tables: Lesson 6. 3. Instances in a private subnet need internet access for software updates. How? Put a NAT gateway (or an older-style NAT instance) in a public subnet. In the private subnet's route table, send 0.0.0.0/0 to the NAT gateway. Bonus point: NAT (network address translation) replaces the instance's private IP with the NAT gateway's public IP, so outside sites never learn your server's address, and nobody outside can start a connection back in. NAT gateway: Lesson 6. 4. Two EC2 instances, \"foo\" and \"bar\", must talk to each other using private IPs. What do you need? Sometimes scenarios are simple: Put them in the same VPC. Every route table has a built-in local route, so subnets in one VPC can reach each other — same subnet or different subnets. Make sure their security groups (and NACLs, if customised) allow the port they use. A neat trick: allow traffic whose source is the other instance's security group. If they are in different VPCs, connect the VPCs with VPC peering and add routes in both route tables pointing to the peering connection. 5. You must enforce strict network access control for your VPC. How? Use network ACLs at the subnet level for broad, fine-grained rules: allow only specific IP ranges and ports, and explicitly deny bad ones. They apply to every instance in the subnet. Use security groups at the instance level to open only the ports each app needs. Using both is defense in depth. Details and a simulator: Lesson 7. 6. Your organisation needs an isolated environment inside the VPC for sensitive workloads. How do you set it up? This checks that you understand subnets as a unit of isolation: Create a dedicated private subnet — a slice of the VPC's range (e.g. a few thousand of its 65,536 addresses). Give it a route table with no route to an internet gateway or NAT gateway — only the local route. Lock it down further with a strict NACL and security groups, and limit who can reach it (not even via the general bastion if not needed). 7. An application in your VPC needs to reach AWS services like S3 privately and securely. How? Use a VPC endpoint. It lets resources in your VPC reach services such as S3 or DynamoDB over AWS's private network, without going through an internet gateway or NAT gateway. For S3 and DynamoDB there is a free gateway endpoint; you add it to the subnet's route table. (It's the \"S3 gateway\" option you see in VPC and more.) Combine it with an IAM role on the instance so the app has permission to use the bucket. S3 itself: Lesson 11. 8. What's the difference between a security group and a NACL? Security group NACL Level Instance Subnet Rules Allow only Allow and deny, numbered order State Stateful — replies allowed automatically Stateless — replies need their own rule Example of \"stateful\": your app calls google.com. With a security group, the response comes back in without any inbound rule because the group remembers the outgoing request. A NACL doesn't remember, so you must allow both directions. Good practice: use both. More in Lesson 7."},{"h":"IAM","id":"s3","t":"9. Explain IAM users, groups, roles and policies. User — an identity for a person (or tool) who needs to log in. Handles authentication: who are you? Policy — a JSON document listing permissions (e.g. read S3, manage EC2). Handles authorization: what may you do? A user without policies can sign in but do nothing. Group — a set of users sharing policies. If 500 developers all need EKS access, update the developers group once instead of 500 users. Role — permissions that a service (or person, temporarily) can assume. Example: attach a role to an EC2 instance so the Python app on it can read a database or S3 bucket — no stored passwords. Full explanation: Lesson 3."},{"h":"Access to private servers","id":"s4","t":"10. Instances in a private subnet must have no direct internet access, but admins still need to log in. How do you set this up? Launch a bastion host (jump server) in a public subnet of the same VPC. Admins SSH to the bastion, then from it to the private instances' private IPs. Use SSH for Linux and RDP for Windows. Allow SSH on the bastion only from trusted IPs, and on private instances only from the bastion's security group. Why it's secure: there is one door to guard, and you can log and audit every login and command there. Hands-on: Lesson 9."},{"h":"Cheat sheet: three kinds of subnet","id":"s5","t":"Several answers above come down to the subnet's route table. Keep this in your head: Subnet Default route (0.0.0.0/0) Used for Public → internet gateway Load balancers, NAT gateways, bastion Private → NAT gateway App servers that need updates Isolated none (local only) Sensitive workloads, databases"},{"h":"Check yourself","id":"s6","t":"The quickest way to stop one subnet from reaching the internet, without touching each instance, is to… Deleting the IGW would cut off every subnet. Editing that one subnet's route table affects only it. An app on EC2 needs to read from S3. What's the right way to give it permission? Roles give services temporary credentials without storing secrets on the machine. Which pair gives a two-tier app high availability and scalability? AZs keep the app up if one location fails; an Auto Scaling Group adds servers as load grows."},{"h":"Key takeaways","id":"s7","t":"Break scenario questions into parts and map each to a building block. Availability → multiple AZs. Scalability → Auto Scaling Groups. Most subnet questions are answered by the route table: IGW, NAT, or nothing. Security: NACLs at the subnet, security groups at the instance — stateless vs stateful. Give apps permissions with IAM roles; reach private servers through a bastion host."}],"n":10,"slug":"10-interview-ec2-iam-vpc"},{"title":"S3: Object Storage","lead":"Amazon S3 is where companies keep files they can't afford to lose: backups, logs, reports, website files. You'll learn how buckets and objects work, how to keep old versions, how to pay less with storage classes, how to lock a bucket down, and how to host a website from one.","sections":[{"h":"The problem S3 solves","id":"s1","t":"When your laptop runs out of space, you buy a bigger disk or a USB drive. A company has the same problem, only much bigger: Database backups and dumps: one backup of a big shopping site can be several terabytes. Application logs: for example, \"keep the last 30 days of logs\". Reports, CSV/Excel files, dashboard data, config files, images, videos. S3 (Simple Storage Service) is AWS's answer: you put in as much data as you like, pay only for what you store, and AWS takes care of the disks. It was one of the very first AWS services, and it's often the first one people learn because the idea is easy to follow. Think of it like…A huge, never-full online drive. A bucket is a top-level folder you create, and every file you drop into it is an object."},{"h":"Buckets, objects and keys","id":"s2","t":"Object storage stores each file as a whole object (data + some metadata) that you read or write in one go through a web request. It's not a disk you mount and edit block by block (that's EBS, attached to an EC2 instance). Bucket: a container for objects. You create it, name it and pick a region. Object: anything you upload, such as index.html, a video or a 3 TB database dump. S3 doesn't care what type of file it is, so it calls them all \"objects\". Key: the object's full name inside the bucket, e.g. logs/2024/06/app.log. The slashes look like folders, but they're really just part of the name. Limits worth remembering There's no limit on how much data a bucket can hold. A single object can be at most 5 TB. A bigger file must be split up. For large files, use multipart upload: the file goes up in chunks (say 100 MB each). If your network drops, only the failed chunk is retried instead of starting over. AWS recommends it for anything over about 100 MB, and you must use it above 5 GB. Bucket names are globally unique Every object can be reached over HTTPS with a URL, so a bucket name must be unique across all AWS accounts in the world, not just yours. If someone already took abhishek, you can't have it. Teams use a naming convention so names stay unique and meaningful: <app>-<feature>-<environment>-<company domain> app1-payments-prod-example.com Names must be 3–63 characters, using lowercase letters, numbers, dots and hyphens. Global names, regional buckets This trips people up: the name is global and the data can be reached from anywhere, but each bucket lives in one region that you choose. Pick a region close to your users so requests travel a shorter distance. That delay is called latency. (Regions and availability zones are covered in Lesson 2.) You pick one region for a bucket. Inside that region, S3 quietly keeps copies of each object in several availability zones, so losing one data center doesn't lose your data."},{"h":"Why S3 is so popular","id":"s3","t":"Durability: \"11 nines\" S3 is designed for 99.999999999% durability. Put another way, if you store 10 million objects, you can expect to lose one about every 10,000 years. That's possible because S3 copies every object across several data centers. Availability Your data can be read when you need it. S3 Standard is designed for 99.99% availability. Scalability Start with one 100-byte file and grow to petabytes. You don't have to buy or resize any disks. Security Encryption, bucket policies, Block Public Access, access logging and object lock. More on these below. Cost efficiency You pay per GB per month, and old or rarely used data can move to much cheaper storage classes. Performance Choosing a nearby region gives fast transfers, and multipart upload makes huge uploads reliable. Interview angle\"Why S3?\" A strong short answer: it's simple object storage with 11 nines of durability, high availability, practically unlimited scale, strong security controls, and low cost thanks to storage classes."},{"h":"Creating a bucket and uploading","id":"s4","t":"In the console, search for S3, open Buckets, then click Create bucket. Enter a globally unique name and pick a region. Leave Block all public access turned on. That's the safe default. Leave versioning at its default for now. Server-side encryption is on by default: since January 2023, every new object is encrypted with S3-managed keys. Click Create bucket, open it, click Upload, add a file and upload. Your file is now an object, and it stays safe even if your laptop dies."},{"h":"Versioning: keep every old copy","id":"s5","t":"Say you upload app.log today and a changed app.log tomorrow. Without versioning, S3 simply replaces the old file, and yesterday's copy is gone. With versioning turned on (Bucket → Properties → Bucket Versioning → Enable), S3 keeps every version, a bit like Git keeps the history of a file. If someone says \"yesterday's file was wrong, give me the one from the day before\", you can download that exact version. Each version gets its own version ID. Deleting a file in a versioned bucket doesn't destroy the data. S3 adds a delete marker on top, so the file looks gone but older versions are still there. Remove the marker and the file comes back. Once enabled, versioning can only be suspended, not fully turned off. S3 can keep versions, but it isn't a replacement for Git: there are no code reviews, branches or pull requests. Try it: versioning simulator Upload report.csv a few times, delete it, then try again with versioning enabled. Watch the version list and what a normal download returns. Versioning: GET report.csv → 404 Not Found"},{"h":"Storage classes: pay for what you need","id":"s6","t":"Storage is a trade-off. Data you read often has to come back instantly, which costs more. Data you almost never read can sit somewhere slower and much cheaper. S3 lets you pick a storage class for each object. Durability stays at 11 nines for all of them. What changes is price, how fast you get the data back, and the minimum time you pay for. Storage class Best for Getting data back Storage price S3 Standard (default) Data used often Instant (milliseconds) Highest (about $0.023/GB-month in us-east-1) Intelligent-Tiering Unknown or changing access patterns Instant for its frequent and infrequent tiers S3 moves each object between tiers for you, for a small monitoring fee Standard-IA (Infrequent Access) Used about once a month but must be fast Instant, plus a per-GB retrieval fee Lower (minimum 30 days) One Zone-IA Copies you can re-create Instant Lower still, but stored in only one AZ Glacier Instant Retrieval Archives opened a few times a year Milliseconds Very low (minimum 90 days) Glacier Flexible Retrieval Backups you rarely restore Minutes to about 12 hours Very low (minimum 90 days) Glacier Deep Archive Compliance data you keep for years 12–48 hours Lowest (minimum 180 days) Prices change and vary by region. Check the official S3 pricing page. Even small numbers add up fast when you're storing terabytes. Try it: storage class picker How often is it read? When you need it, how long can you wait? I could easily re-create this data if it were lost Lifecycle rules: automate the savings You don't have to move objects by hand. A lifecycle rule (Bucket → Management) does it on a schedule. This pairs nicely with versioning, which would otherwise keep paying for every old copy: Move objects to Standard-IA after 30 days, and to Glacier after 90. Move previous versions to Glacier Deep Archive, or delete them after 5 or 10 days. Delete logs older than 30 days."},{"h":"Other bucket properties at a glance","id":"s7","t":"Tags: key/value labels like project=app1. They make it easy to answer \"which buckets belong to this project?\" and to split costs by team. Default encryption: on by default. S3 supports encryption at rest (on AWS's disks) and in transit (over HTTPS). Server access logging: records who accessed which object, which helps you spot suspicious activity. It's off by default. Event notifications: when an object is uploaded or deleted, S3 can trigger something else, such as a Lambda function (see Lesson 19). Object Lock: makes objects write-once-read-many, so nobody can overwrite or delete them for a set period. Useful for sensitive or regulated data. Static website hosting: covered in demo 2 below."},{"h":"Demo 1: lock a bucket with a bucket policy","id":"s8","t":"The scenario: someone in your company has an IAM policy like AmazonS3FullAccess, which means they can open every bucket, including the one holding sensitive data. As the bucket owner, you can block them anyway with a bucket policy. That's a JSON policy attached to the bucket itself rather than to a user. Create an IAM user with no permissions and log in as them in a private browser window. Opening S3 shows \"you don't have permissions to list buckets\", as expected. Attach AmazonS3FullAccess to that user. Now they can open your bucket and download files. That's the problem. In your bucket, go to Permissions → Bucket policy → Edit and add a statement that denies everyone except you. The policy generator (Add statement) helps you build the JSON. Refresh the other user's window. They now get \"Insufficient permissions to list objects\". { \"Version\": \"2012-10-17\", \"Statement\": [ { \"Sid\": \"DenyEveryoneExceptOwner\", \"Effect\": \"Deny\", \"Principal\": \"*\", \"Action\": \"s3:*\", \"Resource\": [ \"arn:aws:s3:::app1-payments-prod-example.com\", \"arn:aws:s3:::app1-payments-prod-example.com/*\" ], \"Condition\": { \"StringNotEquals\": { \"aws:PrincipalArn\": \"arn:aws:iam::111122223333:root\" } } } ] } Principal \"*\": applies to everyone. Effect Deny + Action s3:*: block every S3 action. Resource: list both the bucket ARN and bucket/* (the objects inside). Forgetting one of the two is a classic mistake. Condition: \"unless the caller is this ARN\", so you don't lock yourself out. Replace 111122223333 with your account ID. Watch outAn explicit Deny always beats an Allow. A careless deny-all policy can lock out your own admins. The account's root user can still delete the bucket policy to recover."},{"h":"Demo 2: host a static website","id":"s9","t":"A static website is plain HTML, CSS, JavaScript and images, with no server-side code. S3 can serve one cheaply to the whole world. In real jobs, developers usually set this up once, but you should know the steps. Create a bucket and upload your index.html. Go to Properties → Static website hosting → Edit, choose Host a static website, set the index document to index.html, then save. You get a website endpoint URL like http://<bucket>.s3-website-us-east-1.amazonaws.com. Open the URL and you get 403 Forbidden, because public access is still blocked. Go to Permissions → Block public access → Edit, untick Block all public access and confirm. If the box can't be changed, Block Public Access is also switched on at the account level and must be turned off there too. Also remove any deny-all policy from demo 1. Still \"Access denied\"? Turning off the block only allows public policies. You still need a policy that grants public read: { \"Version\": \"2012-10-17\", \"Statement\": [ { \"Sid\": \"PublicReadGetObject\", \"Effect\": \"Allow\", \"Principal\": \"*\", \"Action\": \"s3:GetObject\", \"Resource\": \"arn:aws:s3:::app1-payments-prod-example.com/*\" } ] } Refresh the page and your site loads. TipIf your page's JavaScript calls APIs on other domains, the browser may block those calls unless CORS (Cross-Origin Resource Sharing) is configured. For real sites, you'd usually put a CDN in front of the bucket and keep the bucket private. See Lesson 21: CloudFront. A custom domain comes from Route 53."},{"h":"Check yourself","id":"s10","t":"Your bucket has versioning enabled and someone deletes report.csv. What happens? In a versioned bucket, a normal delete only places a delete marker on top. Remove the marker (or download an older version ID) to get the file back. A user has AmazonS3FullAccess in IAM, but you want them out of one sensitive bucket. What's the simplest fix? Bucket policies are attached to the bucket, and an explicit Deny overrides any Allow in the user's IAM policy. You must keep audit files for 7 years and will probably never read them. Waiting 2 days is fine if you ever do. Which class is cheapest? Deep Archive is the cheapest class. Retrieval takes 12–48 hours, which fits rarely read compliance data."},{"h":"Key takeaways","id":"s11","t":"S3 stores objects (any file, up to 5 TB each) in buckets. Bucket names are globally unique, but each bucket lives in one region. 11 nines of durability come from automatic copies across multiple AZs. Versioning keeps old copies, and deletes become delete markers. Use lifecycle rules so old versions don't pile up costs. Pick a storage class by how often you read the data and how fast you need it back. Standard costs the most, Deep Archive the least. Keep Block Public Access on. Use bucket policies to restrict access even for users with broad IAM rights. Static website = upload files + enable website hosting + allow public access + a public-read s3:GetObject policy."}],"n":11,"slug":"11-s3"},{"title":"AWS CLI","lead":"Clicking through the console is fine for learning, but it doesn't scale. The AWS Command Line Interface (CLI) lets you list, create and delete AWS resources from your terminal in seconds. In this lesson you'll install it, connect it to your account with access keys, and learn how to work out any command from the docs.","sections":[{"h":"Why not just use the console?","id":"s1","t":"So far we've built everything in the AWS console, the web UI. That's great for learning, but it's not automation-friendly. Picture a busy DevOps week: \"We need 10 VPCs for new projects by this afternoon.\" \"Create 20 S3 buckets with this exact configuration.\" \"Spin up 15 EC2 instances.\" Doing that by clicking means repeating the same screens over and over, which is slow, boring and easy to get wrong. Every serious tool, whether AWS, Jenkins or GitHub, solves this the same way: with an API."},{"h":"What is an API?","id":"s2","t":"API stands for Application Programming Interface: a set of URLs that a program, rather than a person, can send requests to. You send a request like \"create a bucket named X with versioning on\", along with your credentials, and AWS sends back the result, usually as JSON. Console (UI): you reach AWS manually by clicking. API: you reach AWS programmatically, from a script, a tool or code. Calling raw APIs yourself is hard work. You'd have to build HTTP requests, sign them with your keys and parse JSON. So AWS and others built tools that sit on top of the API and do that work for you: Tool What you write Best for AWS CLI Short commands in a terminal Quick tasks, checks and simple scripts SDKs (e.g. boto3 for Python) Code in your programming language Apps and automation that talk to AWS (see Lesson 20) CloudFormation, Terraform, CDK Templates/code describing the whole setup Building and managing large stacks of resources (Lesson 13, Lesson 26) Every tool ends up calling the same AWS APIs. The CLI is a middleman that turns your typed command into an API call and prints the response. Think of it like…A translator. You say aws s3 ls in plain shorthand, and the CLI translates it into the exact API request AWS understands (here, ListBuckets), then translates the answer back for you."},{"h":"CLI or CloudFormation/Terraform?","id":"s3","t":"If they all call the same API, why learn more than one? Because they suit different jobs: CLI = quick actions. Your manager asks \"which S3 buckets do we have?\" Running aws s3 ls answers in seconds, with no need to log in, switch regions and click around. IaC tools = big, repeatable setups. A VPC, subnets, EC2 instances and a load balancer as one unit is far easier to describe in a template that can be reviewed and reused. A very long CLI command is hard to remember and share. Interview angle\"When do you use the CLI vs CloudFormation/Terraform?\" Say: CLI for quick, short tasks and small scripts; CFT/Terraform when creating or managing a full stack of resources that should be version-controlled and reviewed. Every DevOps engineer uses the CLI daily, whatever IaC tool they pick."},{"h":"Step 1: install the CLI (version 2)","id":"s4","t":"Only install from the official AWS documentation (search \"AWS CLI install\", then User Guide → Get started → Install/update). Make sure you get version 2. Version 1 is old. Version 2 comes with everything it needs bundled in, so you don't have to install Python separately. # Linux (x86_64) curl \"https://awscli.amazonaws.com/awscli-exe-linux-x86_64.zip\" -o \"awscliv2.zip\" unzip awscliv2.zip sudo ./aws/install # macOS (command-line installer) curl \"https://awscli.amazonaws.com/AWSCLIV2.pkg\" -o \"AWSCLIV2.pkg\" sudo installer -pkg AWSCLIV2.pkg -target / # Windows (PowerShell or cmd) msiexec.exe /i https://awscli.amazonaws.com/AWSCLIV2.msi # Verify — should print aws-cli/2.x.y ... aws --version TipOn Windows, a Linux environment makes DevOps practice easier: a VirtualBox Ubuntu VM, WSL, or at least Git Bash for a Unix-like terminal."},{"h":"Step 2: create access keys","id":"s5","t":"The CLI is installed, but it doesn't know which of the millions of AWS accounts to talk to, or who you are. For programs, AWS uses access keys instead of a username and password: Access key ID: like a username (e.g. AKIA…). Secret access key: like a password. It's shown only once when you create it. Create them under your name (top right) → Security credentials → Access keys → Create access key. Each user can have at most two access keys, which lets you rotate them. Watch outAnyone with your secret key can use your account. Never share it, paste it in chats or commit it to Git. Use an IAM user, not the root user. AWS warns you if you try to create root access keys, and in a real company you won't have root access anyway (see Lesson 3: IAM). Delete keys you no longer need."},{"h":"Step 3: aws configure","id":"s6","t":"$ aws configure AWS Access Key ID [None]: AKIAIOSFODNN7EXAMPLE AWS Secret Access Key [None]: wJalrXUtnFEMI/K7MDENG/bPxRfiCYEXAMPLEKEY Default region name [None]: us-east-1 Default output format [None]: json Default region: where commands run unless you pass --region. Output format: json (most common and script-friendly), table (easy to read) or text. The values are saved in ~/.aws/credentials (keys) and ~/.aws/config (region, output). Profiles: more than one account Most engineers work with several accounts or roles (dev, prod…). A profile is a named set of credentials: aws configure --profile dev # save a second set of keys as \"dev\" aws s3 ls --profile dev # run one command as \"dev\" export AWS_PROFILE=dev # use \"dev\" for the whole terminal session aws sts get-caller-identity # \"who am I?\" — shows account and user ARN"},{"h":"Step 4: run commands","id":"s7","t":"Every command has the same shape: aws <service> <action> [--option value ...] # Verify the setup: list your buckets aws s3 ls 2024-06-06 10:12:33 demo-abhishek-bucket # Create an EC2 instance (replace IDs with ones from YOUR account) aws ec2 run-instances \\ --image-id ami-0abcdef1234567890 \\ --instance-type t2.micro \\ --key-name my-key \\ --security-group-ids sg-0123456789abcdef0 \\ --subnet-id subnet-0123456789abcdef0 \\ --count 1 The run-instances command is long because launching an instance in the console also asks for many details: the AMI, instance type, key pair, network and so on (Lesson 4). The JSON reply includes the new instance ID and private IP, and you'll see the same instance in the console if you're looking at the same region. Reading the CLI reference (how to work out any command) Nobody memorizes these commands. Search \"AWS CLI reference <service>\", e.g. \"aws cli reference s3\": Open the service page. Its Available Commands list shows actions like cp, ls, mb (make bucket), mv, rb, rm, sync. Click a command to see its description, every option and examples. For EC2, search the list for what you want to do (e.g. run-instances) and copy only the options you need. You can also run aws ec2 run-instances help right in the terminal. Error messages tell you what's missing. Leave out --image-id and AWS replies: \"An error occurred (MissingParameter) when calling the RunInstances operation: The request must contain the parameter ImageId\". Leaving out optional ones like --key-name still works, though without a key pair you can't SSH in. Try it: command builder Pick a service and an action, fill in the options, and see the command plus the API call the CLI makes for you. Empty a required field to see the kind of error you'd get. Service Action --region --output --profile"},{"h":"Check yourself","id":"s8","t":"What does the AWS CLI actually do when you run aws s3 ls? The CLI is a layer between you and the AWS API. It signs the request with your access keys, calls the API and formats the reply. Your manager wants a 3-tier setup (VPC, subnets, EC2, load balancer) that the team can review and recreate. Best tool? For whole stacks that need review and repeatability, use IaC. Keep the CLI for quick tasks. Which pair of values does aws configure need to authenticate? Access keys are the programmatic equivalent of username and password. Region and output format are just defaults."},{"h":"Key takeaways","id":"s9","t":"The console doesn't scale for repeated work. APIs let programs manage AWS, and the CLI wraps those APIs in simple commands. Install CLI v2 from the official docs and check it with aws --version. Authenticate with an IAM user's access keys through aws configure. Keep the secret key secret and use profiles for multiple accounts. Command shape: aws <service> <action> --options. Look up options in the CLI reference or with help. CLI for quick, short tasks. CloudFormation/Terraform for full, reviewable stacks."}],"n":12,"slug":"12-aws-cli"},{"title":"Infrastructure as Code with CloudFormation","lead":"Instead of clicking resources together, you describe them in a YAML file, and AWS CloudFormation builds them for you, the same way every time. You'll learn what Infrastructure as Code means, how a template is laid out, how stacks work, how to catch manual changes with drift detection, and when to choose Terraform instead.","sections":[{"h":"What is Infrastructure as Code?","id":"s1","t":"Developers write code to build apps. Infrastructure as Code (IaC) applies the same idea to servers, networks and storage: you write code (a template) that describes infrastructure, and a tool creates it. You might say the AWS CLI also uses code. It does, but it doesn't follow the IaC principles. An IaC tool: Acts as a middleman between you and one or more cloud providers. You hand it a template, and it turns that into the provider's API calls. Works from templates that are declarative: you describe the end result, not the steps. Works from templates that are versioned: they live in Git or S3, so you can see what changed 5 or 10 days ago, review changes and audit them. Imperative (step by step) \"Create a bucket. Now enable versioning. Now add a tag.\" You give commands in order, like a CLI script. If you run it twice, you may get errors or duplicates. Declarative (end state) \"There should be one bucket, with versioning on, tagged app1.\" The tool works out what to create or change. In short: what you see is what you get. Reading the template tells you what exists in AWS. Think of it like…An architect's blueprint versus shouting instructions at builders. The blueprint can be reviewed, copied and reused for the next house, and anyone can read it to see what the house should look like. Which tools are IaC? AWS CloudFormation (CFT): AWS only. Azure Resource Manager: Azure only. Terraform, Crossplane: work with many cloud providers."},{"h":"How CloudFormation works","id":"s2","t":"A stack is the set of resources CloudFormation creates from one template. They're created, updated and deleted together. Template: your YAML or JSON file describing resources. Stack: what you get when you submit a template. CloudFormation builds every resource in it and tracks them as one unit. Delete the stack and its resources are deleted too. Update: edit the template and update the stack. CloudFormation works out what changed. A change set lets you preview those changes before applying them. YAML or JSON? CloudFormation accepts both. Choose YAML unless your team already has a lot of JSON: YAML supports comments (# this creates the web server), which matters when a team shares templates. JSON has no comments. YAML uses indentation (like Python) instead of nested braces, so it's easier to read. YAML is everywhere in DevOps: Kubernetes, Ansible, CI pipelines. Watch outIn YAML, indentation is the structure. Pick 2 spaces and stick to it, and never mix tabs and spaces."},{"h":"Anatomy of a template","id":"s3","t":"A template can have up to ten top-level sections, but only Resources is required. Everything else adds reuse, validation and documentation, which matters when many people share templates. Section What it's for AWSTemplateFormatVersion Format version. The only valid value is \"2010-09-09\", unchanged since 2010. Description Plain-text summary so you (in 20 days) and teammates know what this template does. Metadata Extra info, e.g. owner, team, project. Parameters Inputs supplied when the stack is created, so the same template works for many teams (e.g. instance type, environment). Rules Validate parameter values before anything is created, e.g. \"in prod, the instance type must be one of these\". Mappings Fixed lookup tables, e.g. region → AMI ID, read with !FindInMap. Conditions True/false checks (usually based on parameters) that decide whether a resource or setting is created. Transform Macros that expand the template, mainly AWS::Serverless (SAM) for serverless apps (Lesson 19). Resources (required) The actual AWS resources: buckets, instances, VPCs… Outputs Values to show after creation, e.g. an instance's public IP or a bucket name. TipInside Resources, the first key (e.g. MyBucket) is just a logical name you choose. You could call it abhishek1. What defines the resource is its Type, e.g. AWS::S3::Bucket or AWS::EC2::Instance. A template can hold hundreds of resources, each with its own logical name. Try it: template explorer Tap a section name, or any line of the template, to highlight that section and see what it does. Pick a section."},{"h":"Hands-on: create a stack from a template","id":"s4","t":"Write the template. Start from the docs: search \"CloudFormation template reference\", open Resource and property reference, find your service (e.g. Amazon S3 → AWS::S3::Bucket) and copy only the properties you need. Every property has YAML and JSON examples. Open CloudFormation in the console → Create stack. You'll see three choices: Template is ready (upload a file or give an S3 URL), Use a sample template, or build one in the visual designer (drag and drop a resource and it generates YAML or JSON; in today's console this is Infrastructure Composer). Upload your template.yaml, give the stack a name (e.g. s3-bucket), fill in any parameters, optionally choose an IAM role for CloudFormation to use, and click Submit. Watch the Events tab: CREATE_IN_PROGRESS changes to CREATE_COMPLETE. Check S3 and your bucket is there. You'll also see a cf-templates-… bucket that CloudFormation created automatically to store the templates you upload. # template.yaml — the smallest useful template AWSTemplateFormatVersion: \"2010-09-09\" Description: Demo bucket with versioning Resources: S3Bucket: Type: AWS::S3::Bucket Properties: BucketName: demo-aws-abhi-example.com # must be globally unique VersioningConfiguration: Status: Enabled You can do the same from the terminal: aws cloudformation deploy --template-file template.yaml --stack-name s3-bucket aws cloudformation delete-stack --stack-name s3-bucket NoteFor one bucket, a stack is overkill; the CLI or console is faster. CloudFormation pays off when you create a VPC, subnets, EC2 instances and S3 buckets together and want to manage them as one unit."},{"h":"Drift detection: catch manual changes","id":"s5","t":"You created a bucket with versioning enabled through CloudFormation. A week later, a teammate goes into the console and suspends versioning. Now reality no longer matches the template. That gap is called drift. In the stack, choose Stack actions → Detect drift, then View drift results. CloudFormation compares every resource with the template and reports: IN_SYNC: matches the template. MODIFIED: a property changed. View drift details shows expected vs actual side by side, e.g. Status: Enabled → Suspended. DELETED: someone removed the resource completely. With hundreds of resources, you can't check each one by hand. Drift detection tells you that someone changed something. Then you fix it, and use bucket access logs or CloudTrail to find out who, so you can tighten their permissions. Drift detection only runs when you ask for it, so teams often schedule it (e.g. a cron job or scheduled Lambda calling aws cloudformation detect-stack-drift). Try it: drift detection The template says VersioningConfiguration.Status: Enabled. Make a manual change, then run drift detection. Template (expected) Status: Enabled Real bucket (actual) Last drift check: never run."},{"h":"Tips for writing templates faster","id":"s6","t":"Start small. Just Resources and one resource. Add parameters and conditions once you're comfortable. Use the documentation. Nobody memorizes hundreds of properties. The template reference has an example for every resource. VS Code extensions: YAML (by Red Hat) flags indentation and syntax errors, and AWS Toolkit autocompletes CloudFormation. Type AWS::EC2:: and pick Instance, then add ImageId, InstanceType, KeyName, SecurityGroupIds. The visual designer and AI assistants are fine for a first draft, but always read and understand the result. PracticeWrite a template that creates one EC2 instance (image ID, instance type, key pair, security group) and deploy it as a stack. Then delete the stack to clean up."},{"h":"CloudFormation vs Terraform","id":"s7","t":"CloudFormation Terraform Clouds AWS only AWS, Azure, GCP, on-prem and more Language YAML / JSON HCL Who runs it A managed AWS service; state is tracked for you A tool you run; you manage the state file Pick it when Your company is all-in on AWS Multi-cloud or hybrid, or you want one tool everywhere If you can learn only one, Terraform is more widely asked for, because companies often run workloads on more than one cloud. Terraform is covered in Lesson 26. Interview angle\"Why CloudFormation and not Terraform?\" A good answer: \"Our organization runs only on AWS and has no plans to move clouds, so a native, AWS-managed IaC service with built-in drift detection was the simplest choice.\" If multi-cloud comes up, that's the case for Terraform."},{"h":"Check yourself","id":"s8","t":"Which template section is the only one that's required? Only Resources is required. The other sections add documentation, inputs, validation and outputs. A teammate disabled encryption on a stack's bucket through the console. How do you find out? Drift detection compares actual resources with the template and shows expected vs actual values. It doesn't fix or block changes on its own. In MyWebServer: / Type: AWS::S3::Bucket, what gets created? The Type line decides what's created. The logical name is just a label."},{"h":"Key takeaways","id":"s9","t":"IaC = describing infrastructure in declarative, versioned templates that a tool turns into API calls. CloudFormation takes YAML/JSON templates (prefer YAML) and creates a stack: resources managed as one unit. Only Resources is required. Each resource has a logical name, a Type and Properties. Parameters, Mappings, Conditions and Outputs add reuse. Drift detection reveals manual changes (MODIFIED / DELETED) by comparing reality with the template. Use the CLI for quick tasks and CloudFormation for full stacks. Choose Terraform when you need more than one cloud."}],"n":13,"slug":"13-cloudformation"},{"title":"CI/CD Basics & CodeCommit","lead":"CI/CD is how code gets from a developer's laptop to real users, automatically and safely. This lesson explains continuous integration and delivery, the Git basics you need, the AWS \"Code\" services that make up a pipeline on AWS, and AWS's own Git hosting service, CodeCommit, including why many teams still pick GitHub.","sections":[{"h":"The problem: shipping code by hand","id":"s1","t":"Without automation, every release looks like this: someone pulls the latest code, builds it on their machine, runs some tests (if they remember), copies files to a server and restarts the app. It's slow, every person does it a little differently, and bugs slip through. CI/CD turns those steps into an automatic pipeline that runs on every code change."},{"h":"CI, CD and CD","id":"s2","t":"Continuous Integration (CI) Developers merge small changes into a shared repository often. Every change is automatically built and tested. Problems show up within minutes, while the change is still fresh. Continuous Delivery (CD) Every change that passes CI is automatically packaged and deployed to test/staging, so it's always ready to release. Going to production needs one manual approval. Continuous Deployment (CD) Same as delivery, but with no manual gate: if every check passes, the change goes straight to production. Pipeline The ordered list of automatic steps (stages) a change goes through: checkout → build → test → scan → package → deploy. Typical CI stages The exact stages differ between companies and between web, mobile and ML apps, but a common set is: Code checkout: fetch the commit that triggered the pipeline. Build and unit tests: compile (e.g. Maven for Java) and run fast automated tests. Static code analysis (SAST): scan code for bugs and security issues with tools like SonarQube. Image build, scan and push: package the app as a Docker image, scan it for vulnerabilities and push it to a registry (Lesson 22). The CD part then deploys that package to servers (EC2), containers (ECS/Kubernetes) or serverless functions. One push starts the whole chain. A failure at any stage stops the pipeline before bad code reaches users. Try it: run a pipeline Push a commit and watch it move through the stages. Then try pushing a bug, or adding a manual approval gate to see the difference between continuous delivery and continuous deployment. Commit contains a bug (a unit test will fail) Require manual approval before production Waiting for a commit…"},{"h":"CI/CD tools, and the AWS versions","id":"s3","t":"A classic open-source setup uses four kinds of tools. AWS offers a managed service for each role: Role Common tool AWS managed service Host the code (version control) GitHub, GitLab, Bitbucket CodeCommit (this lesson) Orchestrate the pipeline Jenkins, GitHub Actions, GitLab CI CodePipeline (Lesson 15) Build and test Maven, Docker, SonarQube steps in Jenkins CodeBuild (Lesson 16) Deploy Argo CD, Ansible, shell scripts CodeDeploy (Lesson 17) The match-up isn't exact, but it's a useful map. With these four services you can run the whole CI/CD process inside AWS without installing Jenkins anywhere."},{"h":"Git basics you'll need","id":"s4","t":"Git is a version control system: it records every change to your files so you can see who changed what, go back in time, and work in parallel. Services like GitHub and CodeCommit host Git repositories. Repository (repo): a project folder plus its full history. Commit: a saved snapshot with a message (\"fix login bug\"). Branch: a separate line of work. Pull request: a request to merge a branch, where teammates review the code. Clone / push / pull: copy a remote repo to your machine / send your commits up / bring others' commits down. git --version # check Git is installed git clone <repo-url> # copy the repo locally cd demo-repo-cc git config --global user.name \"Your Name\" # one-time identity setup git config --global user.email \"you@example.com\" echo \"hello\" > test.txt git add test.txt # stage the change git commit -m \"Add test file\" # snapshot it git push # send it to the remote repo"},{"h":"What is AWS CodeCommit?","id":"s5","t":"CodeCommit is AWS's managed, private Git hosting, similar to GitHub or GitLab, but running inside your AWS account. Why would anyone use it? Inside companies, code is rarely in public repos. Teams use private GitHub repos, GitHub Enterprise, or run self-hosted GitLab on their own servers. Self-hosting means someone must add servers as repos and teams grow, patch them, secure them and keep them up. CodeCommit removes that work, just as EC2 took over compute and S3 took over storage: Managed: no Git servers to install, patch or back up. Scales automatically: 1 repo or 10,000, and you pay for what you use. Reliable: run by AWS with a service-level agreement (SLA). Always private: repos can't be made public. Access is controlled with IAM. Native AWS integration with CodePipeline, CloudWatch, SNS and so on. Think of it like…Renting a managed safe-deposit vault for your code inside your own AWS building, instead of buying, guarding and maintaining your own safes."},{"h":"Hands-on: create and use a repository","id":"s6","t":"In the console, search for CodeCommit and click Create repository. Name it (e.g. demo-repo-cc) and add a description. You can leave CodeGuru Reviewer off; it's an optional code reviewer limited to Java and Python. The repo page has what you'd expect: a clone URL, files, commits, branches and pull requests. You can upload or edit files in the browser, but only one file at a time. For real work you'll use Git from a terminal or VS Code. Create an IAM user (don't use root: SSH access doesn't work with the root user, and AWS warns against it). Attach the managed policy AWSCodeCommitPowerUser, which covers everything short of deleting repos, including notification integrations. For that user, go to IAM → Users → Security credentials → HTTPS Git credentials for AWS CodeCommit → Generate. This gives a special Git username and password. (Alternatives: SSH keys, or the git-remote-codecommit helper.) Sign in as the IAM user (account ID + username + password) and make sure you're in the same region as the repo. Repos are regional, so a different region shows an empty list. Copy the HTTPS clone URL and clone it. Git asks for the credentials from step 4. Then add, commit and push a file and watch it appear in the console. git clone https://git-codecommit.us-east-1.amazonaws.com/v1/repos/demo-repo-cc TipAuthentication fails even with correct credentials? Your machine may be sending other saved Git credentials (e.g. for GitHub) from its credential manager. Clear the stored entry for git-codecommit.*.amazonaws.com and try again."},{"h":"The catch: why many teams skip CodeCommit","id":"s7","t":"Pros Fully managed, scales on its own Private by default, IAM-based access Smooth integration with other AWS services AWS reliability and support Cons Far fewer features than GitHub/GitLab: no Copilot-style AI help, no built-in CI like Actions or Auto DevOps, no in-browser VS Code Integrations mostly limited to AWS services, with few third-party ones Plainer user interface Ties your code hosting to AWS Because of this, even companies that run everything else on AWS often keep their code in GitHub, GitLab or Bitbucket. That's why the following lessons connect CodePipeline to GitHub. It's the more common real-world setup, and more useful to talk about in interviews. Status noteCodeCommit stopped accepting new customers in June 2024, then AWS reversed that decision and returned it to general availability on 24 November 2025. New accounts can create repositories again. Interview angle\"CodeCommit or GitHub?\" Show you know the trade-off: CodeCommit removes the work of self-hosting Git and keeps code private inside AWS, but GitHub/GitLab have a much richer feature set and ecosystem, so most teams use them and connect them to CodePipeline."},{"h":"Check yourself","id":"s8","t":"What is the difference between continuous delivery and continuous deployment? Both automate build, test and release prep. Only continuous deployment pushes to production with no human gate. Which AWS service plays the role of the \"pipeline orchestrator\", like Jenkins? CodePipeline orchestrates the stages. CodeCommit hosts code, CodeBuild builds/tests, CodeDeploy deploys. Why is CodeCommit used less than GitHub, even by AWS-only companies? CodeCommit is real Git and always private, but its feature set and ecosystem are much thinner than GitHub's or GitLab's."},{"h":"Key takeaways","id":"s9","t":"CI = every change is automatically built and tested. Continuous delivery = always release-ready, with a manual prod gate. Continuous deployment = no gate. A pipeline is a chain of stages (checkout → build → test → scan → package → deploy) triggered by a Git push. AWS's CI/CD services: CodeCommit (code), CodePipeline (orchestration), CodeBuild (build/test), CodeDeploy (deploy). CodeCommit is managed, private, scalable Git. Use an IAM user with AWSCodeCommitPowerUser and HTTPS Git credentials, not root. Its weak spot is features and integrations, so many teams pair GitHub/GitLab with the other AWS Code services."}],"n":14,"slug":"14-cicd-codecommit"},{"title":"CodePipeline vs Jenkins","lead":"AWS CodePipeline is AWS's managed pipeline orchestrator. The quickest way to understand it is to compare it with a Jenkins setup you may already know. You'll see how CodeCommit, CodePipeline, CodeBuild and CodeDeploy fit together, and how to explain in an interview when to choose CodePipeline and when to choose Jenkins.","sections":[{"h":"","id":"","t":"New to CI/CD? Continuous integration, delivery and pipeline stages are explained in Lesson 14: CI/CD Basics & CodeCommit."},{"h":"The classic Jenkins flow","id":"s1","t":"A developer commits a change (e.g. to a .java or .html file) and pushes it to a Git host such as GitHub, GitLab or Bitbucket. A webhook, an automatic \"something happened\" message from GitHub, triggers the Jenkins pipeline. The pipeline is written in Groovy, as a declarative or scripted pipeline. Jenkins implements CI itself: checkout → build and unit tests → static code analysis (SonarQube) → Docker image build → image scan → image push. Jenkins then invokes CD, handing the deployment to another tool: Argo CD or Flux (GitOps, the modern way), Spinnaker, or older approaches like Ansible or shell scripts running kubectl/helm. The app lands on Kubernetes or virtual machines. NoteGitOps means Git is the single source of truth for what should be running. A tool like Argo CD keeps checking the cluster against Git and puts back anything someone changed by hand. Similar in spirit to CloudFormation's drift detection, except Argo CD fixes the drift automatically."},{"h":"The same flow with AWS managed services","id":"s2","t":"The developer pushes code to CodeCommit, or, more commonly, to GitHub connected to AWS. The change triggers CodePipeline, which takes Jenkins's place as the orchestrator. CodePipeline invokes CI by calling CodeBuild, which runs the build, tests, scans and image push (Lesson 16). CodePipeline invokes CD by calling CodeDeploy, which rolls the app out to EC2 instances, on-prem servers, ECS or Lambda (Lesson 17). Put the two pictures side by side: GitHub ↔ CodeCommit, Jenkins ↔ CodePipeline, Jenkins build stages ↔ CodeBuild, Argo CD ↔ CodeDeploy. Interview angleNote the wording: Jenkins usually implements CI (the stages run inside Jenkins) and invokes CD. CodePipeline usually invokes both: it does very little work itself and calls CodeBuild and CodeDeploy. You can skip CodeBuild and run scripts in other ways, but CodePipeline + CodeBuild + CodeDeploy is the standard pattern."},{"h":"How a CodePipeline is put together","id":"s3","t":"Pipeline: the whole workflow for one app. Stages: run in order, e.g. Source → Build → Approval → Deploy. If a stage fails, the pipeline stops there. Actions: the tasks inside a stage, each run by a provider: GitHub/CodeCommit/S3 for source, CodeBuild for build/test, CodeDeploy/ECS/CloudFormation for deploy, a manual approval, or a Lambda invoke. Artifacts: the files passed from one stage to the next (source code, build output). CodePipeline stores them in an S3 artifact bucket. Trigger: usually a push to a branch, received through a connection to your Git host. Watch outCodeDeploy deploys to EC2/on-prem servers, ECS and Lambda, not to EKS. For Kubernetes on AWS, teams typically run kubectl/helm from a CodeBuild step, or use a GitOps tool like Argo CD (see Lesson 24: EKS)."},{"h":"If Jenkins is free, why pay for CodePipeline?","id":"s4","t":"Jenkins software is free, but running it isn't. A typical Jenkins setup has a main server plus worker nodes (agents) that run the builds. As teams add pipelines, two agents become 4, 8, 16, 20 virtual machines, and someone has to: scale them up and down, apply security patches and upgrade plugins, watch their health, clean up disks and replace broken nodes. Often that needs a dedicated engineer. AWS spotted this management burden, as it did with compute (EC2) and storage (S3), and offers CodePipeline as a managed, pay-as-you-go service. Startups and small teams that don't want to babysit CI servers find that very appealing. …and why Jenkins is still more popular Managing it isn't always hard. With Docker agents, each build runs in a container that's created on demand and deleted afterwards, so a couple of servers can handle a lot. You can also run Jenkins on EC2 with Auto Scaling and CloudWatch alarms. No lock-in. A Jenkinsfile kept in Git works on AWS, Azure, GCP or on-prem. If your company moves clouds or goes hybrid, you move Jenkins and keep your pipelines. CodePipeline definitions only work on AWS. Huge ecosystem. Thousands of plugins and integrations with almost any tool. Cost control. Pay-as-you-go services can get expensive if they're used carelessly. Jenkins AWS CodePipeline Type Open-source tool you host Fully managed AWS service Cost Free software; you pay for servers and the people who run them Pay as you go; no servers to run Who maintains it You: scaling, patching, plugins, backups AWS Portability Any cloud or on-prem AWS only Integrations Thousands of plugins Mainly AWS services, plus GitHub and some partners Typical role Implements CI, invokes CD Orchestrates: invokes CodeBuild (CI) and CodeDeploy (CD) Try it: Jenkins or CodePipeline? Answer four questions about a company and see which tool fits better, and why. TipLearning CodePipeline is still worthwhile even if Jenkins, GitHub Actions or GitLab CI are more common. Some companies want everything on AWS managed services, and the pipeline ideas carry over to any tool."},{"h":"Check yourself","id":"s5","t":"In a standard AWS setup, which service does CodePipeline call to run the build and unit tests? CodeBuild handles the CI work. CodeDeploy handles deployment, and CodeCommit only hosts code. Your company runs half its workloads on AWS and half on Azure. Which choice is safer for pipelines? CodePipeline only works on AWS. Jenkins pipelines stored in Git can run anywhere. What's the main benefit a startup gets from CodePipeline? The selling point is the managed service. You pay per use instead of paying engineers to run CI infrastructure."},{"h":"Key takeaways","id":"s6","t":"CodePipeline ↔ Jenkins, CodeCommit ↔ GitHub, CodeBuild ↔ CI build stages, CodeDeploy ↔ Argo CD/Ansible. A pipeline is made of stages (Source, Build, Approval, Deploy…) containing actions. Artifacts pass between them through S3. Jenkins usually implements CI and invokes CD. CodePipeline invokes both, via CodeBuild and CodeDeploy. Pick CodePipeline for an AWS-only shop that doesn't want to run CI servers. Pick Jenkins for portability, plugins and full control. In real setups, CodePipeline is usually paired with GitHub rather than CodeCommit."}],"n":15,"slug":"15-codepipeline"},{"title":"Project: CI with CodeBuild","lead":"Build a real continuous integration pipeline: every commit to GitHub automatically builds a Docker image of a Python app and pushes it to Docker Hub, using AWS CodeBuild and CodePipeline. You'll also see the errors you're likely to hit, and how to fix each one.","sections":[{"h":"","id":"","t":"This is a hands-on project. What CI/CD means is covered in Lesson 14, and how CodePipeline coordinates other services is in Lesson 15. This lesson does only the CI half. The deploy (CD) half comes next in Lesson 17."},{"h":"What we're building","id":"s1","t":"The goal: a developer pushes code, and with no manual steps a fresh, tested Docker image ends up in a registry, ready to deploy. Four pieces work together: GitHub holds the source code. GitHub is used here instead of CodeCommit because it's what most teams use, and it needs a bit of extra setup. CodePipeline is the orchestrator. It notices the commit and tells the other services to run, in order. CodeBuild does the actual work: installs dependencies, builds the Docker image, pushes it. Systems Manager Parameter Store keeps the Docker Hub username and password out of the code. The CI flow. CodePipeline only coordinates; CodeBuild runs the phases written in buildspec.yml."},{"h":"The sample application","id":"s2","t":"The app is a tiny Python Flask \"hello world\" web app. The steps are exactly the same for a big app. The repo folder contains: app.py: the Flask code. requirements.txt: Python dependencies (just flask). Dockerfile: starts from a Python base image, copies requirements.txt, installs it, copies the code, exposes port 5000 and starts Flask. New to Docker images and registries? The quick primer is in Lesson 22: ECR. Here we push to Docker Hub; ECR is AWS's own registry."},{"h":"Step 1: Create the CodeBuild project","id":"s3","t":"CodeBuild is a fully managed build service. You don't run any build servers. When a build starts, AWS spins up a fresh build machine, runs your commands, then throws it away. You pay per build minute. Name and description. In the console, open CodeBuild → Create build project. Give it a clear name (e.g. sample-python-flask-service). In a company you'd name it after the real service, like payments-service. You can leave the concurrent build limit off. Source. Pick GitHub. CodeBuild supports GitHub, GitHub Enterprise, Bitbucket, CodeCommit and S3. For anything else (e.g. GitLab), you mirror the repo to a supported source, meaning you keep a copy that's updated automatically. Click Connect to GitHub, choose OAuth, and confirm in the pop-up. Then enter your repository. Environment. This is the machine your build runs on. Choose a managed image, operating system Ubuntu, runtime Standard, and the latest image. In Jenkins you'd have to create and maintain these worker nodes yourself; here AWS provides them ready-made. Service role. CodeBuild needs permission to call other AWS services. Services get permissions through an IAM role, not an IAM user (users are for people). Let the console create a new service role, or create one in IAM → Roles → Create role → trusted service CodeBuild. You'll add permissions to it later. Buildspec. Choose Insert build commands → Switch to editor to write the build file in the console (next section). You could also keep buildspec.yml in your repo, which is the more common choice in real teams. Roles vs users, and why services use roles, are explained in Lesson 3: IAM."},{"h":"Step 2: Write the buildspec.yml","id":"s4","t":"The buildspec is a YAML file that tells CodeBuild what to run. It's CodeBuild's version of a Jenkinsfile or a GitHub Actions workflow. The editor gives you a commented-out template, so you mostly uncomment what you need. Your commands go into four phases, which always run in this order: Phase What goes here In this project install Pick language runtimes, install tools Python 3.11 pre_build Things needed before building: dependencies, logins pip install -r requirements.txt build The main work: compile, test, build the image docker login, build, push post_build Wrap-up: notifications, extra pushes Print \"build is successful\" You don't need a \"checkout\" step. CodeBuild clones the source repo for you before install starts. version: 0.2 env: parameter-store: DOCKER_REGISTRY_USERNAME: /myapp/docker-credentials/username DOCKER_REGISTRY_PASSWORD: /myapp/docker-credentials/password DOCKER_REGISTRY_URL: /myapp/docker-registry/url phases: install: runtime-versions: python: 3.11 pre_build: commands: - echo \"Installing dependencies...\" - pip install -r simple-python-app/requirements.txt build: commands: - cd simple-python-app - echo \"Building Docker image...\" - echo \"$DOCKER_REGISTRY_PASSWORD\" | docker login -u \"$DOCKER_REGISTRY_USERNAME\" --password-stdin \"$DOCKER_REGISTRY_URL\" - docker build -t \"$DOCKER_REGISTRY_URL/$DOCKER_REGISTRY_USERNAME/simple-python-flask-app:latest\" . - docker push \"$DOCKER_REGISTRY_URL/$DOCKER_REGISTRY_USERNAME/simple-python-flask-app:latest\" post_build: commands: - echo \"Build is successful\" A few things to notice: Paths are relative to the repo root. The app lives in a sub-folder (simple-python-app/), so the pip install path and the cd must include it. If your code is at the repo root, drop the folder part. Runtime matches your app. Python app → python. A Java app would use java, a Go app golang. No secrets in the file. The username, password and registry URL are environment variables filled in from Parameter Store at build time. The image tag looks like docker.io/<username>/simple-python-flask-app:latest. That's registry / user / image name : tag."},{"h":"Step 3: Store credentials in Parameter Store","id":"s5","t":"If you write your Docker Hub password in the buildspec, anyone who can read the file can use your account. Instead, store it in AWS Systems Manager Parameter Store. It plays the same role as Jenkins' credential store or GitHub Actions secrets. Open Systems Manager → Parameter Store → Create parameter. Name it with a clear, consistent pattern, for example /myapp/docker-credentials/username. The pattern is app → kind of credential → field. Once you have hundreds of secrets for many services, a naming standard is what lets you find the right one. Choose type SecureString (encrypted) and keep the default encryption key. Enter the value. Create three parameters: username, password, and registry URL (docker.io). In the buildspec's env: parameter-store: block, the left side is the variable name your commands use (you choose it). The right side must match the parameter name exactly, because that's where the value comes from. When to use Parameter Store, Secrets Manager or Vault (and how rotation works) is covered in Lesson 25: Secrets Management."},{"h":"Step 4: Run the build and fix the errors","id":"s6","t":"Click Start build. Your first build will very likely fail, and that's normal. Reading the build log and fixing one error at a time is the real skill here. These are the errors you're most likely to see, in roughly the order they show up: Error in the log Why Fix Access denied reading the parameters (ssm:GetParameters) The CodeBuild service role isn't allowed to read Parameter Store. IAM → Roles → your CodeBuild role → Add permissions → attach an SSM policy (read-only access is enough; full access is fine only for a throwaway demo). Could not open requirements file: No such file or directory The path in the buildspec doesn't match the repo (e.g. a typo in the folder name). Edit the buildspec with the exact path. \"docker build\" requires exactly 1 argument The . at the end of docker build is missing. The dot is the build context: \"use the Dockerfile and files in this folder\". Add the trailing . Cannot connect to the Docker daemon By default, CodeBuild build machines aren't allowed to run Docker builds. Edit project → Environment → Override image → tick Privileged. denied: requested access to the resource is denied on push The credentials were loaded but nobody logged in to the registry. Add the docker login line before build/push. TipRead the build logs even when builds pass. They show exactly what CodeBuild does in each phase, and that makes the next failure much faster to debug. Once the build succeeds, open Docker Hub, go to your user's repositories and you'll see simple-python-flask-app with a latest tag updated a minute ago. Try it: run a CodeBuild build Tick the fixes you've applied, then start a build. The simulated log stops at the first problem, just like a real build. Turn on all five to get a green build. Service role can read Parameter Store Correct path to requirements.txt docker login step included docker build ends with . Privileged mode enabled Build log appears here."},{"h":"Step 5: Automate it with CodePipeline","id":"s7","t":"Right now you have to press Start build yourself. To make it real CI, let CodePipeline start CodeBuild whenever someone commits: CodePipeline → Create pipeline. Give it a name and let it create a new service role. Source stage: provider GitHub (Version 2). Click Connect to GitHub, name the connection and approve it. This connection is how GitHub tells the pipeline \"something changed\". Pick the repository and branch main. Build stage: provider AWS CodeBuild, choose the project you just made, single build. Deploy stage: skip it for now. It gets added in the CD lesson. Review and Create pipeline. It runs once straight away. Now test it: commit any small change to the repo. The pipeline's Source stage turns \"In progress\", then Build. Click Details to watch the CodeBuild job, and a minute or two later Docker Hub shows the image as \"pushed a few seconds ago\". No one pressed a button. Interview angle\"Walk me through a CI pipeline you built on AWS.\" Mention the trigger (GitHub connection → CodePipeline), what the buildspec phases do, how secrets are kept out of the code (Parameter Store + IAM role permissions), and one real error you fixed, such as privileged mode for Docker builds. Real errors you've fixed make your answer believable."},{"h":"Check yourself","id":"s8","t":"Your CodeBuild log says \"Cannot connect to the Docker daemon\". What do you change? CodeBuild build machines can't run Docker builds unless the environment is in privileged mode. Why is the Docker password stored in Parameter Store instead of the buildspec? The buildspec is usually readable by the whole team, or even public. A SecureString parameter keeps the secret encrypted, and only roles with permission can read it. In this setup, what does CodePipeline do? CodePipeline is the orchestrator. CodeBuild does the building, and Docker Hub stores the image."},{"h":"Key takeaways","id":"s9","t":"CodeBuild is a managed build service: AWS provides the build machine, you provide a buildspec.yml. Buildspec phases run in order: install → pre_build → build → post_build. The source checkout happens automatically. Keep secrets in Parameter Store (SecureString) and load them with env: parameter-store:. The CodeBuild service role needs permission to read them. Docker builds in CodeBuild need Privileged mode, a docker login, and the trailing . on docker build. CodePipeline with a GitHub connection makes it real CI: every commit triggers a build automatically."}],"n":16,"slug":"16-codebuild-ci"},{"title":"Project: CD with CodeDeploy","lead":"Finish the pipeline from the previous lesson. After CodeBuild pushes a new Docker image, CodeDeploy automatically pulls it onto an EC2 server and runs it. By the end, one git commit takes your code all the way to a running app.","sections":[{"h":"","id":"","t":"This continues Lesson 16: CI with CodeBuild. Do that first: CD isn't much use without a CI pipeline that produces something to deploy."},{"h":"The complete CI/CD picture","id":"s1","t":"CodePipeline stays the orchestrator. It now has three stages: Source (GitHub) → Build (CodeBuild) → Deploy (CodeDeploy). CodeDeploy doesn't copy a Docker image itself. It tells a small program on the server (the CodeDeploy agent) to run your scripts, and those scripts pull and start the image. CI (top branch) produces the image; CD (bottom branch) gets it running on EC2."},{"h":"CodeDeploy's building blocks","id":"s2","t":"CodeDeploy is AWS's managed deployment service. It can deploy to EC2 (and on-premises servers), ECS, or Lambda. This project uses EC2. Five terms to know: Application A named container for one thing you deploy. Usually one per microservice: payments, login, and so on. It's like a folder. On its own it doesn't know where or how to deploy. Deployment group The where: the set of servers to deploy to, picked by tags. Similar to a load balancer's target group. Deployment One actual rollout of one version (a revision) of your code, taken from GitHub or S3, to a deployment group. appspec.yml The how: a file in your repo listing which scripts to run at each step of the deployment. It's CodeDeploy's version of buildspec.yml. CodeDeploy agent A small program installed on each target server. It checks in with CodeDeploy, downloads the revision and runs your scripts. Like a Jenkins agent or a GitHub Actions runner."},{"h":"Step 1: Create the application","id":"s3","t":"Open CodeDeploy → Applications → Create application. Give it a name (e.g. sample-python-flask-app) and choose compute platform EC2/On-premises. In a company with 200 microservices you'd repeat this 200 times, which is why teams script it with the CLI once they understand the steps in the console."},{"h":"Step 2: Launch and tag the EC2 instance","id":"s4","t":"Launch an Ubuntu t2.micro in the default VPC with a public IP and your key pair (Lesson 4 covers this). The new part is the tag. A tag is a key/value label you attach to an AWS resource, such as Name = sample-python or project = payments. CodeDeploy uses tags to find its targets. There could be many instances called \"example.com\" in an account, but a deployment group that says \"every instance tagged Name = sample-python\" is clear. This also scales: tag 10 more servers the same way and they automatically become deployment targets. Tags also matter for tracking cost and ownership across teams. Tagging best practices are covered in Lesson 36: Reducing Your AWS Bill."},{"h":"Step 3: Install the CodeDeploy agent","id":"s5","t":"SSH into the instance (Lesson 5) and follow these steps exactly. Skipped steps here cause most CodeDeploy problems. The agent is written in Ruby, so install Ruby first. The download bucket must match your instance's region; for us-east-1 it looks like this: sudo apt update sudo apt install -y ruby-full wget cd /home/ubuntu # bucket name + region must match where your instance runs wget https://aws-codedeploy-us-east-1.s3.us-east-1.amazonaws.com/latest/install chmod +x ./install sudo ./install auto # check it's running; start it if it says \"No AWS CodeDeploy agent running\" sudo service codedeploy-agent status sudo service codedeploy-agent start While you're there, install Docker too, since your deploy scripts will need it: sudo apt install -y docker.io Watch outForgetting Docker on the server is a classic first failure. The deployment reaches your start script and fails with docker: command not found."},{"h":"Step 4: Give the instance and CodeDeploy their roles","id":"s6","t":"Two things need AWS permissions, and both get them from IAM roles (roles are for services, users are for people, as covered in Lesson 3): The EC2 instance, so the agent on it can talk to CodeDeploy and fetch revisions. Create a role with EC2 as the trusted service, then attach it: instance → Actions → Security → Modify IAM role. CodeDeploy itself, so it can look up your instances and tags. Create a role with CodeDeploy as the trusted service (it suggests the AWSCodeDeployRole policy). You'll pick this as the deployment group's service role. For a quick demo, one role with broad EC2 + CodeDeploy permissions works. In real projects, keep them separate and give each one only the permissions it needs. TipAfter you attach or change the instance's IAM role, restart the agent (sudo service codedeploy-agent restart) and check its status again. Otherwise the agent may keep using its old (missing) credentials and never connect."},{"h":"Step 5: Create the deployment group","id":"s7","t":"Open your application → Create deployment group. Name it and choose the CodeDeploy service role. Deployment type: choose In-place (update the existing servers). Blue/green creates a second set of servers and switches traffic over through a load balancer. It's safer, but more setup than we need here. Environment configuration: Amazon EC2 instances, tag Name = sample-python. The console immediately says \"1 unique matched instance\". Type a wrong value and it drops to 0, which is a handy check. Agent install: skip it (you already installed it). Load balancer: untick. Create."},{"h":"Step 6: Write appspec.yml and the scripts","id":"s8","t":"When the source is GitHub, appspec.yml must sit at the root of the repository and have exactly that name. (CodeBuild let you put the buildspec anywhere; CodeDeploy doesn't.) For this project it runs two scripts: version: 0.0 os: linux hooks: ApplicationStop: - location: scripts/stop_container.sh timeout: 300 runas: root AfterInstall: - location: scripts/start_container.sh timeout: 300 runas: root scripts/start_container.sh pulls the image CI just pushed and runs it. The app listens on port 5000 (that's what the Dockerfile exposes), so map host 5000 to container 5000: #!/bin/bash set -e docker pull <your-dockerhub-user>/simple-python-flask-app:latest docker run -d -p 5000:5000 <your-dockerhub-user>/simple-python-flask-app:latest scripts/stop_container.sh removes the container from the previous deployment. Without it, the next deployment fails because port 5000 is already in use: #!/bin/bash # Remove any running containers so port 5000 is free container_ids=$(docker ps -q) if [ -n \"$container_ids\" ]; then docker rm -f $container_ids fi NoteYou'll often see this written as docker ps | awk '{print $1}' to grab the container ID from the first column. That works if you skip the header row. docker ps -q prints just the IDs, so you don't need to deal with the header. The lifecycle hooks A deployment on EC2 moves through fixed steps called lifecycle events. You attach scripts to the ones you care about, which are called hooks. In order: Event What happens Your script? ApplicationStop Stop the old version. Runs the stop script from the previous revision, so it does nothing on the very first deployment. Yes DownloadBundle Agent downloads the revision (your repo at that commit). No (agent) BeforeInstall Prep work, e.g. backups, before files are copied. Optional Install Agent copies files to their destination. No (agent) AfterInstall Configure or start things after the copy. We start the container here. Yes ApplicationStart Start services (a common alternative place to start the app). Optional ValidateService Check the new version works, e.g. curl a health endpoint. Optional Try it: run a CodeDeploy deployment Set up the situation, then deploy. Watch each lifecycle event the way you would in the console's View events page. Try a second deployment with the old container still running and the stop script left as a placeholder. appspec.yml is at the repo root Docker is installed on the EC2 instance A container from an earlier deployment is still running stop_container.sh really removes containers (not just echo hi) Deployment status appears here."},{"h":"Step 7: Test a deployment by hand","id":"s9","t":"Before connecting it to the pipeline, check that CD works by itself. In your application → Create deployment: pick the deployment group, revision type \"My application is stored in GitHub\", connect your GitHub account, enter user/repo and a commit ID (copy it from the repo's commit history). Leave the rest at defaults and create. If something fails, open the deployment → View events. It shows which lifecycle event failed and the script's error output. These are the failures you'll most likely see: Fails right after downloading → no appspec.yml at the repo root. AfterInstall fails with docker: command not found → install docker.io on the instance. AfterInstall fails with \"port is already allocated\" → an old container still holds port 5000. Fix stop_container.sh (or remove it once by hand with sudo docker rm -f <id>). When it succeeds, sudo docker ps on the instance shows the container running. To open the app in a browser at http://<public-ip>:5000, allow inbound port 5000 in the instance's security group."},{"h":"Step 8: Add the Deploy stage to CodePipeline","id":"s10","t":"Open the pipeline from the CI lesson → Edit → Add stage after Build (name it e.g. Deploy). Add action group: action provider AWS CodeDeploy, input artifact SourceArtifact (the repo, which contains appspec.yml and the scripts), then your application name and deployment group. Save. Commit a small change, or click Release change, and watch Source → Build → Deploy all turn green. That's full CI/CD on AWS: a commit triggers the pipeline, CodeBuild builds and pushes a new image, and CodeDeploy pulls it onto the server and restarts the container. Tip: tag your imagesThis project uses the latest tag everywhere for simplicity. In real pipelines, push each image with a unique tag (a version number, a date/time stamp, or the commit ID) and have the deploy script pull that same tag. The rule: whatever tag CI pushes, CD must pull. Unique tags also let you see exactly which version is running and roll back to an earlier one. Interview angle\"How does CodeDeploy know which servers to deploy to?\" Through the deployment group, which selects instances by tags (or an Auto Scaling group). And each instance needs the CodeDeploy agent running, plus an IAM role that lets it talk to CodeDeploy."},{"h":"Check yourself","id":"s11","t":"Your app's source is on GitHub. Where must appspec.yml be? For GitHub revisions, CodeDeploy looks for appspec.yml at the root of the revision. Unlike CodeBuild's buildspec, its location can't be changed. The second deployment fails in AfterInstall with \"port is already allocated\". The cleanest fix is… The old container still holds port 5000. A proper stop script frees it before the new container starts. Changing ports just moves the problem to the next deployment. You just attached an IAM role to the EC2 instance. What should you do next? Restart the agent so it picks up the new credentials. Otherwise it may not be able to reach CodeDeploy."},{"h":"Key takeaways","id":"s12","t":"CodeDeploy = application (what) + deployment group (where, chosen by tags) + appspec.yml (how) + an agent on every target server. Install the agent (Ruby + region-specific installer), give the instance an IAM role, and restart the agent after role changes. appspec.yml lives at the repo root and attaches scripts to lifecycle hooks like ApplicationStop and AfterInstall. Always write a real stop script. Otherwise the second deployment hits \"port already allocated\". Add CodeDeploy as the Deploy stage in CodePipeline to get one-commit-to-production CI/CD. Use matching, unique image tags in CI and CD."}],"n":17,"slug":"17-codedeploy-cd"},{"title":"CloudWatch: Metrics, Logs, Alarms","lead":"CloudWatch is AWS's built-in monitoring service. It collects numbers (metrics) and text records (logs) from your resources, shows them on graphs and dashboards, and alerts you when something goes wrong. That way nobody has to watch servers 24/7.","sections":[{"h":"What CloudWatch is","id":"s1","t":"Think of it like…A security guard for a property. When you come back you can ask, \"What happened while I was away?\" The guard can also be told, \"If you see smoke, call me right away.\" CloudWatch does both for your AWS account: it keeps a record of what happened and raises the alarm when a rule is broken. In one sentence for an interview: CloudWatch is the AWS service for monitoring, alerting, reporting and logging of your resources and applications. Its main features: Metrics: numbers measured over time, like CPU %, network bytes, request count. Many are collected automatically. Alarms: rules on a metric (\"if CPU ≥ 80%…\") that trigger an action such as an email or scaling out. Logs (plus Logs Insights for searching them): text output from services and apps. Custom metrics: your own numbers, for things AWS doesn't measure by default. Dashboards: your chosen graphs on one screen. CloudWatch doesn't scale servers or cut costs by itself, but it's what triggers those actions. For example, an alarm can tell an Auto Scaling group to add a server, or a schedule can start a Lambda function that cleans up waste (see Lesson 20). Data flows in as metrics and logs. Alarms watch metrics and act: notify people through SNS, or call other services."},{"h":"Monitoring vs logging","id":"s2","t":"Monitoring (metrics) Watching numbers over time to answer \"how healthy is it right now?\" Example: CPU was 35% at 10:00, 92% at 10:05. Small, regular data points that are easy to graph and alert on. Logging Keeping text records of events to answer \"what exactly happened?\" Example: ERROR: Could not open requirements.txt. Detailed, used for debugging and audits. You need both. A metric tells you that something is wrong (\"CPU at 100%\"), and logs help you find out why. NoteThe record of who called which AWS API (e.g. \"user X deleted bucket Y at 14:02\") is kept by a sister service, AWS CloudTrail. CloudTrail can also deliver those records into CloudWatch Logs so you can search and alarm on them there."},{"h":"Logs: log groups and streams","id":"s3","t":"Many AWS services send their logs to CloudWatch Logs automatically, with no setup. Logs are organised in two levels: Log group: one per source, e.g. one per CodeBuild project (/aws/codebuild/sample-python-flask-service) or one per Lambda function. This stops 100 projects' logs from mixing together. Log stream: one sequence of events inside a group, e.g. one build run. Example: open the log group of the CodeBuild project from Lesson 16. Every build is there: the successful ones with the full Docker build output, and the early failed one with Could not open requirements file. The logs stay there even if you delete the CodeBuild project. By default log groups keep data forever, so set a retention period to control storage cost. Logs Insights lets you query logs with a small query language, for example to find the latest errors: fields @timestamp, @message | filter @message like /ERROR/ | sort @timestamp desc | limit 20"},{"h":"Metrics","id":"s4","t":"Out of the box, CloudWatch tracks over a thousand metrics across the services in an account, grouped by service (namespace, e.g. AWS/EC2). For EC2 you'll find CPUUtilization, NetworkIn/NetworkOut, disk I/O, CPU credit usage and more, either per instance or across all instances. You only need the few that matter for your project. You can see the same data on the EC2 console's Monitoring tab. That view is CloudWatch underneath. How often: basic vs detailed monitoring Basic monitoring (free): EC2 sends metrics every 5 minutes. Detailed monitoring (paid): every 1 minute. Enable it from the instance's Monitoring tab → Manage detailed monitoring. Statistic and period: Average vs Maximum A graph point is a statistic (Average, Maximum, Minimum, Sum…) over a period (1 min, 5 min…). This choice matters: Maximum catches every brief spike. Good for demos, but noisy: CPU touching 100% for one second isn't a real problem. Average over e.g. 5 minutes shows sustained pressure. Production alarms usually use Average, so CPU stuck above 70% for 5+ minutes pages someone and a one-second blip doesn't. Custom metrics Some things are not collected by default. The classic example is memory usage on EC2: AWS can see the CPU from outside your instance, but not how much RAM your programs use inside it. To track that, or any application number like \"orders per minute\", something has to send it to CloudWatch. That's a custom metric. You can send one with the CloudWatch agent installed on the server, or from your own code or the CLI: aws cloudwatch put-metric-data \\ --namespace \"MyApp\" \\ --metric-name MemoryUsedPercent \\ --unit Percent \\ --value 63 Once it's in CloudWatch, a custom metric works like any built-in one: graphs, dashboards, alarms."},{"h":"Alarms and SNS notifications","id":"s5","t":"Collecting metrics is only half the job. Engineers can't stare at graphs all day, so an alarm watches a metric for you and acts when it crosses a threshold. An alarm is always in one of three states: State Meaning OK Metric is within the threshold. ALARM Metric crossed the threshold for the required number of periods, so actions fire. INSUFFICIENT_DATA Not enough data yet (just created, instance stopped, metric not arriving). The usual action is to send a message through Amazon SNS (Simple Notification Service). You create an SNS topic, which is a named channel, and add subscriptions: who gets messages and how (email, SMS, Slack via a webhook or chat integration, a Lambda function…). The alarm publishes to the topic, and SNS delivers the message to every subscriber. Try it: CPU alarm → SNS email Each tick is one 1-minute period with six samples. Drag the CPU load up past the threshold and watch the alarm change state and \"send\" an email. Then compare Average and Maximum with a load just under the threshold: occasional spikes trip Maximum but not Average. CPU load: 20% Threshold (≥ %) Statistic Email subscription confirmed Alarm state: INSUFFICIENT_DATA Inbox No messages yet."},{"h":"Hands-on: alert when EC2 CPU spikes","id":"s6","t":"Launch an instance. Ubuntu, t2.micro, public IP. Turn on detailed monitoring so metrics arrive every minute instead of every five. Look at the metric. CloudWatch → Metrics → All metrics → EC2 → Per-Instance Metrics → your instance ID → CPUUtilization. It's flat near 0% for now. Create the alarm. Alarms → Create alarm → select that metric. Statistic Maximum, period 1 minute (just for a quick demo; use Average over 5 minutes in real life). Condition: Greater/Equal than 50. Add the notification. Action: \"In alarm\" → Create new topic (e.g. cloudwatch-topic) → your email address. Give the alarm a clear name and description, e.g. \"Priority: EC2 CPU ≥ 50%. Automated notification from CloudWatch, please take action.\" Confirm the subscription. AWS emails a confirmation link. Until you click it, nothing is delivered. Check spam and promotions folders. You can see the status under SNS → Topics → Subscriptions (\"Confirmed\"). Generate load. SSH in and burn some CPU, then stop it: yes > /dev/null & # start a CPU-hungry process top # watch CPU climb (q to quit) pkill yes # stop it Watch it fire. Within a minute or two the alarm goes red (ALARM) and the email arrives with the metric, threshold and time. Watch outNever run CPU-burning scripts on a production server or your work laptop. At 100% CPU the machine becomes unresponsive. Use a throwaway demo instance and terminate it afterwards."},{"h":"Dashboards","id":"s7","t":"A dashboard puts the graphs you care about on one page: a line chart of CPU for all web servers, a number widget for error count, an alarm status widget. Each widget can show one metric or several, as lines, stacked areas, bars or pie charts. Teams often put one on a TV in the office or use it as the first page they check during an incident. Interview angleInterviewers like real problems you've run into. Good examples from this lab: metrics showing up a few minutes late (basic monitoring is every 5 minutes); an alarm stuck in INSUFFICIENT_DATA; alert emails never arriving because the SNS subscription wasn't confirmed or they landed in spam; choosing Average over Maximum to avoid false alarms; and memory not being a default EC2 metric, so it needs a custom metric."},{"h":"Check yourself","id":"s8","t":"You want to alert on EC2 memory usage, but there's no memory metric in CloudWatch. Why? AWS can't see RAM usage from outside the instance. Use the CloudWatch agent (or put-metric-data) to publish it as a custom metric. Your alarm is created but no email ever arrives, even in ALARM state. What do you check first? SNS won't deliver to an email address until the subscription is confirmed via the link AWS sends (check spam and promotions too). For a production CPU alarm, which setting avoids paging people for one-second spikes? Average over a few minutes only fires on sustained load. Maximum fires on every brief spike."},{"h":"Key takeaways","id":"s9","t":"CloudWatch handles monitoring, alerting, reporting and logging for AWS resources and apps. Metrics are numbers over time (monitoring); logs are text records of events (debugging). Logs live in log groups → log streams. EC2 sends metrics every 5 minutes by default, or every 1 minute with detailed monitoring. Memory needs a custom metric. Alarms watch a metric against a threshold (OK / ALARM / INSUFFICIENT_DATA) and act, usually by publishing to an SNS topic that emails subscribers. Use Average over several minutes in production, confirm SNS subscriptions, and use dashboards to see everything at a glance."}],"n":18,"slug":"18-cloudwatch"},{"title":"Lambda: Serverless Functions","lead":"AWS Lambda runs your code only when something happens: a schedule, a file upload, an API call. You don't create or manage any servers, and you pay only for the milliseconds your code runs. That makes it a favourite DevOps tool for automation jobs.","sections":[{"h":"What problem Lambda solves","id":"s1","t":"When you think of S3 you think storage. Lambda belongs to two ideas at once: Compute: like EC2, it gives you a place to run code (Python, Node.js, Java…). Serverless: unlike EC2, you never pick, start, patch or stop a server. With EC2 you tell AWS the image, instance type (CPU and memory), network and security settings, and you get a virtual server that runs until you stop it. With Lambda you hand AWS just your code. When an event arrives, AWS creates the compute it needs, runs the code, scales out if many events come at once, and then removes it all again. What \"serverless\" means Serverless doesn't mean there are no servers. It means the servers aren't your problem. AWS provisions, scales, patches and tears them down, and you don't even see an IP address or subnet. You only care about your code and what triggers it. Think of it like…The checkout in a food-delivery app. A customer taps Pay, the payment code runs for a moment, the order is placed, and the customer is gone. On a serverless design, compute for that checkout exists only while the payment is being processed and disappears once it's done. On a server design, a checkout server sits running (and costing money) all night, whether anyone orders or not. Why DevOps teams run scheduled scripts on Lambda instead of a server that sits idle all day. Who decides: server or serverless? For the company's applications, the architects and developers decide. A food app can be built either way, and it depends on how the code is written. As a DevOps engineer you build the infrastructure they choose. Where you choose Lambda is in your own automation work."},{"h":"Event-driven: triggers","id":"s2","t":"Lambda functions are event-driven: they run in response to an event. The thing that delivers the event is the function's trigger. You can also run a function by hand with a test event, but a function with no trigger loses most of the benefit. Common triggers on the left; what a DevOps function typically does on the right. Schedule: an EventBridge rule (formerly \"CloudWatch Events\") like \"every day at 10:00\" works like a cron job in the cloud. Resource events: \"an EBS volume was created\", \"an EC2 instance changed state\", delivered through EventBridge. S3 events: \"a new object was uploaded to this bucket\". HTTP requests: through a function URL or API Gateway."},{"h":"How DevOps engineers use Lambda","id":"s3","t":"Cost optimization Find stale resources (an EBS volume nobody has used for 30 days, old snapshots, an idle instance holding an Elastic IP) and report or delete them. This is built step by step in Lesson 20. Security & compliance Enforce company rules automatically. Examples: \"all EBS volumes must be gp3, not gp2\" (gp3 is cheaper and faster), or \"no S3 bucket may be public\". The function checks daily and sends an SNS alert, or fixes the problem itself. Routine checks Daily audits, such as listing IAM users or roles that were given extra permissions, and sending a summary to the team. Why Lambda rather than EC2 for these? Say a cleanup script runs for 5 minutes every morning at 10:00. On EC2 you'd have to start a server, run the script and stop the server every day, or leave it running and pay for 23 hours 55 minutes of nothing. If you forget to stop it, you pay even more. With Lambda you set \"every day at 10:00\" as the trigger and AWS handles the rest. Try it: fire events at a Lambda function Click an event to trigger the function. The first call (or a call after a quiet spell) is a cold start: AWS has to set up a fresh environment. Calls that follow soon after reuse it (warm start). Billing counts only the run time. Waiting for an event… (no environment running, nothing being billed) Invocations: 0Billed time: 0 msCompute: 0 GB-s"},{"h":"Creating a function in the console","id":"s4","t":"Open Lambda → Create function. Choose one of: Author from scratch: you write the code (what DevOps engineers usually do). Use a blueprint: AWS samples, e.g. \"process an object uploaded to S3\". Container image: package your code as a Docker image stored in ECR. Runtime: the language. AWS provides managed runtimes for Python, Node.js, Java, .NET and Ruby. Other languages (e.g. Go, or even shell) run on an OS-only \"custom runtime\", which is more work. Python is the most popular choice for DevOps scripts. Execution role: by default Lambda creates an IAM role for the function that can only write logs. If your function needs to read EC2 or publish to SNS, add permissions to that role (or choose an existing role). As always, services get permissions through roles. See IAM. Advanced settings → Enable function URL (optional) gives the function its own HTTPS address. Auth type NONE means anyone can call it, which is fine for a hello-world demo. AWS_IAM restricts it to authorised callers. Without a URL, AWS services and triggers can still invoke the function. DevOps automation rarely needs a URL. The handler The new function comes with sample code: import json def lambda_handler(event, context): # event: the data from the trigger (the S3 object, the schedule, the HTTP request...) # context: info about this run (request id, time remaining...) return { 'statusCode': 200, 'body': json.dumps('Hello from Lambda!') } The handler is the function Lambda calls first, like main() in Java or C. The handler setting lambda_function.lambda_handler means \"file lambda_function.py, function lambda_handler\". You can write as many other functions as you like, but they run only if the handler calls them. You can rename the handler, but then you must also update it under Runtime settings. You can edit code in the browser, add files and folders, or write it in VS Code on your laptop and upload a .zip. Click Deploy to save, then Test with a test event to run it by hand. Open the function URL and you'll see \"Hello from Lambda!\". Useful configuration Setting What it's for Environment variables Change behaviour (thresholds, region, dry-run flags) without editing code. Timeout Max run time per call. Default 3 seconds, max 15 minutes. Raise it for longer scripts, but keep it tight because you pay for run time. Memory 128 MB up to 10 GB. CPU power grows with memory. Permissions The execution role (what the function may do) and resource policy (who may invoke it). Triggers / Destinations What starts the function, and where results go (e.g. SNS) after an asynchronous run. VPC Attach the function to your VPC to reach private resources such as a database."},{"h":"Pricing model","id":"s5","t":"You pay for two things, and nothing while the function is idle: Requests: about $0.20 per million invocations. Duration × memory, measured in GB-seconds: run time (billed per millisecond) × memory size. About $0.0000167 per GB-second on x86. The free tier includes 1 million requests and 400,000 GB-seconds per month, so most DevOps automation costs nothing. (Prices are approximate us-east-1 list prices; check the AWS pricing page for current numbers.) Try it: Lambda cost estimator Invocations / month Avg duration (ms) Memory Apply monthly free tier"},{"h":"Lambda vs EC2","id":"s6","t":"EC2 Lambda You choose AMI, instance type, network, storage Runtime, memory, timeout Runs Until you stop it Only when triggered, up to 15 min per call Scaling You configure (e.g. Auto Scaling groups) Automatic, per event Visibility Full control: IP, subnet, OS Hidden; AWS manages it Billing Per second while running, busy or idle Per request + per ms of run time Best for Long-running apps, full OS control, steady load Short, event-driven tasks; spiky or rare workloads Interview angle\"When would you use Lambda instead of EC2?\" Short, event-driven or scheduled tasks such as cost cleanups, compliance checks and reacting to S3 uploads, where paying for an idle server is wasteful. Mention the limits: max 15 minutes per run, no OS control, and cold starts."},{"h":"Check yourself","id":"s7","t":"A Python script must run for 4 minutes every night. What's the most cost-effective way to run it on AWS? Lambda with a schedule trigger runs only those 4 minutes and bills only for them. EC2 would bill all day. Your function times out after 3 seconds even though the code is correct. Why? The default timeout is 3 seconds. You can raise it up to 15 minutes. What does the handler setting lambda_function.lambda_handler mean? It's file.function, the entry point Lambda calls with (event, context)."},{"h":"Key takeaways","id":"s8","t":"Lambda is serverless compute: you give AWS code, and it creates, scales and removes the servers. Functions are event-driven: schedules, S3 uploads, resource events or HTTP requests trigger them. The handler (lambda_handler(event, context)) is the entry point. Permissions come from the function's execution role. Default timeout is 3 s (max 15 min). You pay per request + GB-seconds, with a generous free tier and nothing while idle. DevOps engineers use Lambda for cost optimization, compliance checks and routine automation."}],"n":19,"slug":"19-lambda"},{"title":"Cloud Cost Optimization Project","lead":"Forgotten AWS resources quietly add to the bill every month. In this project you build a Lambda function that finds stale EBS snapshots and deletes them automatically on a schedule. It's a real task DevOps engineers do, and a solid project to put on your résumé.","sections":[{"h":"","id":"","t":"This project builds on Lesson 19: Lambda (functions, handler, execution role, triggers). For reading the bill, Cost Explorer, budgets and tagging, see Lesson 36: Reducing Your AWS Bill."},{"h":"Why cost optimization matters","id":"s1","t":"Companies, especially startups and mid-size firms, move to the cloud for two main reasons: Less overhead. No data centre to build, no servers to buy, no team needed just to keep hardware running. Lower cost. Pay only for what you use instead of buying servers up front. The second promise only holds if the cloud is used efficiently. Anyone with the right IAM permissions can create resources in seconds, and people forget to delete them. If nobody watches, the bill goes up after the move to cloud, which is the opposite of what the company wanted. Keeping it under control is one of a DevOps engineer's core responsibilities."},{"h":"Stale resources","id":"s2","t":"A stale resource is something someone created, stopped using, and forgot to delete, while AWS keeps charging for it. Common examples: EBS volumes not attached to any instance. EBS snapshots of volumes that no longer exist. S3 buckets full of data nobody reads any more. An EKS cluster left running after a test. An old EC2 instance still holding an Elastic IP. Large accounts use hundreds of resource types across many teams. Checking them by hand isn't realistic, so you automate it. A typical story First, two quick definitions: An EBS volume is the virtual hard disk attached to an EC2 instance. A snapshot is a point-in-time backup copy of a volume. You can create a new volume from it later. Now the story. A developer launches an instance and stores important data on its volume, so every day they take a snapshot \"just in case\". Months later the feature is dropped. They terminate the instance, and its root volume is deleted with it. But the hundreds of snapshots stay behind, and AWS bills for their storage every month. When a DevOps engineer finds stale resources, there are two options: Notify Send an alert (\"you have 120 snapshots of a deleted volume, please clean up\") using an SNS topic, as covered in CloudWatch & SNS. Delete If your team has the permission and the policy allows it, remove them automatically. This project builds the delete option."},{"h":"The architecture","id":"s3","t":"A schedule triggers the function, and the function uses boto3 to talk to the EC2 API. boto3 is the official AWS library for Python. It's the code version of the AWS CLI: ec2.describe_snapshots() in boto3 is the same API call as aws ec2 describe-snapshots. Its documentation lists every call with the request and response format, which makes it easy to look up what you need. The rule: when is a snapshot stale? The cleanup rule used in this project. Every company tunes it differently, for example by adding an age limit. Try it: run the cleanup function This account has a few instances, volumes and snapshots. Run the function, then terminate an instance (which deletes its root volume) and run it again. Try the safety options too. Only delete snapshots older than 30 days Dry run (report only, delete nothing) Function output appears here."},{"h":"Build it step by step","id":"s4","t":"Create test resources. Launch a small Ubuntu instance (it gets a root volume automatically). Go to EC2 → Volumes, find its volume, then EC2 → Snapshots → Create snapshot from that volume. Create the function. Lambda → Create function → author from scratch, name cost-optimization-ebs-snapshot, runtime Python, default execution role. Add the code (below), click Deploy, then create a test event (any name; you're invoking it by hand) and click Test. Raise the timeout. The first test likely fails. The default timeout is 3 seconds, which is too short for API calls across the account. Configuration → General configuration → set about 10 seconds. Keep it as low as works, because run time is part of what you pay. Grant permissions. The next error is UnauthorizedOperation … DescribeSnapshots. The function's execution role can only write logs. Open Configuration → Permissions → the role, then Add permissions → Create inline policy (searching the managed policies for \"snapshot\" finds nothing useful). Allow exactly what the code needs: { \"Version\": \"2012-10-17\", \"Statement\": [{ \"Effect\": \"Allow\", \"Action\": [ \"ec2:DescribeSnapshots\", \"ec2:DescribeVolumes\", \"ec2:DeleteSnapshot\" ], \"Resource\": \"*\" }] } Granting AmazonEC2FullAccess would be quicker, but it breaks least privilege. If your version of the code also looks up instances, add ec2:DescribeInstances. A missing permission shows up as another Unauthorized error naming the exact action, so just add it. New permissions can take a moment to apply; wait and test again. Test: nothing should be deleted. The snapshot's volume still exists and is attached to a running instance. Make it stale. Terminate the instance; its root volume is deleted with it. Test again: the log says the snapshot was deleted because its volume no longer exists. Then try a second case: create a 1 GB volume, don't attach it, snapshot it, and test. The snapshot is deleted, but the volume stays, because this function only cleans snapshots."},{"h":"The code","id":"s5","t":"import os from datetime import datetime, timezone, timedelta import boto3 from botocore.exceptions import ClientError ec2 = boto3.client('ec2') DRY_RUN = os.environ.get('DRY_RUN', 'true').lower() == 'true' MIN_AGE = timedelta(days=int(os.environ.get('MIN_AGE_DAYS', '0'))) def delete(snapshot_id, reason): if DRY_RUN: print(f\"[dry run] would delete {snapshot_id}: {reason}\") return try: ec2.delete_snapshot(SnapshotId=snapshot_id) print(f\"Deleted {snapshot_id}: {reason}\") except ClientError as e: # e.g. InvalidSnapshot.InUse when an AMI still uses the snapshot print(f\"Could not delete {snapshot_id}: {e.response['Error']['Code']}\") def lambda_handler(event, context): # 1. Every EBS snapshot owned by this account (paginated for big accounts) paginator = ec2.get_paginator('describe_snapshots') for page in paginator.paginate(OwnerIds=['self']): for snapshot in page['Snapshots']: snapshot_id = snapshot['SnapshotId'] volume_id = snapshot.get('VolumeId') # Optional safety: leave recent snapshots alone if datetime.now(timezone.utc) - snapshot['StartTime'] < MIN_AGE: continue # 2. No source volume recorded -> stale if not volume_id: delete(snapshot_id, \"no source volume\") continue # 3. Is the source volume still there? try: volume = ec2.describe_volumes(VolumeIds=[volume_id])['Volumes'][0] except ClientError as e: if e.response['Error']['Code'] == 'InvalidVolume.NotFound': delete(snapshot_id, f\"volume {volume_id} no longer exists\") continue raise # 4. Volume exists but isn't attached to any instance -> stale if not volume['Attachments']: delete(snapshot_id, f\"volume {volume_id} is not attached to any instance\") How it works: boto3.client('ec2') creates a client that talks to the EC2 API. The function's role supplies the credentials, so there are no keys in the code. describe_snapshots(OwnerIds=['self']) returns JSON describing your snapshots. Without self you'd also get public snapshots shared by others. The code loops over it and reads SnapshotId and VolumeId. When a volume has been deleted, describe_volumes raises an InvalidVolume.NotFound error. The try/except turns that error into \"this snapshot is stale\". A volume's Attachments list is empty when it isn't connected to any instance. Environment variables control behaviour without code changes. It starts with DRY_RUN=true (only print). Set it to false once you trust the output. MIN_AGE_DAYS=30 adds the age buffer. Watch outDeleting a snapshot is permanent. Before running this in a real account: run with dry run first and read the log; agree the rules with the teams (some keep snapshots of deleted volumes on purpose as backups); add an age limit like \"older than 30 days\"; and consider skipping snapshots tagged e.g. keep=true. Snapshots used by an AMI can't be deleted until the AMI is deregistered. TipDeleting isn't the only option. Some organisations move old snapshots to the much cheaper EBS Snapshots Archive tier, or only notify owners through SNS. Pick what matches your company's policy."},{"h":"Run it automatically","id":"s6","t":"Lambda is event-driven, so the last step is a trigger. Create a schedule with Amazon EventBridge (in the console you'll find it under EventBridge → Schedules, or via CloudWatch → Events → Rules, which now opens EventBridge): Schedule type: recurring, e.g. rate(1 day) or the cron expression cron(0 10 * * ? *) (10:00 UTC daily). Target: AWS Lambda → your function. From then on the cleanup runs every day without anyone remembering to do it. Each run's output lands in the function's CloudWatch log group. Clean up after the labDelete the test instance, volumes, snapshots, and the schedule (or disable it). Leftover lab resources are exactly the kind of waste this project is meant to catch."},{"h":"Going further","id":"s7","t":"Real teams write one small function per resource type instead of one giant script: unattached EBS volumes, unused Elastic IPs, idle RDS instances, old S3 data, forgotten EKS clusters. They all follow the same pattern: list → filter by a rule → notify or delete → run on a schedule. Interview angleDescribe it like this: \"I built a cost-optimization Lambda in Python with boto3. EventBridge triggers it daily; it lists our EBS snapshots and deletes those whose source volume was deleted or is not attached to any instance, with an age buffer and dry-run mode. Its IAM role has only DescribeSnapshots, DescribeVolumes and DeleteSnapshot.\" Then mention a problem you hit, such as the 3-second default timeout or missing permissions."},{"h":"Check yourself","id":"s8","t":"An instance was terminated and its root volume deleted, but its daily snapshots remain. What happens on your bill? Snapshots are billed separately from volumes and stay until someone deletes them. That's exactly what makes them stale. The function fails with \"UnauthorizedOperation … ec2:DescribeVolumes\". What's the right fix? Grant exactly the missing action to the execution role (least privilege). Never embed access keys in Lambda code. What's the best way to make the cleanup run every day? A scheduled EventBridge rule is the event that triggers the function. (Lambda timeouts max out at 15 minutes anyway.)"},{"h":"Key takeaways","id":"s9","t":"The cloud only saves money if it's used efficiently. Finding stale resources is a core DevOps responsibility. Snapshots of deleted or unattached volumes are classic waste: AWS keeps billing until they're deleted. The pattern: EventBridge schedule → Lambda (Python + boto3) → describe → filter → delete or notify. Give the execution role only the actions it needs, and raise the 3-second default timeout. Be careful with deletes: dry run first, add an age buffer, respect tags and team policies, and clean up lab resources."}],"n":20,"slug":"20-cost-optimization"},{"title":"CloudFront: Content Delivery Network","lead":"Learn what a CDN is, why every big website uses one, and how to put CloudFront in front of a private S3 bucket so users worldwide get your content fast without ever touching the bucket directly.","sections":[{"h":"The problem: far away means slow","id":"s1","t":"Picture a photo-sharing app. Someone in Australia uploads a photo, and the app stores it in one central place, say a storage system in North Virginia (US). Now a friend in India opens the app to see it. Without any help, the friend's request has to cross the world and back: The request jumps through many routers on the internet. Each jump is called a hop. More hops and more distance mean more latency, which is the waiting time before data arrives. People near Virginia get photos instantly. People in India, Europe or South America see spinners and buffering. For apps like Instagram, YouTube or Amazon.com, that slow experience is not acceptable. The fix is a CDN."},{"h":"What is a CDN?","id":"s2","t":"A CDN (Content Delivery Network) is a worldwide network of servers that keep copies of your content close to your users. You use CDNs every day: every time a reel, product photo or video loads quickly, a CDN is probably serving it. Origin: the \"real\" home of your content. It could be an S3 bucket, a load balancer, API Gateway, or any web server. Edge location: a CDN data center in a city near your users (Mumbai, Frankfurt, Toronto…). AWS has hundreds of them, many more than it has regions. Cache: the stored copy at the edge. When the edge already has the file, it can answer immediately. Amazon CloudFront is AWS's managed CDN service. You tell it where your origin is, and it handles the worldwide edge network for you. Think of it like…A popular book with one copy in a central library. Instead of everyone travelling to that library, the publisher puts copies in every neighbourhood bookshop. The first reader in a neighbourhood may have to wait while a copy is shipped in, but everyone after that picks it up around the corner. Users talk to the nearest edge. The edge only goes back to the S3 origin when it doesn't have a fresh copy. Nobody can reach the bucket directly."},{"h":"How CloudFront serves a request","id":"s3","t":"A user opens your site. DNS sends them to the nearest edge location (not to your bucket). The edge checks its cache. If it has a fresh copy, that is a cache hit: it replies right away. If not, that is a cache miss: the edge fetches the file from the origin, sends it to the user, and keeps a copy. The next user near that edge (say, someone in Montreal after someone in Toronto) gets a fast cache hit. Caching and TTL A cached copy doesn't live forever. Each object has a TTL (Time To Live): how long the edge may keep serving it before checking the origin again. CloudFront's default is 24 hours, and you can change it in the cache settings or from the origin with Cache-Control headers. Long TTL = fewer trips to the origin, faster and cheaper, but users may see an old version for longer. Short TTL = changes appear sooner, but more requests go back to the origin. If you deploy a new version and can't wait for TTLs to expire, you can create an invalidation to tell every edge to drop its copy: aws cloudfront create-invalidation \\ --distribution-id E1ABCDEXAMPLE \\ --paths \"/index.html\" \"/css/*\" Using \"/*\" clears everything. A limited number of invalidation paths each month are free; beyond that, they cost a little. Try it: cache hit or miss? The origin is an S3 bucket in North Virginia. Pick where the user is, then request logo.png. Request it again to see the cache at work. Each city has its own edge cache. (Numbers are illustrative, not real measurements.) User location Pick a city and press \"Request logo.png\"."},{"h":"Why put CloudFront in front of S3?","id":"s4","t":"You can serve a website straight from an S3 bucket by making it public, but that isn't best practice. CloudFront fixes three things at once: Lower latency Content is served from the edge closest to each user, no matter which region the bucket lives in. Better security The bucket stays private. Users only ever talk to CloudFront, and you can add HTTPS and a web application firewall (AWS WAF) at the edge. Lower cost at scale Most requests are answered from cache, so far fewer requests and less data transfer hit S3. For a busy site, this usually costs less than serving everything from S3. A common setup: an e-commerce app runs on EC2 or Kubernetes, while its images, CSS and JavaScript sit in S3 behind CloudFront. A fully static website can live entirely in S3 + CloudFront. Need a refresher on buckets, bucket policies and \"Block Public Access\"? See Lesson 11: S3."},{"h":"Origin access: keeping the bucket private","id":"s5","t":"If the bucket blocks all public access, how does CloudFront read from it? You give CloudFront its own identity and allow only that identity in the bucket policy. Origin Access Control (OAC) is the current, recommended way. The bucket policy allows the CloudFront service, but only for your specific distribution. Origin Access Identity (OAI) is the older (\"legacy\") way. Think of it as a special virtual user, like a functional ID on a Linux server, that is allowed to read the bucket. You'll still see it in older setups and tutorials. When you choose OAC in the console, CloudFront gives you a policy to paste into the bucket. It looks like this: { \"Version\": \"2012-10-17\", \"Statement\": [{ \"Sid\": \"AllowCloudFrontServicePrincipal\", \"Effect\": \"Allow\", \"Principal\": { \"Service\": \"cloudfront.amazonaws.com\" }, \"Action\": \"s3:GetObject\", \"Resource\": \"arn:aws:s3:::www.example.com/*\", \"Condition\": { \"StringEquals\": { \"AWS:SourceArn\": \"arn:aws:cloudfront::111122223333:distribution/E1ABCDEXAMPLE\" } } }] } In plain words: \"Let CloudFront GetObject (read files) from this bucket, but only when the request comes from my distribution.\""},{"h":"Hands-on: S3 + CloudFront step by step","id":"s6","t":"Watch out: costCloudFront and WAF are not entirely free. If you practise this, delete the distribution and bucket when you're done. To avoid any cost, you can just read along, or stick to plain S3 static hosting. Create an S3 bucket. If you plan to use a custom domain, people often name it after the domain (e.g. www.example.com); any unique name works for this demo. Keep Block all public access turned on. Enabling versioning is optional but lets you recover deleted files. Upload your site files, e.g. index.html and a CSS file. If you open the bucket's own URL now, you get 403 Forbidden. That is what we want. Create a CloudFront distribution. For Origin domain, pick your bucket from the list. (Load balancers, API Gateway and custom servers can also be origins.) Origin access: choose Origin access control settings, create a new OAC, and apply the bucket policy CloudFront gives you (as above). Never choose \"Public\". Viewer settings: set Redirect HTTP to HTTPS. Leave the default cache policy for now. WAF: optional extra protection against common web attacks. It costs extra, so you can disable it for a demo. Price class: \"Use all edge locations\" if your users are worldwide, or a cheaper class (e.g. North America and Europe) if your customers are only there. Alternate domain name + SSL certificate (optional): to serve from www.example.com instead of the CloudFront URL, add the name and an HTTPS certificate from AWS Certificate Manager. CloudFront needs that certificate to be created in us-east-1. Default root object: enter index.html so visiting the bare URL shows your home page. Create and wait 5–10 minutes while it deploys to the edges. Then open the distribution domain, e.g. https://d111111abcdef8.cloudfront.net. Your site loads. The S3 URL still says 403. TipWith OAC you don't need S3's \"static website hosting\" feature: CloudFront reads the files through the normal S3 endpoint, and the default root object handles index.html. If you pick the S3 website endpoint as the origin instead, OAC can't be used and the bucket would have to be public. NoteTo point your own domain at the distribution, create a Route 53 alias record. DNS records are covered in Lesson 8: Route 53. Cleanup: a distribution must be disabled first, then deleted once it has finished disabling. Then empty and delete the bucket. Interview angle\"How would you serve static assets globally and securely?\" Good answer: private S3 bucket as origin, CloudFront in front with OAC, HTTPS redirect, a sensible TTL plus invalidations on deploy, optional WAF, and a price class that matches where the users are."},{"h":"Check yourself","id":"s7","t":"A user in Toronto requests an image that someone else in Toronto loaded five minutes ago (TTL is 24 hours). What happens? The nearby edge already holds a fresh copy, so it answers directly without contacting the origin. You want the S3 bucket to stay private but still be readable by CloudFront. What do you use? OAC gives CloudFront an identity, and the bucket policy allows only that distribution to read objects. The bucket stays blocked for everyone else. You deployed a new index.html, but users still see the old one. What is the quickest fix? An invalidation tells the edges to drop their cached copy so the next request fetches the new file from the origin."},{"h":"Key takeaways","id":"s8","t":"A CDN keeps copies of your content in edge locations near users, which cuts latency. CloudFront is AWS's CDN. The origin (S3, ALB, API Gateway, any web server) is where the real content lives. Cache hit = served from the edge. Cache miss = fetched from the origin once, then cached for its TTL. Keep the S3 bucket private and let CloudFront in with Origin Access Control (OAI is the legacy way). Use invalidations after deploys, choose a price class for your audience, and delete demo distributions to avoid charges."}],"n":21,"slug":"21-cloudfront"},{"title":"ECR: Container Registry","lead":"Containers need a place to live between \"built on my laptop\" and \"running in production\". This lesson gives a quick containers primer, explains what a container registry is, compares Amazon ECR with Docker Hub, and walks you through pushing your first image.","sections":[{"h":"Quick primer: containers and images","id":"s1","t":"Everything in this lesson (and the ECS and EKS lessons after it) is built on a few container words. Here they are in plain language: Container: a lightweight package that holds your application code plus everything it needs to run (runtime, libraries, settings). It runs the same way on your laptop, a test server or production. Image: the read-only template a container starts from. Think \"recipe + ingredients, frozen\". You build an image once and can run many containers from it. Dockerfile: a text file with the steps to build an image (start from a base image like python, copy the code, install packages, set the start command). Tag: a label on an image that marks its version, e.g. my-app:1.4.2 or my-app:latest. Container registry: a server that stores images so others can download (pull) them. You upload with push. Repository: one folder inside a registry that holds all versions (tags) of one image, e.g. demo-app-repo. Think of it like…A registry works like a photo-sharing app for images. You post a photo from your phone, and a friend on the other side of the world can see it. You push an image from your laptop, and a server anywhere in the world can pull it. Note\"Container\" doesn't have to mean \"Docker\". Docker made containers popular, but any tool that follows the OCI (Open Container Initiative) standard, such as Podman or Buildah, can build images that ECR stores. This lesson uses Docker commands because they are the most common."},{"h":"What does ECR stand for?","id":"s2","t":"E: Elastic As with EC2, EBS and EKS, the \"E\" means it scales and stays available. You can store as many images as you need and pay for what you use. AWS keeps the service up. C: Container It stores container images (and other OCI artifacts). R: Registry Like Docker Hub, Quay.io, Google's registry or GitHub Container Registry (GHCR), it's a place to push and pull images. Amazon ECR (Elastic Container Registry) is AWS's fully managed container registry. You don't run any servers. You create repositories and push images to them. Build and tag on your machine, push to an ECR repository, and AWS services pull the image to run it."},{"h":"ECR vs Docker Hub","id":"s3","t":"This is a classic interview question: \"Which registry do you use, and why not Docker Hub?\" Both store images. The difference is focus and integration. Docker Hub Amazon ECR Default repository type Public (anyone can pull) Private (only allowed identities can pull) Who can log in Separate Docker Hub accounts for every person Your existing IAM users and roles, no new accounts Access control Docker Hub teams/orgs IAM policies + repository policies Integration Works everywhere Built-in with ECS, EKS, Fargate, CodeBuild, Lambda Support and uptime Docker, Inc. AWS, under the same support plan as the rest of your account Best for Public images, personal projects Company/private images on AWS Picture a company with 10,000 engineers. With Docker Hub private repos, every person needs another account, and admins must manage access in a second place. With ECR, the IAM users, roles and policies you already have simply apply to images too. Interview angleSay you use your cloud provider's registry for private company images (ECR on AWS, Google's Artifact Registry on GCP), because it plugs into existing IAM and services. Docker Hub is great for public images. Quay.io is another popular private option."},{"h":"Creating a repository","id":"s4","t":"In the console, search for Elastic Container Registry and click Create repository. No sign-up is needed because you're already in your AWS account. Visibility: Private (default and recommended). Public ECR repos exist too. Repository name: e.g. demo-app-repo (lowercase). Tag immutability: when on, nobody can push a different image over an existing tag. v1.0 will always mean the same image, which is safer for production. Scan on push: ECR scans each new image for known security vulnerabilities (CVEs) and shows the results, so others can check an image before using it. The same thing from the AWS CLI: aws ecr create-repository \\ --repository-name demo-app-repo \\ --image-tag-mutability IMMUTABLE \\ --image-scanning-configuration scanOnPush=true \\ --region us-east-1 Watch outECR isn't free beyond the free tier. You pay for stored image data and data transfer. Delete demo repositories and images when you finish."},{"h":"Pushing your first image","id":"s5","t":"Open the repository and click View push commands. AWS shows the exact commands for macOS/Linux and for Windows (PowerShell). Here's what each one does. 0. Have the AWS CLI configured The login step uses the AWS CLI, so install it and run aws configure with your access key, secret key and default region. Full setup is in Lesson 12: AWS CLI. 1. Log Docker in to ECR aws ecr get-login-password --region us-east-1 \\ | docker login --username AWS --password-stdin 123456789012.dkr.ecr.us-east-1.amazonaws.com aws ecr get-login-password asks AWS for a temporary password (valid for 12 hours) using your IAM credentials. The pipe | passes that output as the input to the next command. docker login normally logs in to docker.io (Docker Hub). Giving it your registry address, <account-id>.dkr.ecr.<region>.amazonaws.com, points it at ECR instead. The username is always AWS. 2. Build the image docker build -t demo-app-repo . 3. Tag it with the repository address docker tag demo-app-repo:latest 123456789012.dkr.ecr.us-east-1.amazonaws.com/demo-app-repo:latest The tag tells Docker where the image belongs: registry/repository:tag. Without it, Docker wouldn't know to send the image to your ECR repo. 4. Push docker push 123456789012.dkr.ecr.us-east-1.amazonaws.com/demo-app-repo:latest Refresh the repository in the console and your image appears. Anyone with the right IAM permissions can now pull it from anywhere: docker pull 123456789012.dkr.ecr.us-east-1.amazonaws.com/demo-app-repo:latest Try it: ECR push command builder Fill in your details to generate the exact commands. Account ID Region Repository name Tag"},{"h":"Permissions: when docker push fails","id":"s6","t":"If you use the root account in a demo, everything just works. In real life you use an IAM user or role, and it needs ECR permissions. Typical errors are \"no basic auth credentials\" (you didn't log in, or the 12-hour token expired) or \"not authorized to perform ecr:…\" (missing permissions). Go to IAM → your user/role → Add permissions → Attach policies directly and search \"ECR\". AWS managed policies such as AmazonEC2ContainerRegistryReadOnly (pull only) or AmazonEC2ContainerRegistryPowerUser (push and pull) cover most needs. Follow least privilege: CI pipelines that build images need push. Servers that only run images need pull. TipThe CI/CD project in Lesson 16 pushes to Docker Hub. You can swap it for ECR: give the CodeBuild role ECR push permissions and replace the login/push steps with the commands above."},{"h":"Check yourself","id":"s7","t":"What is the main reason companies on AWS prefer ECR over Docker Hub for internal images? Docker Hub does offer private repos, but ECR plugs into the IAM setup you already have and integrates tightly with ECS, EKS and other AWS services. In aws ecr get-login-password | docker login …, what does the pipe do? The pipe sends the first command's output (the token) into the second command, which reads it with --password-stdin. Which repository setting stops someone from overwriting v1.0 with a different image? With tag immutability on, a pushed tag can't be replaced, so a version label always points to the same image."},{"h":"Key takeaways","id":"s8","t":"An image is the template, a container is a running copy, and a registry stores images in repositories by tag. ECR = Elastic Container Registry: AWS's managed, scalable, private-by-default registry. Pick ECR for company images on AWS (IAM integration, ECS/EKS integration, AWS support). Docker Hub suits public images. The push flow: get-login-password | docker login → build → tag → push. Turn on tag immutability and scan on push. Grant least-privilege ECR permissions and delete demo repos."}],"n":22,"slug":"22-ecr"},{"title":"ECS: Running Containers","lead":"Running one container is easy. Keeping hundreds of them healthy, scaled and reachable is the hard part. This lesson explains why container orchestration exists, how Amazon ECS works (clusters, task definitions, tasks, services), Fargate vs EC2, and the popular interview question: ECS vs EKS vs Kubernetes.","sections":[{"h":"","id":"","t":"New to containers and images? Start with the quick primer in Lesson 22: ECR. This lesson also pulls its image from ECR."},{"h":"Why isn't Docker alone enough?","id":"s1","t":"Say you install Docker on a virtual machine and run your app as container c1. It works, but in production you quickly hit three problems: 1. No auto-healing Containers are ephemeral (short-lived): they can die from a crash, running out of memory, a bad deploy, or someone deleting them by mistake. Plain Docker won't bring them back, so users see downtime. 2. No auto-scaling On a normal day you get 100 requests. During a festival sale you get 10,000. One container can't cope, and plain Docker won't add more copies for you. 3. Changing addresses Even if you restart a container by hand, it usually comes back with a new IP address (e.g. 172.16.3.4 becomes 172.16.3.9). Anything calling the old IP breaks. A container orchestrator solves these. It watches your containers and restarts failed ones (auto-healing), adds or removes copies with demand (auto-scaling), and gives a stable address in front of changing containers (service discovery and load balancing). Orchestration is what took containers from \"works in dev\" to \"safe in production\". Think of it like…A restaurant manager. Cooks (containers) may go home sick or get overwhelmed at dinner rush. The manager notices, calls in replacements, adds extra cooks at peak time, and customers always order at the same counter no matter which cook is working."},{"h":"The orchestration landscape","id":"s2","t":"Kubernetes is the open-source leader. Many companies sell their own \"distribution\" of it, just as Ubuntu and Fedora are distributions of Linux: Red Hat OpenShift (enterprise support and security), Rancher, and managed cloud versions like AWS EKS, Azure AKS and Google GKE. Non-Kubernetes orchestrators also exist: Docker Swarm, Apache Mesos, and AWS's own ECS. AWS built ECS (Elastic Container Service) to be simpler than Kubernetes. It doesn't use Kubernetes concepts like pods or deployments. It has its own: clusters, task definitions, tasks and services. It's also proprietary: it only runs on AWS."},{"h":"How ECS is organised","id":"s3","t":"A task definition is the blueprint; tasks are running copies; a service keeps the right number of tasks alive and connects them to a load balancer. ECS term What it is Rough Kubernetes cousin Cluster A logical group where your containers run, backed by Fargate and/or EC2 capacity. Cluster Task definition A JSON blueprint: which image, how much CPU/memory, which ports, environment variables, IAM roles, log settings. Versioned as \"revisions\". Pod spec Task A running instance of a task definition: one or more containers actually running. Pod Service Keeps a desired count of tasks running, replaces failed ones, can auto-scale and register tasks with a load balancer. Deployment + Service Kubernetes objects (pods, deployments, services, ingress) are explained in Lesson 24: EKS. Tip\"Run task\" starts a one-off task. If it dies, nothing replaces it. For a real app, create a service. That's where auto-healing and scaling come from."},{"h":"Fargate vs EC2 launch type","id":"s4","t":"When you create a cluster or task, you choose where the containers physically run: Fargate (serverless) You only say \"this task needs 0.5 vCPU and 1 GB\". AWS finds the compute. There are no servers to patch, scale or monitor. Like Lambda, but built for long-running containers. You pay per task for the CPU/memory it uses while running. Most teams start here. EC2 (servers you manage) ECS places tasks on EC2 instances in your cluster. You choose instance types, configure an Auto Scaling group, patch the OS and watch server health. In return you get more control: special instance types, GPUs, custom AMIs, and possibly lower cost at steady high usage. Try it: who manages what? Pick a way of running containers on AWS and see which jobs land on AWS and which land on you."},{"h":"ECS vs EKS vs Kubernetes: the interview question","id":"s5","t":"On AWS you have three main ways to orchestrate containers: ECS, EKS (managed Kubernetes), or Kubernetes you install yourself on EC2 (with tools like kubeadm or kops). Where ECS shines Simple: no control plane with many components to learn and run. Create a cluster, a task definition and a service, and you're live. Cluster creation with Fargate takes about a minute. Fully managed, and with Fargate there are no servers at all. Cost-effective: no Kubernetes system workloads eating resources, and no control-plane fee. Deep AWS integration: IAM roles per task, CloudWatch logs and metrics built in, load balancers, ECR. Where ECS falls short Lock-in: ECS task definitions and services only work on AWS. Move to Azure, and you rebuild from scratch. Kubernetes manifests (pods, services, deployments, ingress) carry over to any cloud with mostly small changes. In a multi-cloud or hybrid world, that matters. (Lock-in is good for AWS, not for you.) Smaller ecosystem: Kubernetes is open source with a huge community adding features constantly. It can be extended with custom resources (CRDs) and controllers, which is how tools like Argo CD, Flux and Istio work. None of those run on ECS. Fewer advanced options: Kubernetes has hundreds of ingress controllers and many security and secret-management add-ons. ECS mainly relies on AWS's load balancers and services. ECS EKS DIY Kubernetes on EC2 Control plane AWS (invisible) AWS-managed Kubernetes You Learning curve Low High (Kubernetes) Highest Portable to other clouds No Mostly Yes Ecosystem/extensions AWS only Full Kubernetes Full Kubernetes Good fit Startups, simple apps, AWS-only teams Most companies running Kubernetes on AWS Special needs, full control Interview angleKnow ECS, but lead with EKS/Kubernetes skills unless the job description says ECS: far more jobs ask for Kubernetes. A crisp answer: \"ECS is simpler and tightly integrated with AWS, great for small teams. But it's AWS-only and lacks Kubernetes' ecosystem, so most organisations pick EKS for portability and extensibility.\""},{"h":"Hands-on: deploy a Flask app on ECS Fargate","id":"s6","t":"Watch out: costFargate tasks, public IPs and log storage are billed while running. Stop tasks and delete the cluster as soon as you finish. Create a cluster. ECS → Clusters → Create. Name it demo-ecs-cluster, keep the default VPC and subnets, and choose AWS Fargate as infrastructure. (Choosing EC2 would also ask you for instance types and an Auto Scaling group.) CloudWatch monitoring is on by default. Prepare the image. The sample is a small Python Flask app listening on port 3000. Build it and push it to a private ECR repository, using the login/build/tag/push flow from Lesson 22. A handy shortcut builds and tags in one go: aws ecr get-login-password --region us-east-1 \\ | docker login --username AWS --password-stdin 123456789012.dkr.ecr.us-east-1.amazonaws.com docker build -t 123456789012.dkr.ecr.us-east-1.amazonaws.com/demo-app-repo:latest . docker push 123456789012.dkr.ecr.us-east-1.amazonaws.com/demo-app-repo:latest Create a task definition. Task definitions → Create new. Name it, keep launch type Fargate, OS Linux, and choose the smallest CPU/memory that fits. Pick the two IAM roles (see below). Let the console create a new task execution role so logs can go to CloudWatch. Leave the task role empty unless your code calls AWS services. Container details: name example, image URI …/demo-app-repo:latest, container port 3000 (HTTP). Optionally set memory soft/hard limits, similar to Kubernetes resource requests/limits. Keep \"Use log collection\" on with CloudWatch. Run it. Open the task definition → Deploy → Run task → choose your cluster → Create. The task goes from Provisioning to Running. Check logs. Open the task → Logs tab. These come from CloudWatch, and you'll see Flask reporting it's running on port 3000. To open the app in a browser, the task needs a public IP, and its security group must allow inbound port 3000 (see Lesson 7). Task role vs task execution role These two names confuse almost everyone, and interviewers know it. Task execution role Used by ECS itself (the agent) to start your task: pull the image from ECR, send logs to CloudWatch, read secrets for environment variables. Task role Used by your application code inside the container to call AWS APIs, e.g. read from S3 or write to DynamoDB. Optional: only if your app talks to AWS. TipGood habit to mention in interviews: \"I always send ECS container logs to CloudWatch. When something breaks, CloudWatch is my first stop.\" Logging and alarms are covered in Lesson 18."},{"h":"Check yourself","id":"s7","t":"Your ECS container crashes at 2 a.m. Which setup automatically starts a replacement? A service constantly compares running tasks with the desired count and replaces any that stop. A one-off task or plain Docker won't. Your container needs to upload files to S3. Which IAM role gets the S3 permission? The task role is for your application code's AWS calls. The execution role is for ECS itself: pulling images and shipping logs. Your company may move to another cloud next year. Why might that favour EKS over ECS? ECS is proprietary to AWS. Kubernetes resources carry over to AKS, GKE or self-managed clusters with mostly small changes."},{"h":"Key takeaways","id":"s8","t":"Orchestrators add what plain Docker lacks: auto-healing, auto-scaling and stable networking. ECS building blocks: cluster → task definition (blueprint) → task (running) → service (keeps N tasks alive, LB). Fargate = serverless, nothing to manage. EC2 = more control, but you manage the servers. ECS is simple, cheap and AWS-integrated, but proprietary with a smaller ecosystem. Most organisations choose EKS. Execution role = ECS pulls images and writes logs. Task role = your app calls AWS."}],"n":23,"slug":"23-ecs"},{"title":"Project: Kubernetes on EKS","lead":"Kubernetes is the most requested skill in DevOps job posts. In this project you'll learn what EKS manages for you, the core Kubernetes objects (pods, deployments, services, ingress), and then deploy the 2048 game on an EKS cluster with Fargate, exposed to the internet through an Application Load Balancer created by the AWS Load Balancer Controller.","sections":[{"h":"","id":"","t":"Why orchestrate containers at all, and how ECS compares with EKS, is covered in Lesson 23: ECS. Container images and ECR are in Lesson 22."},{"h":"Kubernetes in two halves","id":"s1","t":"Every Kubernetes cluster has two parts: Control plane (the \"master nodes\", the brain). Its parts: the API server (the front door, which every kubectl command talks to), etcd (the database holding cluster state), the scheduler (decides which node runs each pod), the controller manager (keeps reality matching what you asked for), and the cloud controller manager (talks to the cloud provider). Data plane (the \"worker nodes\", the muscle). This is where your apps actually run. Each node runs kubelet (starts and watches pods), kube-proxy (networking rules), a container runtime, plus a network plugin (CNI) and cluster DNS. For high availability, production clusters usually run three control-plane nodes. The pain of doing it yourself On plain EC2 you'd launch, say, six instances (three masters, three workers), install all the control-plane parts with kubeadm, install a CNI, container runtime, DNS and kube-proxy on the workers, then join them to the cluster. Tools like kops automate the setup, but day-2 problems are still yours: A master node dies, or the API server becomes slow or unresponsive. etcd crashes, and you need backups and restores. Certificates expire and must be renewed and re-attached. Now multiply that by tens or hundreds of clusters."},{"h":"What EKS does for you","id":"s2","t":"Amazon EKS (Elastic Kubernetes Service) is a managed control plane. You ask for a cluster, and AWS runs a highly available control plane you never see: no master nodes to patch, no etcd to babysit, no certificates to renew. It's backed by an AWS service level agreement (SLA). EKS manages the control plane, not fully the data plane. For worker capacity you choose: EC2 node groups Your own instances, joined to the cluster. Use these when you need a specific OS (e.g. RHEL), instance type or configuration. You handle auto scaling, health and patching (managed node groups help). Fargate AWS's serverless compute for containers. Each pod runs on capacity AWS provides, so there are no nodes to manage. Combined with EKS, AWS runs both the control plane and the compute. EKS runs the control plane for you. Your workloads run in private subnets on Fargate or EC2; the load balancer sits in a public subnet. Interview angle\"Why EKS instead of self-managed Kubernetes?\" Because the control plane is the hardest part to operate: etcd, API server health, upgrades, certificates. EKS makes that AWS's job (with an SLA), so the team focuses on applications. Ways to run Kubernetes on AWS: DIY on EC2 (kubeadm/kops), or EKS. On-premises you'd install it on your own data-centre servers."},{"h":"Core Kubernetes objects you'll use","id":"s3","t":"Object What it does Pod The smallest deployable unit: one (or a few tightly coupled) containers with their resources and settings, described in YAML. Deployment Manages a set of identical pods: keeps replicas: 5 running, replaces failed ones, rolls out new versions. Service A stable name/IP in front of changing pods. It finds pods using labels and selectors. Ingress Rules for HTTP traffic from outside: \"requests for example.com/abc go to service X\". Ingress controller The program that reads Ingress rules and configures a real load balancer to follow them. Namespace A folder-like boundary to group resources, e.g. game-2048, kube-system. Service account An identity for pods. On EKS it can be linked to an IAM role. Getting traffic in: the three Service types A pod on its own only has a cluster-internal IP. Users outside can't reach it. A Service offers three modes: Type Who can reach the app Catch ClusterIP (default) Only things inside the cluster Not reachable from outside NodePort Anyone who can reach a node's IP on a high port Nodes sit in private subnets, so only things inside the VPC can reach them LoadBalancer The internet, via a cloud load balancer One load balancer per service. With hundreds of microservices, that's a big bill. The better pattern is Ingress: keep services as ClusterIP/NodePort, write an Ingress per app, and let one ingress controller manage the load balancer(s) for all of them. Ingress vs ingress controller An Ingress resource by itself does nothing. It's just rules written down. Someone has to read them and act. That's the ingress controller: NGINX, F5 and many others each have one, usually installed with a Helm chart. On AWS we use the AWS Load Balancer Controller: it watches for Ingress resources and creates and configures an Application Load Balancer (listeners, target groups, rules, security groups) to match them. Think of it like…The Ingress is a written seating plan for a wedding. The ingress controller is the usher who reads the plan and actually walks guests to their tables. A plan with no usher seats nobody. NoteIf you run several controllers (say NGINX and ALB) in one cluster, the ingressClassName field says which one should handle each Ingress. What an Application Load Balancer is, and how it differs from NLB, is explained in Lesson 28: Load Balancers. Try it: follow a request into the cluster Step through how a player's browser reaches a 2048 pod. Then switch off the ingress controller and try again. Ingress controller installed"},{"h":"IAM for pods: OIDC and service accounts","id":"s4","t":"On EC2, when an app needs S3 access you attach an IAM role to the instance. Pods need the same, at the pod level. The ALB controller, for instance, is just a pod that must call AWS APIs to create load balancers. Every EKS cluster has an OpenID Connect (OIDC) provider URL. OIDC is a standard way to trust identities from another system, like \"Log in with Google\" on a website. You associate that OIDC provider with IAM, so IAM trusts identities issued by your cluster. Then you link an IAM role to a Kubernetes service account. Any pod using that service account gets the role's permissions. This is called IRSA (IAM Roles for Service Accounts). In real teams, developers say \"my service needs RDS access\", and the DevOps engineer creates a service account bound to a role with exactly those permissions."},{"h":"Fargate profiles","id":"s5","t":"With Fargate there are no nodes, so how does EKS know which pods to run on Fargate? A Fargate profile lists namespaces (and optionally labels). Pods created in those namespaces run on Fargate. A new cluster made with --fargate gets a profile for default and kube-system. To deploy into another namespace, you add a profile for it. Pods in a namespace with no profile stay Pending."},{"h":"Hands-on: deploy the 2048 game","id":"s6","t":"Watch out: costEKS charges per cluster per hour, and the ALB, NAT gateway and Fargate pods cost extra. Run the cleanup step as soon as you finish. The demo uses a root account for simplicity. At work, use an IAM user/role with the needed permissions. Prerequisites kubectl: the Kubernetes command line (install from the Kubernetes docs). eksctl: a command-line tool for creating and managing EKS clusters (on macOS: brew install eksctl). AWS CLI, configured with aws configure (see Lesson 12). helm: the Kubernetes package manager, used to install the controller. 1. Create the cluster eksctl create cluster --name demo-cluster --region us-east-1 --fargate You could click through the console, but it asks for IAM roles, VPC, subnets and more. In companies, eksctl (or IaC tools) is preferred. Behind the scenes it uses CloudFormation to create a new VPC with public and private subnets, the control plane and a Fargate profile. Expect 10–20 minutes. When it's done, the console shows the cluster. The Resources tab lets you browse pods, deployments and more without kubectl. The Overview shows the API server endpoint and the OIDC provider URL. 2. Point kubectl at the cluster aws eks update-kubeconfig --name demo-cluster --region us-east-1 This writes the cluster's connection details into your ~/.kube/config. 3. Add a Fargate profile for the app's namespace eksctl create fargateprofile \\ --cluster demo-cluster --region us-east-1 \\ --name alb-sample-app --namespace game-2048 4. Deploy the app: namespace, deployment, service, ingress kubectl apply -f https://raw.githubusercontent.com/kubernetes-sigs/aws-load-balancer-controller/v2.5.4/docs/examples/2048/2048_full.yaml This one file (from the official AWS example) creates the game-2048 namespace, a Deployment with 5 replicas, a Service and an Ingress. The key parts: # Service: finds pods by label, forwards port 80 to the container's port 80 apiVersion: v1 kind: Service metadata: namespace: game-2048 name: service-2048 spec: type: NodePort selector: app.kubernetes.io/name: app-2048 # must match the pod labels ports: - port: 80 targetPort: 80 # the container port protocol: TCP --- # Ingress: \"send all HTTP traffic to service-2048\" apiVersion: networking.k8s.io/v1 kind: Ingress metadata: namespace: game-2048 name: ingress-2048 annotations: alb.ingress.kubernetes.io/scheme: internet-facing alb.ingress.kubernetes.io/target-type: ip spec: ingressClassName: alb # handled by the AWS LB Controller rules: - http: paths: - path: / pathType: Prefix backend: service: name: service-2048 port: number: 80 Check what got created: kubectl get pods -n game-2048 # 5 pods, Pending → Running kubectl get svc -n game-2048 # NodePort with a cluster IP, no external IP kubectl get ingress -n game-2048 # class \"alb\", but ADDRESS is empty! The empty ADDRESS proves the point: there's no ingress controller yet, so nothing has acted on the Ingress. Tiptarget-type: ip tells the ALB to send traffic straight to pod IPs. It's required on Fargate, because there are no EC2 nodes to send NodePort traffic to. 5. Connect IAM to the cluster (OIDC) eksctl utils associate-iam-oidc-provider --cluster demo-cluster --approve This is the same as Authentication → Associate identity provider in the console. Without it, the next steps fail. 6. Create the IAM policy and service account for the controller curl -O https://raw.githubusercontent.com/kubernetes-sigs/aws-load-balancer-controller/v2.5.4/docs/install/iam_policy.json aws iam create-policy \\ --policy-name AWSLoadBalancerControllerIAMPolicy \\ --policy-document file://iam_policy.json eksctl create iamserviceaccount \\ --cluster=demo-cluster \\ --namespace=kube-system \\ --name=aws-load-balancer-controller \\ --role-name AmazonEKSLoadBalancerControllerRole \\ --attach-policy-arn=arn:aws:iam::<YOUR_ACCOUNT_ID>:policy/AWSLoadBalancerControllerIAMPolicy \\ --approve The policy JSON comes from the controller's official docs, so you don't write it yourself. The last command creates an IAM role and a Kubernetes service account linked to it (IRSA). Replace the account ID with yours. 7. Install the AWS Load Balancer Controller with Helm helm repo add eks https://aws.github.io/eks-charts helm repo update eks helm install aws-load-balancer-controller eks/aws-load-balancer-controller \\ -n kube-system \\ --set clusterName=demo-cluster \\ --set serviceAccount.create=false \\ --set serviceAccount.name=aws-load-balancer-controller \\ --set region=us-east-1 \\ --set vpcId=<YOUR_VPC_ID> Find the VPC ID on the cluster's Networking tab. serviceAccount.create=false tells Helm to reuse the IAM-linked service account from step 6. Then verify: kubectl get deployment -n kube-system aws-load-balancer-controller # wait until READY shows 2/2 (two replicas for availability) 8. Play kubectl get ingress -n game-2048 # ADDRESS: k8s-game2048-ingress2-xxxxxxxx.us-east-1.elb.amazonaws.com The controller saw the Ingress and created an ALB named like k8s-game2048-… (see EC2 → Load Balancers). Wait until its state is Active (2–4 minutes), then open the address with http:// in your browser. The 2048 game appears. Troubleshooting: controller stuck at 0/2 ready Run kubectl describe deploy aws-load-balancer-controller -n kube-system (or look at the status in kubectl edit deploy …). A common error is \"error looking up service account kube-system/aws-load-balancer-controller\". That means step 6 didn't really create the service account, often because a leftover CloudFormation stack from an earlier attempt exists. Delete the old eksctl-…-iamserviceaccount-… stack in CloudFormation, then rerun step 6 (adding --override-existing-serviceaccounts if needed). If Helm says \"cannot reuse a name that is still in use\", run helm uninstall aws-load-balancer-controller -n kube-system and install again. If helm install complains about unexpected arguments, check for stray spaces after the \\ line breaks. 9. Clean up kubectl delete -f https://raw.githubusercontent.com/kubernetes-sigs/aws-load-balancer-controller/v2.5.4/docs/examples/2048/2048_full.yaml eksctl delete cluster --name demo-cluster --region us-east-1 Delete the app first so the controller removes the ALB it created. Otherwise the leftover ALB can block VPC deletion and keep costing money."},{"h":"What you just built","id":"s7","t":"This is how it works in organisations: for each microservice, the DevOps engineer writes deployment.yaml, service.yaml and ingress.yaml. Installing the ingress controller is a one-time job per cluster, and from then on it creates and updates load balancers automatically. On EKS the only extra wiring is IAM (OIDC + service account). On a self-managed cluster you'd give the controller permissions through Kubernetes RBAC instead."},{"h":"Check yourself","id":"s8","t":"You applied an Ingress, but kubectl get ingress shows no ADDRESS. What's the most likely cause? An Ingress is just rules. Without a controller (like the AWS Load Balancer Controller), no load balancer gets created, so there's no address. What does EKS manage for you? EKS is a managed, highly available control plane. Worker capacity comes from EC2 node groups or Fargate, and your app manifests are still yours. Why do we associate an IAM OIDC provider before installing the ALB controller? OIDC lets IAM trust the cluster's service accounts (IRSA). The controller needs AWS permissions to create load balancers and target groups."},{"h":"Key takeaways","id":"s9","t":"EKS = AWS runs the Kubernetes control plane. You choose EC2 node groups or Fargate for the data plane. Pod → Deployment (replicas) → Service (stable address via labels/selectors) → Ingress (HTTP routing rules). An Ingress needs an ingress controller. On AWS, the AWS Load Balancer Controller turns Ingress rules into an ALB. Pods get AWS permissions through IAM OIDC + service accounts (IRSA). Fargate needs a Fargate profile per namespace. Tooling: eksctl to create clusters, aws eks update-kubeconfig, kubectl, and helm. Always delete demo clusters."}],"n":24,"slug":"24-eks"},{"title":"Secrets Management","lead":"\"How do you handle secrets?\" comes up in nearly every DevOps interview. This lesson explains why secrets need special care, the three common options on AWS (Parameter Store, Secrets Manager and HashiCorp Vault), and how to choose between them with a confident, cost-aware answer.","sections":[{"h":"What counts as a secret?","id":"s1","t":"A secret is any piece of information that would let someone do damage if it leaked. As a DevOps engineer you handle them all the time: CI/CD: container registry username, password and URL, used to push images. Leaked: someone deletes your images or pushes a malicious one. Databases: DB usernames and passwords. Leaked: someone reads or deletes your data. Automation tools: Ansible or Terraform need AWS credentials. Leaked: someone controls your cloud account. Apps: third-party API tokens, certificates and private keys. Secrets management means storing these values in one protected place, encrypting them, controlling exactly who and what can read them, auditing access, and changing them regularly. That way they never need to sit in code, Git repos, or plain-text config files. A single leak can hurt the whole organisation, which is why this is a core DevOps responsibility. Watch outThe classic mistake is hard-coding a password in a script or committing a .env file to Git. Even if you delete it later, it stays in the Git history. Treat any leaked secret as compromised and change it."},{"h":"The three options","id":"s2","t":"SSM Parameter Store Part of AWS Systems Manager. A simple, hierarchical key-value store for configuration and secrets. Values can be encrypted with KMS. Standard parameters are free. AWS Secrets Manager A dedicated AWS secrets service. Its headline feature is automatic rotation. Costs more, per secret per month. HashiCorp Vault Not an AWS service. A specialised, cloud-agnostic secrets platform you install and run yourself (or buy as HashiCorp's managed cloud service). Works across AWS, Azure, GCP and on-premises."},{"h":"Systems Manager Parameter Store","id":"s3","t":"Parameter Store holds values under path-like names such as /myapp/prod/docker/username. Each parameter has a type: String: plain text, e.g. a registry URL. StringList: comma-separated values. SecureString: encrypted with an AWS KMS key. Use this for anything sensitive. # store values aws ssm put-parameter --name /myapp/docker/registry-url --type String \\ --value \"docker.io\" aws ssm put-parameter --name /myapp/docker/username --type String \\ --value \"myuser\" # read a SecureString (decrypted) aws ssm get-parameter --name /myapp/db/password --with-decryption \\ --query Parameter.Value --output text Access is controlled by IAM: give the service that needs the value (CodeBuild, EC2, Lambda…) an IAM role allowed to read those parameter paths. In Lesson 16, CodeBuild reads Docker credentials from Parameter Store exactly this way. It's easy to use and easy to integrate."},{"h":"AWS Secrets Manager","id":"s4","t":"If Parameter Store can hold encrypted values, why does Secrets Manager exist? Mainly for rotation. What is rotation? Rotation means automatically replacing a secret with a new one on a schedule, say every 90 days. Even if an old password or certificate leaks, it stops working soon after, so the window of risk shrinks. Think of it like…A hotel that re-codes every room key card at checkout. If a guest keeps their card, it's useless the next day. Rotation does the same for passwords. Built-in rotation for supported databases such as Amazon RDS, Aurora and Redshift: Secrets Manager changes the password in the database and in the secret together. Custom rotation for anything else (API tokens, other systems): you supply a Lambda function with your own rotation logic, and Secrets Manager runs it on schedule. Apps always fetch the current value at runtime, so they keep working after rotation. Extra features: cross-region replication of secrets, resource policies, and fine-grained auditing through CloudTrail. # create a secret (JSON is common for DB credentials) aws secretsmanager create-secret --name prod/db/credentials \\ --secret-string '{\"username\":\"admin\",\"password\":\"S3cure!Pass\"}' # read it aws secretsmanager get-secret-value --secret-id prod/db/credentials \\ --query SecretString --output text # rotate every 90 days using a rotation Lambda aws secretsmanager rotate-secret --secret-id prod/db/credentials \\ --rotation-lambda-arn arn:aws:lambda:us-east-1:123456789012:function:my-rotator \\ --rotation-rules AutomaticallyAfterDays=90 The trade-off is price. Secrets Manager charges per secret per month (around $0.40) plus API calls. Standard Parameter Store parameters cost nothing to store. A few secrets cost very little either way, but organisations have thousands. Check the AWS pricing page for current numbers."},{"h":"The smart combo: use both","id":"s5","t":"You don't have to pick one. A cost-aware strategy is to sort secrets by how sensitive they are: Important but not highly sensitive (useless on their own): usernames, registry URLs, config. Use Parameter Store. Highly sensitive or needs rotation: passwords, DB credentials, API tokens. Use Secrets Manager. Example: a CodePipeline/CodeBuild job pushes images to a container registry. It needs a username, password and registry URL. A leaked username alone can't do anything without the password. One job, two stores: low-sensitivity values in Parameter Store, the password in Secrets Manager. The job's IAM role is allowed to read both. In a CodeBuild buildspec.yml, both stores plug straight into environment variables: version: 0.2 env: parameter-store: REGISTRY_URL: /ci/registry-url DOCKER_USERNAME: /ci/username secrets-manager: DOCKER_PASSWORD: ci/registry:password # secret-id:json-key phases: build: commands: - echo \"$DOCKER_PASSWORD\" | docker login -u \"$DOCKER_USERNAME\" --password-stdin \"$REGISTRY_URL\" The CodeBuild service role needs ssm:GetParameters for the parameters and secretsmanager:GetSecretValue for the secret (plus kms:Decrypt if you use a customer-managed key)."},{"h":"Why would anyone use HashiCorp Vault?","id":"s6","t":"If AWS offers two services, why add a third-party tool? Because AWS-managed services tie you to AWS. Multi-cloud and hybrid: if your company runs on AWS and Azure, or on-prem too, or might migrate later, secrets locked in AWS services become a migration headache. Vault is one central store that works everywhere. Specialist features: Vault has been a dedicated secrets tool for years. It offers things like dynamic secrets (it creates short-lived DB credentials on demand), encryption-as-a-service and many authentication methods. Large community: it grew up as an open-source project with broad community use and integrations. (HashiCorp moved Vault to a source-available licence in 2023, and the community fork OpenBao stays fully open source.) The cost: if you self-host it, you install, secure, back up, upgrade and keep it highly available. That's real operational work. Try it: where should this secret go? Pick an example, or answer the questions yourself, to get a recommendation. How bad if it leaks? Should it change automatically on a schedule? Where do the apps using it run?"},{"h":"Quick comparison","id":"s7","t":"Parameter Store Secrets Manager HashiCorp Vault Who runs it AWS AWS You (or HashiCorp's cloud service) Encryption SecureString via KMS Always, via KMS Yes, own encryption Automatic rotation No Yes (built-in for RDS etc., Lambda for custom) Yes, plus dynamic short-lived secrets Cost Standard tier free Per secret + per API call Your servers + ops time (or licence) Works outside AWS No No Yes Best for Config and low-sensitivity values Passwords, DB creds, tokens that need rotation Multi-cloud / hybrid organisations Interview angleDon't go blank. A strong answer: \"We use a combination: Parameter Store for non-sensitive config like usernames and registry URLs, which keeps cost down, and Secrets Manager for passwords and DB credentials with 90-day rotation. Access is through IAM roles, never hard-coded. If we were multi-cloud, I'd centralise on HashiCorp Vault.\" If you migrated from elsewhere, you can mention what you used before (e.g. Vault on-prem)."},{"h":"Check yourself","id":"s8","t":"Which feature is the main reason to choose Secrets Manager over Parameter Store? Secrets Manager can rotate secrets on a schedule (built-in for RDS and similar, or via a custom Lambda). Neither AWS service works outside AWS. Your company runs apps on AWS and Azure and wants one place for all secrets. What fits best? Vault is cloud-agnostic, so one central store serves every platform. AWS's services only work inside AWS. A pipeline needs a registry username, a registry URL and a password. What's the cost-aware split? Low-sensitivity values go in cheap Parameter Store. The password, which actually grants access, gets Secrets Manager's extra protection and rotation. Never hard-code secrets."},{"h":"Key takeaways","id":"s9","t":"Secrets (passwords, tokens, keys, credentials) must never live in code or Git. Store them centrally and control access with IAM. Parameter Store: simple, cheap (standard tier free), SecureString for encryption. Great for config and low-sensitivity values. Secrets Manager: built for highly sensitive secrets, with automatic rotation (RDS built-in, Lambda for custom). Costs more. Use both together, split by sensitivity, to balance security and cost. HashiCorp Vault is for multi-cloud or hybrid setups and advanced features, but you operate it yourself."}],"n":25,"slug":"25-secrets-management"},{"title":"Terraform with AWS","lead":"Learn the Terraform basics (providers, resources, variables, state, plan and apply), then use them to build a real project: a VPC with two public subnets, two web servers, an S3 bucket and an Application Load Balancer, all from code.","sections":[{"h":"","id":"","t":"New to Infrastructure as Code? The idea, and how declarative tools compare, is explained in Lesson 13: CloudFormation. This lesson is about Terraform itself."},{"h":"What is Terraform?","id":"s1","t":"Terraform is an Infrastructure as Code tool made by HashiCorp. You describe the resources you want in text files ending in .tf, and Terraform creates, changes or deletes real cloud resources until they match your files. The files are written in HCL (HashiCorp Configuration Language). It reads a lot like JSON or YAML: blocks, names and key = value pairs. It works with many platforms (AWS, Azure, Google Cloud, OpenStack, Alibaba Cloud…), not only AWS. It is fast and repeatable. Building a setup 100 times by clicking in the console takes hours; with Terraform it's one command each time. If everything is lost, you can run the code again and get it back. Learn the console firstTerraform only writes down what you would otherwise click. If you don't know that a subnet needs a route table association, or that a load balancer needs a target group and a listener, Terraform won't tell you. Build the setup by hand once (see Lesson 9), then automate it. Keep the AWS console and the Terraform docs open side by side while you write code."},{"h":"Before you start","id":"s2","t":"Install Terraform and the AWS CLI. Check with terraform -version and aws --version. Create access keys for an IAM user (IAM → your user → Security credentials → Create access key). Terraform talks to AWS through its API, so it needs keys, not a console password. Never share them. Run aws configure and paste the keys and a region (for example us-east-1). Terraform's AWS provider reads these saved credentials automatically. Details in Lesson 12: AWS CLI. Check permissions. Terraform can only do what the IAM user is allowed to do. If the user has no EC2 or VPC permissions, apply fails. See Lesson 3: IAM. Optional: install the official HashiCorp Terraform extension in VS Code for autocomplete and syntax highlighting. Watch outThe provider docs let you put access_key and secret_key directly in the provider block, but they also warn against it. Hard-coded keys end up in Git. Use aws configure, environment variables or a role instead."},{"h":"The building blocks","id":"s3","t":"Provider The plugin that lets Terraform talk to a platform's API. provider \"aws\" means \"build things on AWS\". You find every provider and its versions in the Terraform Registry. Resource One thing to create, for example resource \"aws_vpc\" \"myvpc\". The first label is the type (fixed by the provider), the second is a local name that only Terraform uses. It's not the name you see in the console. Variable An input value kept in one place (usually variables.tf) and used as var.name. Change it once, and every place that uses it follows. Output A value Terraform prints after apply, for example the load balancer's DNS name. Handy when you don't have console access. State The file terraform.tfstate, where Terraform remembers what it already created. It compares your code with the state to work out what to change. Don't edit it by hand, and never push it to GitHub: it can contain secrets. Data source A read-only lookup of something that already exists, for example \"find the latest Ubuntu AMI\". Declared with a data block instead of resource."},{"h":"The Terraform workflow","id":"s4","t":"Command What it does terraform init Prepares the folder and downloads the provider plugins. Run it first, and again after adding a provider. terraform fmt Formats your code neatly (aligns the = signs, fixes indentation). terraform validate Checks syntax and references, and tells you the file and line of any mistake. terraform plan A dry run: shows what would be created, changed or destroyed. Nothing is touched. terraform apply Shows the plan again, asks you to type yes, then makes the changes. -auto-approve skips the question. terraform destroy Deletes everything this configuration created. Use it when you finish practising so you don't get billed. In the plan output, + means create, ~ means change in place, - means destroy, and -/+ means destroy and re-create (needed when a setting can't be changed on a live resource). The summary line reads like Plan: 7 to add, 0 to change, 0 to destroy. Try it: plan & apply simulator The checkboxes are your .tf code. The \"state\" column is what Terraform remembers creating. Edit the code, press Plan to see the diff, then Apply to make the state match. Try unticking the VPC while subnets still use it, or changing the VPC CIDR after it exists. Press \"terraform plan\" to start. The state is empty, so everything in the code will be created."},{"h":"The project we'll build","id":"s5","t":"This is the same setup as the production-style VPC project, now written as code. To keep it simple, both web servers sit in public subnets. What the Terraform code creates. Every arrow and box here is its own resource in code. Need a refresher on the parts? Subnets, route tables and internet gateways: Lesson 6. Security groups: Lesson 7. Load balancers, target groups and listeners: Lesson 28."},{"h":"Step 1: provider and variables","id":"s6","t":"Copy the provider block from the Terraform Registry (click Use provider on the AWS provider page). The terraform block pins the provider version. It's also where you would later set a remote backend to keep state in S3. # provider.tf terraform { required_providers { aws = { source = \"hashicorp/aws\" version = \"~> 5.0\" } } } provider \"aws\" { region = \"us-east-1\" } # variables.tf variable \"cidr\" { default = \"10.0.0.0/16\" } Now run terraform init. It creates a hidden .terraform folder and a lock file. Leave those alone; you only edit the .tf files."},{"h":"Step 2: networking","id":"s7","t":"Splitting code across files (provider.tf, variables.tf, main.tf…) is good practice; Terraform reads every .tf file in the folder together. # main.tf resource \"aws_vpc\" \"myvpc\" { cidr_block = var.cidr } resource \"aws_subnet\" \"sub1\" { vpc_id = aws_vpc.myvpc.id cidr_block = \"10.0.0.0/24\" availability_zone = \"us-east-1a\" map_public_ip_on_launch = true } resource \"aws_subnet\" \"sub2\" { vpc_id = aws_vpc.myvpc.id cidr_block = \"10.0.1.0/24\" availability_zone = \"us-east-1b\" map_public_ip_on_launch = true } resource \"aws_internet_gateway\" \"igw\" { vpc_id = aws_vpc.myvpc.id } resource \"aws_route_table\" \"rt\" { vpc_id = aws_vpc.myvpc.id route { cidr_block = \"0.0.0.0/0\" gateway_id = aws_internet_gateway.igw.id } } resource \"aws_route_table_association\" \"rta1\" { subnet_id = aws_subnet.sub1.id route_table_id = aws_route_table.rt.id } resource \"aws_route_table_association\" \"rta2\" { subnet_id = aws_subnet.sub2.id route_table_id = aws_route_table.rt.id } Think of it like…Writing aws_vpc.myvpc.id is like writing \"the ID of the VPC I named myvpc, whatever it turns out to be\". The ID doesn't exist yet, so Terraform learns it must create the VPC first. These references are how Terraform works out the build order on its own. Read the docs to see what each argument expects. Most want .id, but some (like target groups and listeners) want .arn. Some take a single string, others a list in [ ]. Run terraform validate, then terraform plan. It should say 7 to add: the VPC, two subnets, the internet gateway, the route table and two associations. Then run terraform apply and type yes."},{"h":"Step 3: security group, S3 bucket and web servers","id":"s8","t":"resource \"aws_security_group\" \"webSg\" { name = \"web\" vpc_id = aws_vpc.myvpc.id ingress { description = \"HTTP from anywhere\" from_port = 80 to_port = 80 protocol = \"tcp\" cidr_blocks = [\"0.0.0.0/0\"] } ingress { description = \"SSH\" from_port = 22 to_port = 22 protocol = \"tcp\" cidr_blocks = [\"0.0.0.0/0\"] } egress { from_port = 0 to_port = 0 protocol = \"-1\" cidr_blocks = [\"0.0.0.0/0\"] } } resource \"aws_s3_bucket\" \"example\" { bucket = \"my-unique-terraform-project-bucket-2023\" # must be globally unique } resource \"aws_instance\" \"webserver1\" { ami = \"ami-xxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxx\" # Ubuntu AMI id from YOUR region instance_type = \"t2.micro\" vpc_security_group_ids = [aws_security_group.webSg.id] subnet_id = aws_subnet.sub1.id user_data = base64encode(file(\"userdata.sh\")) } resource \"aws_instance\" \"webserver2\" { ami = \"ami-xxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxx\" instance_type = \"t2.micro\" vpc_security_group_ids = [aws_security_group.webSg.id] subnet_id = aws_subnet.sub2.id user_data = base64encode(file(\"userdata1.sh\")) } Ingress = inbound rules, egress = outbound. protocol = \"-1\" means all protocols. AMI IDs differ per region. Copy one from the EC2 launch page in your region, or look it up with a data \"aws_ami\" block. User data is a start-up script that runs on first boot. Keeping it in its own .sh file keeps main.tf short. file() reads the file; base64encode() encodes it the way the user-data field expects. Each script installs Apache and writes a small page. The two scripts print different text, so you can see the load balancer switching between servers later: #!/bin/bash apt update apt install -y apache2 # Ask the instance metadata service for this server's ID INSTANCE_ID=$(curl -s http://169.254.169.254/latest/meta-data/instance-id) cat <<EOF > /var/www/html/index.html <h1>Terraform Project Server 1</h1> <p>Instance ID: $INSTANCE_ID</p> EOF systemctl start apache2 systemctl enable apache2 Note169.254.169.254 is the instance metadata address. From inside any EC2 instance you can call it to learn the instance's ID, IP, region and more. Newer instances may require IMDSv2, which needs a session token first. If the ID shows up blank, that's why. TipUser data errors are silent: the script runs in the background and you won't see the error in Terraform. Test the script on a Linux machine first. You can check what an instance received under Actions → Instance settings → Edit user data."},{"h":"Step 4: the load balancer","id":"s9","t":"In the console, one wizard creates the load balancer, target group and listener together. In Terraform each is a separate resource, and you have to connect them yourself: resource \"aws_lb\" \"myalb\" { name = \"myalb\" internal = false # internet-facing load_balancer_type = \"application\" security_groups = [aws_security_group.webSg.id] subnets = [aws_subnet.sub1.id, aws_subnet.sub2.id] tags = { Name = \"web\" } } resource \"aws_lb_target_group\" \"tg\" { name = \"myTG\" port = 80 protocol = \"HTTP\" vpc_id = aws_vpc.myvpc.id health_check { path = \"/\" port = \"traffic-port\" } } resource \"aws_lb_target_group_attachment\" \"attach1\" { target_group_arn = aws_lb_target_group.tg.arn target_id = aws_instance.webserver1.id port = 80 } resource \"aws_lb_target_group_attachment\" \"attach2\" { target_group_arn = aws_lb_target_group.tg.arn target_id = aws_instance.webserver2.id port = 80 } resource \"aws_lb_listener\" \"listener\" { load_balancer_arn = aws_lb.myalb.arn port = 80 protocol = \"HTTP\" default_action { type = \"forward\" target_group_arn = aws_lb_target_group.tg.arn } } output \"loadbalancerdns\" { value = aws_lb.myalb.dns_name } The target group holds the servers and health-checks them on /. Unhealthy servers get no traffic. The attachments put each instance into the target group. With many servers you'd use count or for_each instead of copying the block. Without the listener, the load balancer and target group exist but aren't connected, and you get no response. The output prints the DNS name at the end of apply. Open it in a browser and refresh: the page switches between server 1 and server 2. Wait until the load balancer is Active and both targets are Healthy. This can take a few minutes. Better securityFor simplicity, the load balancer and instances share one security group here. In real work, give the load balancer its own group and let the instances accept port 80 only from that group."},{"h":"Common errors (and how to fix them)","id":"s10","t":"Error Cause Duplicate resource … already declared You copied a block and forgot to change its local name (e.g. two rta1 or two attach1). Reference to undeclared resource A reference points to a name that doesn't exist, often left over from code pasted from an older project. Unsupported block type \"tags\" tags is an argument, so it needs an equals sign: tags = { … }. Plan doesn't show your change The file wasn't saved. Terraform reads the files on disk. Access denied on apply The IAM user lacks permissions for that service. Errors are normal, even for engineers with years of experience. Read the message, find the line, check the docs. Fixing them yourself teaches you more than copying a working repo."},{"h":"Finishing up","id":"s11","t":"Add a .gitignore with .terraform/, *.tfstate and *.tfstate.backup before pushing code to GitHub. Run terraform destroy when done. The load balancer costs money even when idle. Challenge: add an IAM role and instance profile so the servers can read from the S3 bucket (roles are covered in Lesson 3). Next steps: modules and remote state in S3 with DynamoDB locking are covered in Lesson 34. Interview angle\"Walk me through a Terraform project\" is common. Explain the order: provider → network (VPC, subnets, IGW, route table, associations) → security group → compute (EC2 + user data) → load balancer (LB, target group, attachments, listener) → output. Then mention init/plan/apply, state hygiene (not in Git, remote backend) and how references create dependencies."},{"h":"Check yourself","id":"s12","t":"What does terraform plan do? plan is a dry run. init downloads providers, apply makes changes, destroy deletes. You wrote subnet_id = aws_subnet.sub1.id in an EC2 resource. What else does this do? A reference creates an implicit dependency. Terraform builds a graph from them and creates resources in the right order. Your ALB and target group were created, but the browser gets no response from the ALB. What is most likely missing? The listener connects the load balancer to the target group. Also check that both targets show as healthy."},{"h":"Key takeaways","id":"s13","t":"Terraform describes infrastructure in .tf files: a provider (AWS) plus resources, variables and outputs. The workflow is init → validate/fmt → plan → apply, and destroy to clean up. The state file is Terraform's memory of what exists. Keep it out of Git. References like aws_vpc.myvpc.id wire resources together and set the build order. Things the console does for you in one wizard (route table associations, target groups, listeners) are separate resources in code. Know how to build it in the console first, keep the docs open, and treat errors as part of learning."}],"n":26,"slug":"26-terraform"},{"title":"AWS Config: Compliance Checks","lead":"Companies have rules about how cloud resources must be set up. AWS Config watches your resources and flags which ones follow those rules and which don't. You'll learn the idea of compliance, managed vs custom rules, and how to write a custom rule backed by a Lambda function.","sections":[{"h":"Start with a story","id":"s1","t":"Your company has a rule: every EC2 instance must have detailed monitoring turned on. Detailed monitoring sends metrics every minute instead of every five, which helps the team spot security or application problems quickly. Now picture an account with thousands of instances, created by many developers and DevOps engineers. Some will forget the checkbox. Who notices? Checking by hand doesn't scale. That's the problem AWS Config solves."},{"h":"What is compliance?","id":"s2","t":"Compliance means your resources follow your organisation's rules. A resource that follows them is compliant; one that breaks them is non-compliant. Every organisation has different rules, depending on its industry. A bank, a government project and a startup will care about very different things. Some examples: Every EC2 instance must have detailed monitoring enabled. No S3 bucket may allow public access. Every S3 bucket must have a lifecycle policy. Every resource must carry an owner tag. As a DevOps engineer, you don't invent these rules. Ask your manager or security team for the list of mandatory checks, then turn each one into a Config rule. Compliance is part of security, so companies treat it as a high priority."},{"h":"What AWS Config does","id":"s3","t":"In the console, Config is described as \"track resource inventory and changes\". It does three things: Records the configuration of your resources (what exists and how it's set up), and each time that configuration changes. Evaluates each resource against your rules. Shows a dashboard of compliant and non-compliant resources, per rule and per resource. NoteConfig has to be switched on first: you set up a recorder (which resource types to track) and a place, usually an S3 bucket, where it delivers configuration history. Config also has its own charges per item recorded and per rule check, so watch the bill on large accounts."},{"h":"Two kinds of rules","id":"s4","t":"AWS managed rules Ready-made rules written by AWS: pick one from the list, fill in a few settings, done. Examples: s3-bucket-public-read-prohibited, required-tags, ec2-instance-detailed-monitoring-enabled. Always check this list first. Custom rules For checks AWS doesn't provide. You write the logic yourself, most often as a Lambda function (a \"custom Lambda rule\"). The console also offers custom rules written in Guard, a policy language. This lesson focuses on Lambda. A rule runs on one of two kinds of trigger: Configuration changes: runs whenever a matching resource is created, changed or deleted. Results update within minutes. Periodic: runs on a schedule, like a cron job (for example every 24 hours)."},{"h":"How a custom Lambda rule works","id":"s5","t":"Config detects the change and calls your Lambda, which does the actual check and reports the result back. Lambda functions are event-driven: something has to invoke them. Here, Config is the caller. It passes the function a JSON event describing the resource that changed. The function checks that resource and sends back a verdict. See Lesson 19: Lambda for the basics."},{"h":"Build it step by step","id":"s6","t":"Create the Lambda function first (Python runtime, default execution role is fine). You need its ARN before you can create the rule. Give the function's IAM role permissions to read EC2 (ec2:DescribeInstances), report to Config (config:PutEvaluations) and write CloudWatch logs. For a first demo, broad policies such as EC2 full access, CloudWatch full access and the AWS_ConfigRole managed policy get you going. Then trim them down to least privilege (Lesson 3). Paste the code below and deploy. In Config → Rules → Add rule, choose Create custom Lambda rule, give it a name (e.g. test-demo) and paste the Lambda ARN. Set the trigger to When configuration changes, scope = resources, resource type = AWS::EC2::Instance. Leave the resource ID blank to watch all instances. Save. Config runs the first check and fills in the dashboard."},{"h":"The Lambda code","id":"s7","t":"The function is written in Python with boto3, the AWS SDK for Python. It talks to EC2 and Config. The entry point is lambda_handler, which is like main in other languages. import json import boto3 ec2 = boto3.client(\"ec2\") config = boto3.client(\"config\") def lambda_handler(event, context): # Config sends the changed resource as a JSON string inside the event invoking_event = json.loads(event[\"invokingEvent\"]) item = invoking_event[\"configurationItem\"] instance_id = item[\"resourceId\"] compliance = \"COMPLIANT\" # assume good until proven otherwise if item[\"configurationItemStatus\"] == \"ResourceDeleted\": compliance = \"NOT_APPLICABLE\" else: resp = ec2.describe_instances(InstanceIds=[instance_id]) instance = resp[\"Reservations\"][0][\"Instances\"][0] if instance[\"Monitoring\"][\"State\"] != \"enabled\": compliance = \"NON_COMPLIANT\" # Report the verdict back to AWS Config config.put_evaluations( Evaluations=[{ \"ComplianceResourceType\": item[\"resourceType\"], \"ComplianceResourceId\": instance_id, \"ComplianceType\": compliance, \"OrderingTimestamp\": item[\"configurationItemCaptureTime\"], }], ResultToken=event[\"resultToken\"], ) return {\"compliance\": compliance} invokingEvent holds the resource's configuration. resultToken tells Config which evaluation run this answer belongs to. The function assumes \"compliant\", then flips to \"non-compliant\" if monitoring isn't enabled. You don't need to memorise boto3. Everyone, experienced engineers included, writes this kind of code with the boto3 documentation open. Try it: compliance checker Choose which rules are active, then change the resources' settings. Each resource is checked against every active rule that applies to its type, the same way Config evaluates them."},{"h":"Troubleshooting: when the dashboard doesn't update","id":"s8","t":"Config, Lambda and EC2 are separate services, so results can take a few minutes. If nothing changes: Check the rule's last successful evaluation time. If it's old, the rule hasn't run yet. Open CloudWatch → Log groups and find the function's log group (/aws/lambda/<name>). Read the latest log stream. See Lesson 18. A common error is \"Task timed out after 3.0 seconds\". Lambda's default timeout is 3 seconds, and a cold start plus two API calls can exceed it. Raise it under Configuration → General configuration (10 seconds is plenty). Back in Config, choose Actions → Re-evaluate to run the rule again right away. TipBe patient. Even when everything is correct, a change can take 2–3 minutes or more to appear on the dashboard."},{"h":"Practice: do the same for S3","id":"s9","t":"Once the EC2 rule works, create a second rule with resource type AWS::S3::Bucket. Write a function that marks a bucket non-compliant if public access isn't blocked. Bucket settings are explained in Lesson 11: S3. Interview angle\"How do you make sure resources follow company policy?\" Answer: collect the mandatory rules from security/management, use AWS Config managed rules where they exist, write custom Lambda rules for the rest, trigger on configuration change, and review the compliance dashboard (and alert on it). Mentioning a concrete example like EC2 detailed monitoring makes the answer sound real."},{"h":"Check yourself","id":"s10","t":"Your rule needs a check that no AWS managed rule offers. What do you create? Custom Lambda rules let you write any check you need. Config invokes the function and the function reports the result with put_evaluations. Your rule never updates and the Lambda log says \"Task timed out after 3.0 seconds\". What's the fix? Lambda's default timeout is 3 seconds. Raise it, then use Re-evaluate on the rule. Which trigger updates compliance soon after someone edits an instance? Change-triggered rules run when a matching resource is created, modified or deleted. Periodic rules wait for the schedule."},{"h":"Key takeaways","id":"s11","t":"Compliance = resources follow your organisation's rules. Config labels each resource COMPLIANT or NON_COMPLIANT. AWS Config records resource configurations and their changes, and evaluates them against rules. Use managed rules when AWS already has the check; write custom Lambda rules for everything else. A rule triggers on configuration changes or periodically. The Lambda reads the event, checks the resource with boto3, and reports back with put_evaluations. Its role needs the matching permissions. Debug with CloudWatch Logs; watch the 3-second default Lambda timeout."}],"n":27,"slug":"27-aws-config"},{"title":"Load Balancers: ALB vs NLB vs GWLB","lead":"A load balancer spreads incoming traffic across several servers so no single server gets overwhelmed. AWS has three modern types. Picking the right one depends on which network \"layer\" it works at, so we'll cover just enough of the OSI model first, then compare ALB, NLB and GWLB with real use cases.","sections":[{"h":"Why you need a load balancer","id":"s1","t":"Say you build a game and run it on one EC2 instance. Five players, no problem. The game goes viral and now hundreds of people play at once. One server can't keep up: some players see slowness, others see downtime. Either way, it's a bad experience. The fix: run the game on several identical instances and put a load balancer in front. Users connect to the load balancer, never to a server directly, and it decides which server handles each request. Spreads the load: 100 requests across 3 servers ≈ 33 each. Scales out: still too slow? Add more instances behind it, for example with an Auto Scaling Group (Lesson 9). Keeps the app up: it checks each server's health and stops sending traffic to broken ones. This is what makes an app highly available. Load-balancing algorithms An algorithm here just means the rule for choosing a server: Round robin: take turns, 1 → 2 → 3 → 1… The simplest option, good when all servers are identical (same CPU, RAM, OS). Weighted / ratio-based: a bigger server gets a bigger share, e.g. 50% to the powerful one and 25% each to the other two. Least outstanding requests: send the next request to the server that is least busy right now (an ALB option). Load balancers aren't unique to AWS. NGINX, F5, Envoy, Traefik and Ambassador are popular ones, each with its own set of algorithms. Try it: watch requests get distributed Pick an algorithm and send traffic. Mark a server unhealthy to see the health check take it out of rotation. Algorithm No requests yet."},{"h":"Just enough OSI model","id":"s2","t":"When you open linkedin.com/in/someone in a browser, the request passes through seven layers on its way to the server. Each layer adds one job. You only need a rough idea of each, because different load balancers work at different layers. # Layer What happens there (simplified) 7 Application The protocol you speak: HTTP, FTP… Your browser builds the HTTP request for you. ALB works here. 6 Presentation Encoding and encryption, e.g. TLS (the successor to SSL) for HTTPS. 5 Session Opens and tracks the session between client and server. 4 Transport Splits data into small segments and delivers them reliably (TCP) or quickly (UDP), using ports. NLB works here. 3 Network IP addresses; packets hop across routers toward the destination. GWLB works here. 2 Data link Switches move frames inside the local network or data centre. 1 Physical Cables and signals that finally reach the server. Think of it like…A Layer 7 balancer is a receptionist who opens your letter, reads it, and walks it to the right department. A Layer 4 balancer is a mail sorter who only reads the address and port on the envelope, which makes it much faster, but it can't route by what's inside."},{"h":"Application Load Balancer (ALB): Layer 7","id":"s3","t":"The ALB reads each HTTP(S) request: the path, the host name, the headers, query strings. Then it decides where to send it. That allows content-based routing: One ALB, one domain, many services: listener rules send each path to its own target group. Path-based: /payments → payment service, /login → login service. Host-based: shop.example.in and shop.example.com go to different servers. Header/query-based and weighted routing, e.g. send 10% of traffic to a new version. TLS termination (SSL offloading): the ALB decrypts HTTPS so your servers don't have to; it can also re-encrypt toward the servers. Built for websites, APIs and microservices. It's also what Kubernetes ingress uses on EKS (Lesson 24). Trade-off: reading every request costs time and money. The ALB adds a little latency (an extra \"hop\" that inspects the request) and costs more per unit of traffic than an NLB."},{"h":"Network Load Balancer (NLB): Layer 4","id":"s4","t":"The NLB doesn't look inside the request. It forwards TCP and UDP traffic based on IP address and port, which makes it very fast: Ultra-low latency and huge throughput (millions of requests per second). Handles non-HTTP protocols: game servers, video streaming, IoT, databases, any custom TCP/UDP protocol. Can have a static IP per Availability Zone, which helps when clients or firewalls need to allow-list fixed IPs. A TCP connection stays on the same target for as long as it lasts, which suits long streams. NLB can also pin a client to one target by source IP (stickiness). Cheaper than an ALB for the same volume of traffic. Typical users: online games and live/video streaming platforms, where even a small delay is not acceptable and HTTP-level routing isn't needed. Clearing up a common mythYou'll sometimes hear \"only NLB has sticky sessions\". Not quite: an ALB supports sticky sessions too, using cookies, so a user's requests keep going to the same target. The real difference is the layer: ALB sticks at HTTP level with a cookie; NLB keeps each TCP connection on one target and can stick by client IP."},{"h":"Gateway Load Balancer (GWLB): Layer 3","id":"s5","t":"GWLB is a different tool for a different job. It sits in front of virtual appliances: third-party firewalls, intrusion detection/prevention systems (IDS/IPS) and deep packet inspection tools, usually running on EC2. It works at Layer 3 (IP packets) and is transparent: it passes the original packets unchanged to the appliance fleet, wrapped in a tunnel (the GENEVE protocol), and gets them back after inspection. It spreads load across the appliances and scales them, while keeping each traffic flow on the same appliance. ALB and NLB aren't designed for this. They terminate or forward connections to an application rather than passing every packet through a security appliance and back. Typical users: companies that build or run firewall/VPN-style security appliances, or that must route all traffic through a central inspection layer. NoteThere's also the Classic Load Balancer, the older previous-generation option. New designs use ALB, NLB or GWLB."},{"h":"The pieces of every AWS load balancer","id":"s6","t":"Listener A port and protocol the load balancer listens on (e.g. HTTPS :443), plus rules saying what to do with matching traffic: forward, redirect (HTTP→HTTPS) or return a fixed response. Target group The set of destinations: EC2 instances, IP addresses, Lambda functions (ALB) or containers. Each group has its own port and protocol. Health check The load balancer regularly calls each target (e.g. GET /). Targets that fail are marked unhealthy and get no traffic until they recover. Subnets / AZs You enable the load balancer in subnets in two or more Availability Zones, so losing one AZ doesn't take the app down. Want to see these pieces in code? Lesson 26 creates an ALB, target group, attachments and listener with Terraform."},{"h":"Side-by-side","id":"s7","t":"ALB NLB GWLB OSI layer 7 (application) 4 (transport) 3 (network) Traffic HTTP, HTTPS, gRPC, WebSockets TCP, UDP, TLS All IP packets Routing by Path, host, headers, query, weights IP + port Flow, to appliances Speed Fast, slight extra latency Fastest, ultra-low latency Depends on appliances Cost Higher Lower Specialised Use it for Websites, APIs, microservices Games, streaming, non-HTTP, static IPs Firewalls, IDS/IPS, inspection Try it: which load balancer? Pick the requirement that matters most. Choose a requirement above. Interview angleDevOps engineers and developers choose the load balancer together. A strong answer names the layer and gives the reason: \"Our microservices sit behind one ALB with path-based rules because we route on URL; our real-time game servers use an NLB because they speak UDP and need low latency; traffic inspection goes through a GWLB in front of our firewall appliances.\""},{"h":"Check yourself","id":"s8","t":"You need /api/* to go to one service and /images/* to another, under the same domain. Which load balancer? Path-based routing requires reading the HTTP request, which is Layer 7, the ALB's job. A multiplayer game uses UDP and players hate lag. Which fits best? NLB works at Layer 4, supports UDP and adds very little latency. All traffic must pass through a fleet of third-party firewall appliances. Which load balancer is built for that? Gateway Load Balancer transparently distributes packets to virtual appliances such as firewalls and IDS/IPS."},{"h":"Key takeaways","id":"s9","t":"A load balancer spreads traffic across healthy targets so apps stay fast and available. Round robin takes turns; weighted sends more to stronger servers; health checks remove broken ones. ALB = Layer 7: reads HTTP, routes by path/host/header, terminates TLS. Smart but slightly slower and pricier. NLB = Layer 4: TCP/UDP by IP and port, ultra-low latency, static IPs. For games, streaming and non-HTTP traffic. GWLB = Layer 3: transparently feeds traffic through firewalls and other security appliances. Every load balancer is built from listeners, rules, target groups and health checks, spread across multiple AZs."}],"n":28,"slug":"28-load-balancers"},{"title":"AWS Interview Q&A Collection","lead":"You've done the theory and the hands-on work. Before an interview you also need a quick-reference you can skim the night before. This lesson covers how to use a large bank of AWS DevOps interview questions well, and gives you a representative set of questions by service, each linked to the lesson that explains it.","sections":[{"h":"Why a question bank?","id":"s1","t":"Courses and projects build real skill, but interviews test recall under pressure. A well-organised question bank, roughly 500 questions across about 30 AWS services, gives you: One place to revise. One file per service (e.g. an ecs.md), each a list of questions with short answers. A fast gap check. Read a question, try to answer it, then compare. If you couldn't answer it, that's a topic to revisit. Job-description targeting. If the posting says \"strong ECS experience\", go straight to the ECS questions. Questions in such a bank come in three levels: Basic / theory \"What is…?\", \"What's the difference between…?\" These check that you know the vocabulary. Advanced How things work inside, limits, trade-offs, best practices. Scenario-based \"Your app is slow / insecure / expensive. What do you do?\" Interviewers increasingly focus on these, because they show real experience. Keep it growing Add questions you meet in real interviews or at work. A shared repo that others can contribute to stays up to date."},{"h":"How to prepare","id":"s2","t":"Split each study session between theory, hands-on work and interview practice, and let the gaps decide what you study next. Balance your time. With four hours, spend about two on theory and hands-on work and two on interview prep. Learning ECS today? One hour theory, one hour practical, one hour on ECS questions. Answer out loud before reading the answer. Rehearse how you would put it to an interviewer, not the book's wording. Tie answers to something you built. \"In my project I put the ALB in public subnets and the app in private ones…\" sounds more convincing than a definition. Interview tomorrow? Skip new labs. Skim the bank for the services in the job description and revise the scenario questions. Be honest about gaps. \"I haven't hit that scenario yet; here's how I'd approach it\" is better than bluffing. TipA strong answer has three parts: a one-line definition, how you'd use it (or did use it), and one trade-off or gotcha. Scenario answers add the steps you'd take, in order. More career advice (resume, projects, how to approach the job hunt) is in Lesson 31. Earlier scenario questions on EC2, IAM and VPC are in Lesson 10. Try it: random interview flashcards Pick a service (or \"All\"), read the question, answer it out loud, then reveal the model answer. Service Press \"Next question\"."},{"h":"Representative questions by service","id":"s3","t":"A sample of the kind of questions you'll face. Each answer is short on purpose; the linked lesson has the full explanation. Compute, identity and networking EC2: what's the difference between stopping and terminating an instance? Stopping shuts it down but keeps the root EBS volume, so you can start it again (you stop paying for compute, but still pay for storage). Terminating deletes the instance, and by default its root volume too. → Lesson 4 IAM: role vs user. When do you use a role? A user is a long-lived identity for a person or tool with its own credentials. A role has no permanent credentials; it's assumed and hands out temporary ones. Use roles for EC2/Lambda/other services and for cross-account access, so access keys never sit on a server. → Lesson 3 VPC: what makes a subnet \"public\"? Its route table has a route 0.0.0.0/0 to an internet gateway. Private subnets reach the internet outbound through a NAT gateway instead. → Lesson 6 Security groups vs NACLs? Security groups are attached to instances (ENIs), are stateful and only have allow rules. NACLs are attached to subnets, are stateless (return traffic needs its own rule), support allow and deny, and are evaluated in rule-number order. → Lesson 7 Scenario: users report the site is down after one AZ failed. How do you prevent this next time? Run instances in at least two AZs with an Auto Scaling Group, put a load balancer in front with health checks, and use Multi-AZ for the database. → Lesson 9, Lesson 28 Storage, DNS and content delivery S3: how do you protect against accidental deletes? Turn on versioning (deleted objects can be restored), optionally MFA Delete, restrict delete permissions in bucket policies, and replicate critical data. → Lesson 11 Route 53: CNAME vs alias record? A CNAME points a name to another name and can't be used at the zone apex (example.com). An alias is Route 53-specific, works at the apex, points directly to AWS resources such as an ALB or CloudFront, and alias queries to AWS resources are free. → Lesson 8 CloudFront: users in Asia see slow downloads from a US bucket. Fix? Put CloudFront in front of the bucket so content is cached at edge locations near users, and lock the bucket to CloudFront with origin access control. → Lesson 21 Infrastructure as Code and tooling CloudFormation vs Terraform? Both are declarative IaC. CloudFormation is AWS-only and AWS manages its state for you. Terraform is multi-cloud, uses HCL, and keeps its own state file, which you must store safely (e.g. S3 + DynamoDB lock). → Lesson 13, Lesson 26 Terraform: why must the state file never go to Git? It can contain secrets in plain text, and if two people apply from different copies they overwrite each other. Use a remote backend with locking instead. → Lesson 34 AWS CLI: how do you work with several accounts from one laptop? Use named profiles (aws configure --profile dev) and pick one with --profile or the AWS_PROFILE variable. → Lesson 12 CI/CD, monitoring and serverless What are buildspec.yml and appspec.yml? buildspec.yml tells CodeBuild how to build and test (phases and commands). appspec.yml tells CodeDeploy how to deploy: which files go where, and which hook scripts to run. → Lesson 16, Lesson 17 CloudWatch: how do you get alerted when CPU goes above 80%? Create a CloudWatch alarm on the CPUUtilization metric with an 80% threshold and an SNS topic as the action. Memory and disk need the CloudWatch agent (custom metrics). → Lesson 18 Lambda: when would you not use it? For jobs longer than the 15-minute limit, steady high-traffic workloads where EC2/containers cost less, or apps that need special OS-level control. → Lesson 19 Scenario: the bill doubled because of old EBS snapshots. How do you stop it happening again? Automate cleanup: a scheduled Lambda (boto3) finds snapshots not linked to any active volume or instance and deletes them, plus budgets and alerts. → Lesson 20 Containers, security and compliance ECS vs EKS? ECS is AWS's own simpler orchestrator; EKS is managed Kubernetes, which is portable and has a huge ecosystem but is more complex. Both can run on Fargate. → Lesson 23 Where should database passwords live? Not in code or environment files. Use Secrets Manager (built-in rotation) or Systems Manager Parameter Store (SecureString, cheaper), with IAM controlling who can read them. → Lesson 25 How do you find EC2 instances that break a company rule? AWS Config: use a managed rule if one exists, or write a custom Lambda rule, and review the compliance dashboard. → Lesson 27 ALB or NLB for a UDP game server? NLB: it handles UDP at Layer 4 with very low latency. ALB only understands HTTP(S). → Lesson 28 Scenario: \"How did you migrate an app to AWS?\" Walk through the phases (prepare, plan, migrate, monitor, optimize) and name the strategy you used for each group of apps, instead of only saying \"lift and shift\". → Lesson 30 Interview angleInterviewers can tell memorised definitions from experience. After a definition, add \"and here's where I used it…\" with a detail from one of your projects. Even a lab project counts if you can explain the decisions you made."},{"h":"Check yourself","id":"s4","t":"Your interview is tomorrow and the job description stresses ECS. What's the best use of tonight? With little time, target the services in the job description and rehearse your answers. Which kind of question are interviewers focusing on more and more? Scenarios show whether you can apply knowledge, which is what the job needs."},{"h":"Key takeaways","id":"s5","t":"A question bank is a revision tool, not a replacement for theory and hands-on practice. Split study time between theory, practicals and interview prep; let unanswered questions show you what to study next. Expect basic, advanced and especially scenario-based questions. Answer with definition → how you used it → a trade-off; for scenarios, give ordered steps. Target the services in the job description, and be honest about gaps."}],"n":29,"slug":"29-interview-qa"},{"title":"Migrating Applications to AWS","lead":"Many companies, especially banks and large enterprises, still run on their own servers and are moving to the cloud gradually. This lesson covers how a real migration project runs (five phases), the \"7 Rs\" of migration strategy, how databases are moved safely, and how to talk about all of it in an interview.","sections":[{"h":"","id":"","t":"On-premises vs cloud, and why companies move, is covered in Lesson 2: Introduction to AWS."},{"h":"Don't start with the 7 Rs","id":"s1","t":"Ask most candidates \"how did you migrate to AWS?\" and they'll reply \"we used lift and shift\". Anyone can read that in a blog. Interviewers want to hear how the project actually ran: the phases, how apps were grouped, how you checked things were working and what you improved afterwards. The 7 Rs are just one decision inside that process."},{"h":"The five phases of a migration project","id":"s2","t":"Preparation and planning happen once. Migrate and monitor loop for every wave of applications. Optimization comes at the end. 1. Prepare Look at the current architecture. Is the application one big monolith (everything in one codebase and one deployment), or already split into microservices (small, independently deployable services)? If it's a monolith and the company wants to be cloud native, developers and DevOps engineers may first split it into microservices. Microservices fit containers well: each service becomes its own container, deployed on an orchestrator like EKS or ECS. See Lesson 22 for containers and Lesson 23 for orchestration. 2. Plan Two big decisions: Waves (phases). Don't move 200 services at once. Group them, for example: Wave 1: ~50 low-risk, less user-facing apps. Use them as a proof of concept. Middle waves: slightly more critical, then critical apps. Last wave: the ~20 most critical apps, once the process is proven. Strategy per app: which of the 7 Rs fits each group (below). Preparation and planning are done once. After that, you don't go back to them. 3. Migrate Move one wave at a time. As a DevOps engineer you'll typically: Write Terraform or CloudFormation to create the target infrastructure (Lesson 26). Write shell scripts for start-up and setup tasks, and cron jobs for recurring work. Set up CloudWatch dashboards and alarms (Lesson 18). Schedule the test team's automation suites, e.g. a daily Jenkins job against the new environment. 4. Monitor After a wave goes live, watch it for weeks or months, depending on how critical it is. Compare performance before and after with dashboards, and collect feedback from internal users or beta customers. Only when the wave looks healthy do you start the next one. Migrate + monitor repeat for every wave, so a project with 10 waves runs this loop 10 times. 5. Optimize When everything has moved, evaluate: what did the migration achieve? Lower cost? Less maintenance? Better availability? Then improve on it. For example, \"we cut costs by 40%; with rightsizing and reserved capacity we can reach 50% within six months\". Each improvement becomes a ticket in the backlog."},{"h":"The 7 Rs of migration","id":"s3","t":"For each application you choose one strategy. In practice, most companies rely on the first three. Strategy What it means Example Rehost \"lift and shift\" Move as-is, with minimal or no changes. Fastest and most popular. Works well when the app doesn't depend much on specific hardware or OS. Gains in cost and scaling are small until you optimise later. On-prem Kubernetes with 3 namespaces → the same cluster layout built on EC2 in AWS. Replatform \"lift, tinker and shift\" Mostly as-is, plus a few changes to use AWS features for availability, scaling or cost. No change to the core code. Move a self-managed MySQL to Amazon RDS; spread nodes across several AZs. Refactor / re-architect Redesign the app to be cloud native. Most effort, most long-term benefit. Break a monolith into containerised microservices on EKS or serverless. Relocate Move a whole platform to its AWS-hosted equivalent, without buying new hardware or rewriting apps. VMware vSphere → VMware Cloud on AWS. On-prem OpenShift → Red Hat OpenShift Service on AWS (ROSA). Repurchase \"drop and shop\" Replace the app with a different product, usually SaaS. A self-hosted CRM → a SaaS CRM. Less common today. Retain Keep it on-premises for now, often for security, compliance or because it's not worth moving yet. Cloud apps connect to it securely. A bank keeps its mainframe payment systems on-prem; the other apps on AWS reach them through a secure gateway. Retire Switch it off. Nobody uses it, so migrating it would waste effort. 10 of 200 apps have zero active users. Think of it like…Moving house. Rehost: pack everything and unpack it the same way. Replatform: same furniture, but you use the new building's laundry room instead of your own machine. Refactor: redesign the furniture to fit the new place. Repurchase: sell the old sofa, buy a new one. Retain: leave some things in storage. Retire: throw out what you never use. Relocate: move a whole furnished room in one piece. TipYou don't need hands-on experience with all seven. A company typically picks one main approach, so knowing one well and understanding the rest is enough. Retain and retire are less \"migration methods\" and more team decisions about what not to move. Try it: pick the right R Read the scenario and choose the best strategy. Loading…"},{"h":"Migrating databases safely","id":"s4","t":"Apps usually depend on databases, and data is the riskiest part to move. Two rules: Find the right managed fit. Running your own database takes a lot of effort. If AWS has a managed equivalent, like Amazon RDS for MySQL or PostgreSQL, moving to it hands patching, backups and failover to AWS. This is a classic replatform. Always have a way back. Large databases can lose data or become unreachable mid-migration. Take backups and keep a copy running (on-prem prod or a pre-prod/staging database). If the cloud database has problems, point the application's connection URL back to that copy right away. Tools worth knowingAWS offers dedicated services: AWS Application Migration Service for rehosting servers, AWS Database Migration Service (DMS) for copying databases with minimal downtime (it can keep source and target in sync until cutover), and AWS Migration Hub for tracking progress across tools. Interview angleStructure your answer: \"We prepared by assessing the architecture (and split the monolith), planned waves from least to most critical and chose a strategy per group, mostly rehost with some replatform to RDS. Each wave we migrated with Terraform and scripts, then monitored for a month with CloudWatch dashboards before the next. At the end we measured cost savings and opened optimisation tasks.\" If you didn't experience something, say so: \"I was on the project for six months and didn't handle that part, but I'd approach it like this…\""},{"h":"Check yourself","id":"s5","t":"Which phases repeat for every wave of applications? Preparation and planning happen once; migrate + monitor loop per wave; optimization comes at the end. You move an app unchanged, but swap its self-managed MySQL server for Amazon RDS. Which R is that? A small change to use a managed AWS service, without redesigning the app, is replatforming. A bank keeps its mainframe payment systems on-premises while everything else moves to AWS. Which strategy applies to the mainframe? Keeping an application where it is, on purpose, is \"retain\"."},{"h":"Key takeaways","id":"s6","t":"A migration is a project: prepare → plan → (migrate → monitor) per wave → optimize. Move apps in waves, from least to most critical, and monitor each wave before the next. The 7 Rs: rehost, replatform, refactor, relocate, repurchase, retain, retire. Rehost, replatform and refactor are the most common. Databases need extra care: prefer managed services like RDS, and always keep a backup and a fallback copy. In interviews, describe the process and your part in it, not only \"lift and shift\"."}],"n":30,"slug":"30-cloud-migration"},{"title":"How to Land an AWS DevOps Job","lead":"Five practical tips for getting hired: what order to learn things in, where certifications fit, how to read job descriptions, how to pick a course and why hands-on practice matters most.","sections":[{"h":"","id":"","t":"Many people study AWS for months and still struggle in interviews. Usually they don't lack effort. They learn things in the wrong order, chase the wrong goals, or only watch and never build. The five tips below fix those problems. The recommended order. Certifications come last, and the job market decides what you focus on."},{"h":"Tip 1: Start with DevOps, not with AWS","id":"s1","t":"A common mistake is to open the AWS console on day one and work through service after service. Without DevOps basics, AWS feels abstract. You create a VPC or an EKS cluster without knowing what you'll run on it or why. Many people find it too hard and give up. Get a broad understanding of the popular DevOps tools first: Linux and shell scripting: almost every server you touch runs Linux. (See Lesson 35 for a starter script.) Git: version control for all code and configuration. Docker and Kubernetes: how modern apps are packaged and run. Terraform and Ansible: creating and configuring infrastructure with code. Then move on to AWS. AWS services will make more sense once you know what problems they solve. EKS is \"Kubernetes that AWS runs for you\", and CodePipeline is \"a CI/CD tool built into AWS\". TipYou may need AWS early anyway, for example to launch a Linux virtual machine if your laptop runs Windows. That's fine. Using one EC2 instance as a practice box is different from trying to master AWS first. For how the lessons in this course connect, see Lesson 1: Course Roadmap."},{"h":"Tip 2: Certifications come last","id":"s2","t":"Beginners often start by studying for an exam such as Cloud Practitioner or Solutions Architect Associate. Certifications are not useless. But they check knowledge you already have, and they can't replace building that knowledge. A better order: Learn the concepts, tools and technologies. Do the demos and practicals yourself. Study interview questions and prepare for interviews. If you have time, take a certification to test what you know. Think of it like…A driving test. Passing it shows you can drive, but you learn to drive by driving, not by memorising the test. An interviewer cares whether you can do the job."},{"h":"Tip 3: Study job descriptions","id":"s3","t":"DevOps and AWS are both huge. You can't learn everything, so let the job market tell you what matters. Spend 15–20 minutes a day reading job descriptions (JDs) instead of only scrolling your feed. Collect many JDs (around 100 is a good target) for the roles you want. Write down the tools and skills that keep coming up. AI chat tools can help you summarise them. Compare that list with what you know. Every gap is your next thing to study. Don't copy someone else's path just because a LinkedIn post said a certain certificate got them hired. Base your preparation on the jobs you're applying for. Try it: job description gap finder Paste one or more job descriptions below, or load the sample. The tool finds common DevOps/AWS skills in the text. Tap a skill to mark it as \"I know this\", and the gap list shows what to study next. Results appear here."},{"h":"Tip 4: Pick the right course, and follow it in order","id":"s4","t":"There are courses on YouTube, Udemy and many other platforms. Look for courses that are built around jobs: real projects and the questions interviewers ask, not trivia or exam tricks. When you have built the same kind of project an interviewer asks about, you can connect their question to something you've done and answer with confidence. Once you choose a course, follow its order. Lessons build on each other. If you skip a long video because it looks boring, you may miss a concept that the next lesson depends on. Watch outJumping between five courses at once usually means finishing none of them. Pick one good path and finish it."},{"h":"Tip 5: Do the practicals","id":"s5","t":"This tip matters most, and people skip it most often. Watching videos and reading theory feels productive, but it doesn't give you real experience. Learners who only watch often say: \"I don't understand real-time troubleshooting.\" Troubleshooting comes from hands-on work: When you do the demo yourself, things break: wrong region, missing IAM permission, a port not open, a typo in YAML. When things break, you investigate and fix them. Real DevOps engineers do exactly this every day. Each fix becomes a story you can tell in an interview: \"I hit this error, here's how I found the cause, and here's how I fixed it.\" This course has many hands-on lessons, such as the production VPC project, CI with CodeBuild, an e-commerce app on EKS and EKS with Terraform. If you build all of them yourself, you'll have plenty of real problems to talk about. Interview angleKeep a short \"issues log\" while you practise: the error, the cause and the fix. Read it before interviews. Troubleshooting stories show real experience better than any definition you can recite. For question practice, see Lesson 29: AWS Interview Q&A."},{"h":"Quick summary","id":"s6","t":"Don't Do instead Start with AWS services cold Learn DevOps basics first (Linux, Git, Docker, Kubernetes, Terraform), then AWS Start with certifications Concepts → practicals → interview prep → certification (optional) Follow whatever's trending on social media Read JDs, list the common skills, close your gaps Skip lessons or hop between courses Pick a job-focused course and follow its order Only watch videos Do every demo; break things and fix them"},{"h":"Check yourself","id":"s7","t":"Where do certifications best fit in your preparation? A certification checks what you know. Build the knowledge first with concepts, practicals and interview prep, then use the exam to test yourself. What's the best way to decide which tools to focus on? Job descriptions show what employers want right now. List the common skills and compare them with what you know. Why are hands-on practicals so important? Real problems only show up when you build things yourself. Fixing them gives you troubleshooting skills and stories for interviews."},{"h":"Key takeaways","id":"s8","t":"Learn DevOps fundamentals (Linux, shell, Git, Docker, Kubernetes, Terraform/Ansible) before diving deep into AWS. Certifications check knowledge; take them last, not first. Read job descriptions every day, list the common skills, and study your gaps. Choose a job-focused course and follow it in order without skipping. Do every practical yourself. Breaking and fixing things builds troubleshooting skill and interview stories."}],"n":31,"slug":"31-crack-devops-job"},{"title":"Three-Tier Architecture on AWS","lead":"Most apps you use every day are split into three layers: the screen you see, the logic behind it, and the database that stores the data. This lesson explains that split and how a classic EC2-based version is built on AWS.","sections":[{"h":"What is a \"tier\"?","id":"s1","t":"A tier is a separate layer of an application that has one job and usually runs on its own servers. Follow a shopper on an online store: The user opens the site in a browser and sees a web page: the user interface (UI), also called the front end. They log in, search, and click a product. The UI does not hold product details. It sends a request to the back end, an application written in Java, Python, Go, etc. The back end checks the request and decides what data is needed. The back end asks the database, which stores the product name, price, description and images. The database answers the back end, and the back end sends the result to the front end, which shows it to the user. Tier 1: Presentation The front end / UI. Runs in the browser and on web servers. Shows pages and collects input. Tier 2: Application (logic) The back end. Business rules: login checks, pricing, cart, orders. Sits between the UI and the data. Tier 3: Data The database. Stores users, products, orders. Only the logic tier talks to it. Think of it like…A restaurant. The dining room (presentation) is where guests order. The kitchen (logic) does the work. The pantry (data) holds the ingredients. Guests never walk into the pantry; the kitchen fetches what's needed. One, two or three tiers? Type Layers Example One-tier Everything in one place A static website: just HTML pages, no back end or database Two-tier Front end + back end, no database A web calculator (the UI sends \"2 + 2\", the back end returns 4), or the 2048 game deployed in Lesson 24: EKS Three-tier Front end + back end + database Online shops, social media, banking apps: almost every real app"},{"h":"Why split an app into tiers?","id":"s2","t":"Security: the database is never reachable from the internet. Each tier only accepts traffic from the tier in front of it. Independent scaling: if the web tier is busy but the app tier isn't, add web servers only. Availability: each tier runs in at least two Availability Zones, so one data-center failure doesn't take the app down. Separate teams and changes: front-end developers can ship a UI change without touching the database."},{"h":"Building it on AWS (EC2 version)","id":"s3","t":"Here is the classic layout, from the outside in. A VPC for the project. One AWS account may host many projects, so give this app its own isolated network, e.g. 172.16.0.0/16. (VPCs and CIDR) Subnets per tier, in two AZs. A public subnet for the internet-facing load balancer, and private subnets for the web tier, the app tier and the database. Spreading across two Availability Zones (e.g. us-east-1a and us-east-1b) keeps the app alive if one zone fails. Web tier: EC2 instances in an Auto Scaling Group, which keeps the right number of servers running, adds more under load, removes them when quiet, and spreads them across AZs. (Auto Scaling Groups) App tier: a second Auto Scaling Group for the back-end servers, with an internal load balancer in front of it that only the web tier can reach. Data tier: Amazon RDS, a managed database service (MySQL, PostgreSQL…). Run it as Multi-AZ: a primary database plus a standby copy in another AZ that takes over automatically if the primary fails. The front door: users reach the app through Route 53 (DNS), optionally CloudFront (a CDN that caches content near users), and then a public Application Load Balancer. Try it: explore the tiers Tap any box in the diagram to see what it does and which AWS services it uses. Tap a tier above. Who may talk to whom? Each tier only accepts traffic from the layer directly in front of it. Pick a source and a destination. From To Result appears here. How a request flows User → Route 53 resolves the domain → CloudFront (cached content served from the edge) → public ALB → one web server → internal ALB → one app server → RDS primary. The response travels back the same way. Locking it down with security groups The usual trick is security-group chaining: instead of allowing IP ranges, each tier's security group allows traffic only from the security group of the tier in front of it. Security group Allows inbound from Public ALB Internet (0.0.0.0/0) on 80/443 Web servers Public ALB's security group only Internal ALB Web servers' security group only App servers Internal ALB's security group only RDS App servers' security group only, on the DB port (e.g. 3306) How security groups work (inbound rules, stateful replies) is covered in Lesson 7: Security Groups & NACLs. Private servers still reach the internet for updates through a NAT gateway. Going further: disaster recovery Larger setups copy the whole stack into a second region. If an entire region has an outage, Route 53 can send users to the backup region. Multi-AZ protects against a data-center failure; multi-region protects against a regional one. TipBuild a one-tier app (a static site) and a two-tier app before this one. A full three-tier build has many parts: VPC, subnets, two ALBs, two ASGs, RDS, Route 53 and CloudFront. It's much less scary when you've already built the smaller pieces."},{"h":"When to talk about this in an interview","id":"s4","t":"This EC2-based design is a classic, but many companies now run apps on containers (Kubernetes / EKS) or serverless instead of fleets of EC2 instances. Match your story to the job: JD mentions EC2, ASG, VMs Describe this architecture: the VPC, subnets per tier, ASGs in two AZs, internal and public ALBs, Multi-AZ RDS. JD mentions containers / Kubernetes Describe the three tiers running as microservices on EKS instead. See Lesson 33. Interview angleDon't mix the two stories. Saying \"we ran everything on Kubernetes\" and then describing fleets of EC2 web servers in Auto Scaling Groups makes it sound like you haven't worked on either for real."},{"h":"Check yourself","id":"s5","t":"A web calculator has a front end and a back end that does the math, but no database. What is it? Front end + back end without a data layer is a two-tier application. Which tier should be allowed to connect to the RDS database? The data tier's security group allows only the app tier's security group. The front end never talks to the database directly. What does running RDS as Multi-AZ give you? Multi-AZ keeps a synchronized standby in a second Availability Zone for automatic failover. It's about availability, not speed or public access."},{"h":"Key takeaways","id":"s6","t":"Three tiers = presentation (UI) + application (logic) + data (database). No database means two-tier. On AWS: a dedicated VPC, a public ALB in public subnets, web and app tiers in private subnets with Auto Scaling Groups, and RDS in its own subnets. Spread every tier across at least two AZs; use Multi-AZ RDS. For disaster recovery, copy the stack to a second region. Each tier only accepts traffic from the tier in front of it (security-group chaining). The database is never internet-facing. In interviews, use this EC2 design for VM-focused roles and an EKS/container design for Kubernetes roles."}],"n":32,"slug":"32-three-tier-architecture"},{"title":"Project: E-Commerce App on EKS","lead":"Deploy Robot Shop, a demo online store made of many microservices and several databases, onto Amazon EKS. You'll see how a three-tier app maps to Kubernetes, why microservices exist, and which extra AWS plumbing a real cluster needs.","sections":[{"h":"","id":"","t":"This is a hands-on project. The three-tier idea is explained in Lesson 32, and Kubernetes objects (pods, deployments, services, ingress), OIDC and the AWS Load Balancer Controller are covered in Lesson 24: EKS. This page focuses on what's new."},{"h":"The project: Robot Shop","id":"s1","t":"Robot Shop is an open-source demo store (originally built by IBM's Instana team to show off monitoring) that sells robots and AI products. It behaves like a real shop: register, log in, browse a catalogue, see ratings, add to cart, calculate shipping, pay, and get an order ID. It's a great learning project because: It's split into many small services, each written in a different language (Node.js, Java, Python, PHP, Go). You get to see a Dockerfile for each kind of app. It uses several data stores: MongoDB, MySQL, Redis and RabbitMQ. The flow (catalogue → cart → shipping → payment) is the same one Amazon or Flipkart uses, so the high-level design carries over to real shops. Its three tiers: the presentation tier is the web UI (AngularJS served by Nginx), the logic tier is the set of microservices, and the data tier is the databases."},{"h":"Monolith vs microservices","id":"s2","t":"Catalogue, ratings, cart, payment and shipping all need their own code. Developers can package it in two ways: Monolith All features in one application / one binary. Simple at first, but a change to the catalogue can break payments, so QA has to re-test everything (regression testing) on every release. Microservices Each feature is a separate service with its own API, deployed on its own. The catalogue team changes and tests only the catalogue. Large companies run hundreds or thousands of these. Each microservice can use the language that suits it best. A company might write 998 services in Go and two in Python. Robot Shop uses many languages on purpose, for teaching. In a real company each service would usually live in its own Git repository; Robot Shop keeps them in one repo because it's small. Think of it like…A food court instead of one giant kitchen. Each stall (service) has its own staff and menu. If the pizza stall changes its recipe, the noodle stall keeps cooking unaffected. Try it: Robot Shop microservices map Tap a box to see what it does, its language, and what it talks to. Or press Follow an order to step through a purchase. Tap a component above. That adds up to 12 components: web, 7 microservices, 2 databases, Redis and RabbitMQ. Each one gets its own Kubernetes Deployment (or StatefulSet) and Service. Why Redis for the cart? If you add an item, log out and come back days later, the item should still be there. Keeping the cart only in the app's own memory would lose it whenever the pod restarts. Redis is an in-memory data store: very fast, and it can save its data to disk. Run it as a StatefulSet with a persistent volume and the cart survives restarts. (Caching in general: Lesson 39.) Why two databases? MongoDB (document DB) and MySQL (relational DB) are both used so you can practise deploying each kind on Kubernetes. A real app could use just one. Why RabbitMQ? It's a message queue. When you pay, the payment service drops the order into a queue and returns right away. The dispatch service picks orders up in the background. If dispatch is slow or down, orders wait in the queue instead of being lost."},{"h":"What's new compared with the 2048 game (Lesson 24)","id":"s3","t":"Topic 2048 game (L24) Robot Shop (this lesson) App shape One container, two-tier 12 components, three-tier, microservices Worker nodes Fargate (serverless pods) EC2 nodes Storage None (stateless) Persistent volumes on EBS via the EBS CSI driver Packaging Plain YAML manifests A Helm chart Exposure Ingress → ALB Same: Ingress → ALB (via the AWS Load Balancer Controller) Why EC2 nodes instead of Fargate? Two reasons. The Redis pod is given the extra Linux privilege NET_ADMIN, which Fargate doesn't allow, and Fargate pods can't mount EBS volumes. Stateful workloads like this need EC2 nodes. Persistent storage: StatefulSet → PVC → EBS A normal pod loses its files when it's replaced. Redis needs its data to survive, so it runs as a StatefulSet, a Kubernetes object for stateful apps that gives each pod a stable name and its own disk. The pieces: PersistentVolumeClaim (PVC): the pod's request: \"I need 1 GiB of disk.\" StorageClass: describes how to make that disk (on AWS: an EBS volume of type gp2/gp3). EBS CSI driver: the add-on that actually calls AWS to create the EBS volume and attach it to the node. Without it, the PVC stays Pending forever. The EBS CSI driver needs IAM permission to create volumes. It gets it through an IAM role linked to its Kubernetes service account. Helm in one minute Helm is a package manager for Kubernetes. A Helm chart is a folder with: Chart.yaml: the chart's name and version. values.yaml: settings you might change, such as the image repository and tag. templates/: the Kubernetes YAML files (a Deployment + Service per component), with placeholders filled in from values.yaml. Why bother? Robot Shop has 12+ manifests that all pull images from the same repository. To switch every image to a new repo or version, you change one line in values.yaml instead of editing 12 files. One helm install deploys everything."},{"h":"Step-by-step deployment","id":"s4","t":"Prerequisites: kubectl, eksctl, helm and the AWS CLI installed and configured. Start the cluster first, because it takes 15–20 minutes. Create the cluster (EC2 nodes). eksctl create cluster --name demo-cluster-robot-shop --region us-east-1 If the default region of your AWS CLI differs from the cluster's region, later commands fail with confusing \"not found\" errors. Run aws configure and set the same region, or pass --region everywhere. Connect the cluster to IAM (OIDC provider). This lets Kubernetes service accounts use IAM roles, so pods like the load balancer controller and the EBS CSI driver can call AWS APIs. export cluster_name=demo-cluster-robot-shop oidc_id=$(aws eks describe-cluster --name $cluster_name \\ --query \"cluster.identity.oidc.issuer\" --output text | cut -d '/' -f 5) aws iam list-open-id-connect-providers | grep $oidc_id # empty = not created yet eksctl utils associate-iam-oidc-provider --cluster $cluster_name --approve Install the AWS Load Balancer Controller. It watches Ingress objects and creates ALBs for them. Create the IAM policy, a service account linked to an IAM role, then install with Helm: curl -O https://raw.githubusercontent.com/kubernetes-sigs/aws-load-balancer-controller/v2.5.4/docs/install/iam_policy.json aws iam create-policy --policy-name AWSLoadBalancerControllerIAMPolicy \\ --policy-document file://iam_policy.json eksctl create iamserviceaccount --cluster=$cluster_name --namespace=kube-system \\ --name=aws-load-balancer-controller --role-name AmazonEKSLoadBalancerControllerRole \\ --attach-policy-arn=arn:aws:iam::<ACCOUNT_ID>:policy/AWSLoadBalancerControllerIAMPolicy \\ --approve helm repo add eks https://aws.github.io/eks-charts helm repo update eks helm install aws-load-balancer-controller eks/aws-load-balancer-controller -n kube-system \\ --set clusterName=$cluster_name --set serviceAccount.create=false \\ --set serviceAccount.name=aws-load-balancer-controller \\ --set region=us-east-1 --set vpcId=<VPC_ID> kubectl get deployment -n kube-system aws-load-balancer-controller Find the VPC ID under your cluster's Networking tab. Wait until the controller pods are running before moving on. It installs in kube-system because one controller watches all namespaces. Install the EBS CSI driver add-on, so Redis (and the databases) can get EBS volumes: eksctl create iamserviceaccount --name ebs-csi-controller-sa --namespace kube-system \\ --cluster $cluster_name --role-name AmazonEKS_EBS_CSI_DriverRole --role-only \\ --attach-policy-arn arn:aws:iam::aws:policy/service-role/AmazonEBSCSIDriverPolicy \\ --approve eksctl create addon --name aws-ebs-csi-driver --cluster $cluster_name \\ --service-account-role-arn arn:aws:iam::<ACCOUNT_ID>:role/AmazonEKS_EBS_CSI_DriverRole --force Deploy Robot Shop with Helm. From the project's EKS/helm folder: kubectl create ns robot-shop helm install robot-shop --namespace robot-shop . kubectl get pods -n robot-shop # wait until all pods are Running (can take 5–10 min) Expose the web UI with an Ingress. The web Service listens on port 8080; the Ingress tells the controller to put an internet-facing ALB in front of it: apiVersion: networking.k8s.io/v1 kind: Ingress metadata: name: robot-shop namespace: robot-shop annotations: alb.ingress.kubernetes.io/scheme: internet-facing alb.ingress.kubernetes.io/target-type: ip spec: ingressClassName: alb rules: - http: paths: - path: / pathType: Prefix backend: service: name: web port: number: 8080 kubectl apply -f ingress.yaml kubectl get ingress -n robot-shop In the EC2 console under Load Balancers, wait for the new ALB to change from Provisioning to Active, then open its DNS name in the browser. Test every workflow: register, log in, browse, rate, add to cart, ship, pay, get an order ID. Clean up. EC2 nodes, EBS volumes and ALBs cost money: eksctl delete cluster --name $cluster_name --region us-east-1 Watch outeksctl create iamserviceaccount works through a CloudFormation stack. If it fails, a retry will keep failing until you delete the failed stack in the CloudFormation console. Also, commands copied from Markdown sometimes get \"smart\" dashes; if a flag is rejected, retype the --. TipA Service of type LoadBalancer also exposes an app, but you get one load balancer per service. Ingress + ALB controller is the more common approach: one ALB can route to many services by path or host."},{"h":"What about CI/CD?","id":"s5","t":"Once you understand the architecture and the Dockerfiles, a pipeline is straightforward. The key rule: one CI/CD pipeline per microservice. Robot Shop would need about 12. One pipeline for everything defeats the purpose of deploying independently. Likewise, a real setup would usually have a Helm chart per service rather than one big chart. Practise by building one pipeline, for example for the Go dispatch or Python payment service. (CI/CD concepts) Interview angle\"Why is Redis a StatefulSet, and how does it get storage on EKS?\" Answer: a StatefulSet gives stable identity and a dedicated volume. The PVC is fulfilled by a StorageClass backed by the EBS CSI driver, which uses an IAM role (via OIDC/IRSA) to create and attach the EBS volume."},{"h":"Check yourself","id":"s6","t":"Why did this project use EC2 worker nodes instead of Fargate? Fargate pods can't mount EBS volumes or get extra privileges like NET_ADMIN. Ingress works fine with Fargate; Lesson 24 used it. A PVC for Redis stays in Pending forever. What's the most likely missing piece? Without the EBS CSI driver, nothing creates the EBS volume the StorageClass asks for, so the claim is never fulfilled. What is the main benefit of microservices over a monolith? Independent deployment is the point. Services can share a language or not; that's up to the team."},{"h":"Key takeaways","id":"s7","t":"Robot Shop = web UI (presentation) + 7 microservices (logic) + MongoDB, MySQL, Redis, RabbitMQ (data): a realistic three-tier e-commerce app. Microservices are deployed independently, so a change to one feature doesn't force re-testing the whole app. Stateful pods need EC2 nodes, a StatefulSet, and the EBS CSI driver to turn PVCs into EBS volumes. Setup order: cluster → OIDC → ALB controller → EBS CSI driver → helm install → Ingress → test → delete the cluster. Helm keeps shared settings (image repo, versions) in values.yaml. In production, use one pipeline (and usually one chart) per microservice."}],"n":33,"slug":"33-eks-ecommerce-project"},{"title":"Project: EKS + VPC with Terraform","lead":"Use community Terraform modules to build a production-style VPC and an EKS cluster with auto-scaling worker nodes in about 55 resources and one command. You'll also learn how teams store Terraform state safely so two people can't break it at the same time.","sections":[{"h":"","id":"","t":"New to Terraform? Providers, resources, variables, state and plan/apply are explained in Lesson 26: Terraform. EKS itself is covered in Lesson 24."},{"h":"What we're building","id":"s1","t":"Terraform runs on your machine and creates everything inside your AWS account. The node group keeps 2 nodes normally and can grow to 6 under load."},{"h":"Step 1–3: Install and connect","id":"s2","t":"Install the AWS CLI (from the AWS \"Install or update the AWS CLI\" page for your OS), then check: aws --version. Install Terraform (HashiCorp's install page: package manager on Linux, Homebrew on Mac, Chocolatey or a zip on Windows), then check: terraform version. Connect Terraform to AWS by running aws configure and entering an access key, secret key, default region and output format (json). This saves the keys in ~/.aws/credentials. Terraform's AWS provider reads that same file, so configuring the CLI also configures Terraform. Watch outUse an IAM user or role with the right permissions, not root access keys, in any real account. Delete demo access keys when you're done. (IAM, AWS CLI & access keys)"},{"h":"How to structure the Terraform files","id":"s3","t":"You could put everything in one main.tf, but a 500-line file is hard to read and fix. Split by purpose, so the person fixing a security-group rule opens security-groups.tf and nothing else: File What's inside versions.tf Required Terraform version and providers (aws, random, …) variables.tf Inputs: AWS region, Kubernetes version, VPC CIDR vpc.tf The aws provider, a random suffix for the cluster name, and the VPC module eks-cluster.tf The EKS module and its managed node group security-groups.tf Extra security group with inbound/outbound rules for the nodes outputs.tf Values printed after apply (cluster name, endpoint, region…)"},{"h":"Terraform modules","id":"s4","t":"A module is a reusable package of Terraform code. You call it, pass in a few inputs, and it creates all the resources inside. Think of it as a function: you don't rewrite the code for a VPC (subnets, route tables, internet gateway, NAT…) every time; you call the VPC module with your settings. Think of it like…A flat-pack furniture kit. You don't cut wood and design joints yourself; you pick the \"wardrobe\" kit and choose the size and colour. A module is the kit; the inputs are your choices. Why modules are a best practice: No duplicate code. If project X and project Y both need \"S3 bucket + EC2\", write it once as a module and call it twice. Fix once, fix everywhere. When a security update changes how something should be built, you update the module and every caller benefits. Callers only pass variables, so they don't break. Battle-tested code. Common building blocks like VPC and EKS already exist as community modules. Where to look for a module, in order: The Terraform Registry (registry.terraform.io), e.g. the community-maintained terraform-aws-modules collection, which has VPC, EKS, S3, RDS and more. Your organisation's own modules (often in a Git repo), written by another team for the same need. If neither exists, write the code yourself, then consider turning it into a module for the next person. The VPC module Every module call has a source (where the module comes from) and a version. Always pin the version. Without it you get whatever is newest, and a breaking change could hit you without warning. For a new project, start with the latest version. provider \"aws\" { region = var.aws_region } locals { cluster_name = \"abhi-eks-${random_string.suffix.result}\" } resource \"random_string\" \"suffix\" { length = 8 special = false } module \"vpc\" { source = \"terraform-aws-modules/vpc/aws\" version = \"5.7.0\" name = \"abhi-eks-vpc\" cidr = var.vpc_cidr azs = data.aws_availability_zones.available.names private_subnets = [\"10.0.1.0/24\", \"10.0.2.0/24\", \"10.0.3.0/24\"] public_subnets = [\"10.0.4.0/24\", \"10.0.5.0/24\", \"10.0.6.0/24\"] enable_nat_gateway = true single_nat_gateway = true enable_dns_hostnames = true enable_dns_support = true tags = { \"kubernetes.io/cluster/${local.cluster_name}\" = \"shared\" } } data \"aws_availability_zones\" \"available\" {} How do you know which inputs exist? Open the module's page in the registry and read its Inputs, Outputs and Examples. Some inputs are required (like name and cidr), most are optional. With practice you'll know which ones matter. The random_string adds an 8-character suffix so every cluster name is unique. The EKS module module \"eks\" { source = \"terraform-aws-modules/eks/aws\" version = \"~> 20.0\" cluster_name = local.cluster_name cluster_version = var.kubernetes_version vpc_id = module.vpc.vpc_id subnet_ids = module.vpc.private_subnets eks_managed_node_group_defaults = { ami_type = \"AL2_x86_64\" instance_types = [\"t3.medium\"] vpc_security_group_ids = [aws_security_group.all_worker_mgmt.id] } eks_managed_node_groups = { node_group = { min_size = 2 desired_size = 2 max_size = 6 } } } Note how modules plug together: module.vpc.vpc_id and module.vpc.private_subnets are outputs of the VPC module fed into the EKS module. Terraform works out the order: VPC first, then EKS. An EKS managed node group means AWS creates an Auto Scaling Group and launch template for the worker nodes; you set min/desired/max. (The module's examples also show Fargate profiles, Karpenter and self-managed node groups.) Variables and outputs # variables.tf variable \"aws_region\" { default = \"us-east-1\" } variable \"kubernetes_version\" { default = \"1.29\" } variable \"vpc_cidr\" { default = \"10.0.0.0/16\" } # outputs.tf output \"cluster_name\" { value = module.eks.cluster_name } output \"cluster_endpoint\" { value = module.eks.cluster_endpoint } output \"region\" { value = var.aws_region } Outputs are printed at the end of apply, so you don't have to dig through the console to find the cluster name or endpoint."},{"h":"Step 4: Run it","id":"s5","t":"terraform init # downloads providers and modules into .terraform/ terraform plan # preview: about 55 resources to add terraform apply # type \"yes\" (or add -auto-approve); takes roughly 10-15 min Read the init output. It says which backend it's using (local here), downloads each module into .terraform/modules/ (you'll find eks and vpc), and downloads every provider listed in versions.tf into .terraform/providers/. Unused providers left in versions.tf still get downloaded, so remove them. Read the plan. You'll see the VPC, subnets, route tables, internet gateway, NAT gateway, security groups, IAM roles, the cluster and the node group. Reading a plan line by line is one of the best ways to learn what a module actually builds. Getting access to the cluster When you open the new cluster in the console, the Compute and Resources tabs may say you have no access, even as root. EKS uses access entries to decide who can see inside the cluster. Under the cluster's Access tab: Click Create access entry and choose your IAM principal (user or role ARN). Attach the AmazonEKSClusterAdminPolicy access policy. Refresh: the nodes (2 by default) and resources now appear. You can also do this in Terraform (e.g. enable_cluster_creator_admin_permissions = true or an access_entries block in EKS module v20). Then connect kubectl: aws eks update-kubeconfig --name <cluster-name> --region us-east-1 kubectl get nodes Watch outNAT gateways, EKS control planes and EC2 nodes are not free. When you're done, run terraform destroy."},{"h":"Remote state and locking","id":"s6","t":"This demo uses the local backend: terraform.tfstate sits on your laptop. That's fine for learning, but in a team it causes two problems: Nobody else has the state. A teammate running apply from their machine has an empty state, so Terraform thinks nothing exists and tries to create everything again. Simultaneous applies. Even with a shared state file, two people running apply at the same time can corrupt it or overwrite each other's changes. The usual fix is a remote backend: keep the state in an S3 bucket (shared, versioned, encrypted) and use a lock so only one apply can run at a time. Traditionally the lock is a row in a DynamoDB table: terraform { backend \"s3\" { bucket = \"my-company-terraform-state\" key = \"eks/terraform.tfstate\" region = \"us-east-1\" encrypt = true dynamodb_table = \"terraform-locks\" # table with a string partition key \"LockID\" } } NoteTerraform 1.10+ can also lock using S3 itself (use_lockfile = true), and newer versions mark the DynamoDB option as deprecated. You'll still see S3 + DynamoDB in most existing projects and interviews, and the idea is the same: one writer at a time. Try it: two engineers, one apply Pick a backend, then press Apply for both engineers quickly, one right after the other. See what happens to the state. Asha Idle. Change: add node group \"gpu\" Ben Idle. Change: add security rule 443 Log appears here. Interview angle\"How do you manage Terraform state in a team?\" Remote backend in S3 (versioning + encryption on, public access blocked), state locking (DynamoDB or S3 native lockfile), access limited with IAM, and applies run from a CI/CD pipeline rather than laptops. Also: \"Why use modules?\" Reuse, consistency, and fixing things in one place."},{"h":"Check yourself","id":"s7","t":"Why should you always set version when calling a registry module? Pinning keeps builds repeatable. You upgrade on purpose, after reading the changelog, not by accident. Two engineers run terraform apply at the same moment against an S3 backend with a DynamoDB lock table. What happens? The first apply takes the lock (a LockID row in DynamoDB). The second can't get it and stops. The state lives in S3; DynamoDB only holds the lock. The cluster was created, but the console says you can't see its nodes. What's the fix? EKS access entries control who can see Kubernetes objects. Even root needs one. You can also set this in Terraform."},{"h":"Key takeaways","id":"s8","t":"aws configure stores credentials that Terraform's AWS provider also uses. Split Terraform into files by purpose (vpc, eks, security groups, variables, outputs, versions) for readability. Modules are reusable packages. Use registry or company modules, always pin version, and wire them together via outputs like module.vpc.private_subnets. init → plan (≈55 resources) → apply; then add an EKS access entry and run aws eks update-kubeconfig; finish with terraform destroy. In teams, store state remotely in S3 with locking (DynamoDB or S3 lockfile) so only one apply runs at a time."}],"n":34,"slug":"34-eks-terraform"},{"title":"Shell Scripting: AWS Resource Report","lead":"Write a beginner-friendly shell script that lists the active resources in your AWS account for any region and service, run it on a schedule with cron, and pick up the scripting and security habits interviewers look for.","sections":[{"h":"Why this project?","id":"s1","t":"Interviewers rarely ask \"what's the command to list processes?\" anymore. They ask \"What do you use shell scripts for in your job?\" This project gives you a real answer. Companies care about cost. Forgotten EC2 instances, unattached EBS volumes and old load balancers all cost money. So DevOps engineers often write a script that reports what's running, schedule it with cron to run every day, and (optionally) email the report to managers. Many teams write these in shell; others use Python with boto3. The script works like this: ./aws_resource_list.sh <region> <service> ./aws_resource_list.sh us-east-1 ec2 # running EC2 instances in us-east-1 ./aws_resource_list.sh us-east-1 s3 # S3 buckets ./aws_resource_list.sh us-east-1 ebs # EBS volumes TipAWS has 200+ services, and no company uses all of them. First find out which services your organisation actually uses and support only those. Anything else should get a clear \"invalid service\" message."},{"h":"How the script works","id":"s2","t":"Every check that fails stops the script early with a clear message and a non-zero exit code."},{"h":"Shell basics you'll use","id":"s3","t":"Syntax Meaning #!/bin/bash The shebang: the first line, telling the system to run the file with Bash. $0, $1, $2 Command-line arguments: $0 is the script's own name, $1 the first input (region), $2 the second (service). $# How many arguments were passed. if … then … fi A condition. Blocks end with the word reversed: if → fi. case … in … esac A switch: pick one branch based on a value. Ends with esac (case reversed). exit 1 Stop now with an error code. 0 means success; anything else means failure."},{"h":"The full script","id":"s4","t":"#!/bin/bash ############################################################################### # Author: DevOps Team # Version: v0.0.1 # # Lists active AWS resources in a given region for a given service. # # Supported services: ec2, s3, rds, dynamodb, lambda, ebs, elb, cloudfront, # cloudwatch, sns, sqs, route53, cloudformation, iam, vpc # # Usage: ./aws_resource_list.sh <region> <service> # Example: ./aws_resource_list.sh us-east-1 ec2 ############################################################################### # 1. Check that exactly two arguments were passed if [ $# -ne 2 ]; then echo \"Usage: $0 <region> <service>\" echo \"Example: $0 us-east-1 ec2\" exit 1 fi region=$1 service=$2 # 2. Check that the AWS CLI is installed if ! command -v aws &> /dev/null; then echo \"AWS CLI is not installed. Please install it and try again.\" exit 1 fi # 3. Check that the AWS CLI is configured if [ ! -d ~/.aws ]; then echo \"AWS CLI is not configured. Run 'aws configure' and try again.\" exit 1 fi # 4. Run the right AWS CLI command for the chosen service case $service in ec2) aws ec2 describe-instances --region \"$region\" \\ --filters \"Name=instance-state-name,Values=running\" ;; s3) aws s3api list-buckets ;; rds) aws rds describe-db-instances --region \"$region\" ;; dynamodb) aws dynamodb list-tables --region \"$region\" ;; lambda) aws lambda list-functions --region \"$region\" ;; ebs) aws ec2 describe-volumes --region \"$region\" ;; elb) aws elbv2 describe-load-balancers --region \"$region\" ;; cloudfront) aws cloudfront list-distributions ;; cloudwatch) aws cloudwatch describe-alarms --region \"$region\" ;; sns) aws sns list-topics --region \"$region\" ;; sqs) aws sqs list-queues --region \"$region\" ;; route53) aws route53 list-hosted-zones ;; cloudformation) aws cloudformation describe-stacks --region \"$region\" ;; iam) aws iam list-users ;; vpc) aws ec2 describe-vpcs --region \"$region\" ;; *) echo \"Invalid service: $service\" echo \"Supported: ec2 s3 rds dynamodb lambda ebs elb cloudfront cloudwatch sns sqs route53 cloudformation iam vpc\" exit 1 ;; esac Walking through it Header comments first. Before any code: what the script does, the author (or team) so people know who to ask, a version, supported services, and usage with an example. Start at a low version like v0.0.1, bump it on every change, and move to 1.0.0 once it's stable. In an interview, start by writing this header; it shows good habits. Validate the arguments. If someone runs ./aws_resource_list.sh ec2 and forgets the region, print the usage and exit. Using $0 in the message means it stays correct even if the file is renamed. Whenever a script takes arguments, validate them first. Check the AWS CLI is installed. You could call the AWS APIs directly with curl, but then you'd have to handle request signing, headers and JSON yourself. The AWS CLI makes those API calls for you. (In Python you'd use boto3 instead.) If the CLI is missing on this machine, say so clearly instead of failing with a confusing error. Check it's configured. aws configure creates the ~/.aws folder with your credentials and default region. [ ! -d ~/.aws ] means \"if this directory does not exist\". Pick the command with case. The region stays the same and only the command changes per service. A case statement is cleaner than a long if / elif chain: one readable line per service, and a *) catch-all for invalid input. TipChecking for ~/.aws is a quick heuristic. A stronger check is aws sts get-caller-identity, which actually confirms the credentials work. Also note: S3, IAM, CloudFront and Route 53 are global, so the region doesn't change their results. Interview angle\"How do you remember all those CLI commands?\" It's fine to say: \"I remember the common ones like ec2 describe-instances and s3api list-buckets. For the rest I check the AWS CLI reference.\" That's what working engineers do."},{"h":"Running it on EC2","id":"s5","t":"If you're on Windows, the easiest place to run Bash is a small Ubuntu EC2 instance (launch one, then SSH in). # Install the AWS CLI v2 on Ubuntu sudo apt update sudo apt install -y unzip curl \"https://awscli.amazonaws.com/awscli-exe-linux-x86_64.zip\" -o \"awscliv2.zip\" unzip awscliv2.zip sudo ./aws/install aws --version # Configure it (use an IAM user's access keys, not root) aws configure # Make the script executable and run it chmod +x aws_resource_list.sh ./aws_resource_list.sh us-east-1 ec2 Test the failure paths too: run it with one argument (you should see the usage message), and before installing the CLI (you should see \"AWS CLI is not installed\"). Checking that your error handling works is part of testing a script."},{"h":"Scheduling it with cron","id":"s6","t":"cron is the Linux scheduler. It runs commands at set times. Each line in your crontab has five time fields followed by the command: ┌──────── minute (0-59) │ ┌────── hour (0-23) │ │ ┌──── day of month (1-31) │ │ │ ┌── month (1-12) │ │ │ │ ┌ day of week (0-7, 0 and 7 = Sunday) │ │ │ │ │ 0 21 * * * /home/ubuntu/aws_resource_list.sh us-east-1 ec2 >> /home/ubuntu/ec2_report.log 2>&1 That line runs the report every day at 9 PM and appends the output (and any errors, via 2>&1) to a log file. Edit your crontab with crontab -e and list it with crontab -l. Watch outcron runs with a very short PATH (usually just /usr/bin:/bin). The AWS CLI lives in /usr/local/bin, so the script may say \"AWS CLI is not installed\" under cron even though it works in your terminal. Add PATH=/usr/local/bin:/usr/bin:/bin at the top of the crontab or the script, and always use full paths in cron lines. Try it: cron expression explainer Edit the five fields or pick a preset. Supports *, numbers, lists (1,15), ranges (1-5) and steps (*/15). min hour day month weekday"},{"h":"Securing the script","id":"s7","t":"Other people (developers, managers) may need to run the report, but they shouldn't edit it. Use file permissions. Each digit of chmod is a sum of 4 = read, 2 = write, 1 = execute, given for owner, group and others in that order: Command Owner Group Others Use when chmod 750 rwx (7) r-x (5) none (0) Your team can run it; nobody else can chmod 770 rwx (7) rwx (7) none (0) Your whole DevOps team maintains it chmod 755 rwx (7) r-x (5) r-x (5) Everyone can run it, only you can edit Watch outYou may see advice to use chmod 711 (\"others may only execute\"). That works for compiled programs, but not for shell scripts: Bash must read a script in order to run it, so execute-only users get \"Permission denied\". Give read + execute (5) to anyone who should run it. Either way, never put secrets (access keys, passwords) in a script. Rely on aws configure, IAM roles, or a secrets manager."},{"h":"Check yourself","id":"s8","t":"You run ./aws_resource_list.sh us-east-1 ec2. What is $0? $0 is the script's own name; $1 is the region and $2 is the service. Which crontab schedule runs at 9 PM every day? Minute 0, hour 21. 21 0 means 00:21 (just after midnight), and * 21 runs every minute from 21:00 to 21:59. The script works in your terminal but cron logs \"AWS CLI is not installed\". Most likely cause? cron uses a minimal PATH. Set PATH in the crontab or script, or call /usr/local/bin/aws directly."},{"h":"Key takeaways","id":"s9","t":"A resource-report script (region + service arguments) is a realistic, interview-friendly DevOps automation that helps control cost. Start with a header (author, version, usage), then validate: argument count, CLI installed, CLI configured. Fail early with exit 1. Use the AWS CLI rather than raw API calls, and a case statement to map each service to its command, with a *) catch-all. Schedule with cron (0 21 * * * = daily 9 PM), using full paths and a proper PATH. Lock it down with chmod (e.g. 750 or 755; scripts need read + execute), and never hard-code secrets."}],"n":35,"slug":"35-shell-scripting-project"},{"title":"Reducing Your AWS Bill","lead":"A surprise bill usually means a resource you forgot to delete, often in a region you weren't looking at. Here you'll learn how to read the bill, find every running resource in every region, set a budget that emails you before things get expensive, and the team habits that keep costs down.","sections":[{"h":"Why bills surprise people","id":"s1","t":"You can't get your cloud bill to exactly $0 while you're learning. Every time you create resources, AWS charges for them. What you can do is keep the bill small and predictable. The usual story goes like this: You follow a tutorial and click through the console (the AWS website) to build something. You \"delete everything\" when you're done, but you miss one piece, like a NAT gateway or an EKS cluster. A week or a month later, AWS emails you a bill. That's when you find out something is still running. Cleaning up by hand in the console is easy to get wrong. Resources are spread over many pages and many regions, and some are hidden behind others. The four habits below fix that. The cost-control loop. Budgets warn you, and the bill and Tag Editor show you what to delete."},{"h":"1. Read the bill","id":"s2","t":"Open Billing and Cost Management in the console. The home page shows your month-to-date cost, the total so far this month. Beginners often stop at that one graph, but it doesn't tell you what is costing money. Two places do: Bills Lists this month's charges by service (EC2, EKS, VPC…), and inside each service by region. This is the quickest way to answer \"who is charging me, and where?\" Cost Explorer Charts your costs over time. Group by Service or Region to see which one jumped. It's useful for spotting trends over several months. TipNAT gateway charges are listed under the VPC or EC2 service, not under a service called \"NAT\". If \"VPC\" shows a cost you didn't expect, a NAT gateway or a public IPv4 address is usually the reason."},{"h":"2. Find every resource, in every region","id":"s3","t":"The console only shows one region at a time. If you built something in us-east-1 and are now looking at ap-south-1, the forgotten resource is simply not on your screen. You need a view that covers all regions. Tag Editor (console) Tag Editor is part of the Resource Groups & Tag Editor service. It is meant for adding tags, but it's also the easiest way to list everything you own: Open Resource Groups & Tag Editor → Tag Editor. Regions: choose All regions. Resource types: choose All supported resource types. Tag Editor doesn't support all 200+ AWS services, but it does cover the common ones that usually cost money. Click Search resources. You get one list of everything, with its region. The list will include many things that are free, such as IAM users, security groups, and the default VPC. A VPC by itself costs nothing. The paid parts are what you attach to it, like a NAT gateway, a public IPv4 address, or EC2 instances. Look for the paid items and delete those. Watch outDeleting in the console often fails with a dependency error, for example \"this VPC still has a NAT gateway\". That's useful, because it shows you what depends on what. Delete the dependent resource first, then try again. Beginner ruleUse one region for all your practice work. Once you pick a single region in Tag Editor, the list gets much shorter and nothing hides in another region. AWS Resource Explorer is another option: once it's turned on, it can search resources across all regions from one search box. The same thing from the CLI If you prefer the terminal, set up the AWS CLI with aws configure using access keys from an IAM user. Don't create access keys for the root user. Then use the Resource Groups Tagging API: # everything in one region aws resourcegroupstaggingapi get-resources --region ap-south-1 # save it to a file so you can search it aws resourcegroupstaggingapi get-resources --region ap-south-1 > resources.json # loop over several regions for r in ap-south-1 us-east-1; do echo \"== $r ==\" aws resourcegroupstaggingapi get-resources --region \"$r\" \\ --query 'ResourceTagMappingList[].ResourceARN' --output text done NoteThis API returns resources that are tagged, or were tagged in the past. If a resource has never had a tag, it may not appear. Use Tag Editor or the Bills page to double-check. This is one more reason to tag everything (see best practices below)."},{"h":"3. Try it: hunt for forgotten resources","id":"s4","t":"Try it: forgotten-resource hunt + budget alerts This is a pretend account left over from a few tutorials. Pick a region and click Search resources, like in Tag Editor. Tick the resources you'd delete. The budget meter always counts all regions, because so does your bill. Prices are rough monthly estimates. Region Monthly budget $ Day of month: 20 Search to see resources."},{"h":"4. Guard your wallet with AWS Budgets","id":"s5","t":"Cleaning up fixes today's problem. A budget warns you about tomorrow's. In Billing and Cost Management → Budgets: Zero spend budget emails you as soon as you spend even a cent. That sounds safe, but once you're learning real services (an EKS cluster is never free), it just sends a lot of noise. Monthly cost budget is the practical choice. Set an amount, such as $10, and an email address to notify. With the monthly cost budget template, AWS doesn't wait until you've already gone over. It emails you: Alert For a $10 budget Meaning Actual spend reaches 85% $8.50 Early warning: go and check what's running. Actual spend reaches 100% $10.00 You've hit your limit. Forecasted spend reaches 100% predicted ≥ $10 At the current rate, you'll go over by the end of the month. Watch outA budget only sends alerts. It does not stop or delete anything. You still have to act on the email. (Budgets can be set up with budget actions that apply restrictions, but that's an advanced option.) In a company, the same feature lets you set a budget per team or per project. You can also schedule budget reports that email a regular summary to managers. DevOps and SRE engineers who look after cloud costs set these up as a matter of routine."},{"h":"Best practices for teams","id":"s6","t":"Infrastructure as Code, not clicks Build resources with Terraform, CloudFormation or the AWS CDK, not by hand in the console. Then terraform destroy (or deleting the stack) removes everything it created, in the right order. There are no forgotten NAT gateways and no dependency puzzles to solve. Least privilege Give people only the access they need (IAM). A new developer usually needs read access, not permission to create EKS clusters or IAM users. When they need a resource, they ask the DevOps team or run an approved pipeline (for example, a Jenkins job that can only create an EC2 instance). Tag everything Add tags such as owner, project and env to every resource. Tag Editor and the billing tools can then filter by tag, so you can see which team or project each cost belongs to. Automate stale-resource cleanup Unused EBS volumes, snapshots, FSx file systems and idle EC2 instances add up. A scheduled Lambda function can find them and either email you (SNS) or delete them. That's covered in Lesson 20. AWS Config rules can flag them too. Done well, cost optimization can save a large share of what many companies spend, because stale resources are very common. Got a bill you can't pay?If you're a learner and genuinely forgot to delete something, contact AWS Support. Explain that you're learning and what happened. AWS often waives one-time charges for genuine cases. Don't ignore the bill or try to get around it with new accounts and cards. NoteAzure's resource groups make cleanup simpler: you delete the group and everything inside it goes too. On AWS, IaC stacks are the closest equivalent. That's another reason to use them."},{"h":"Check yourself","id":"s7","t":"Your bill shows EC2 charges, but the EC2 console looks empty. What's the most likely reason? The console shows one region at a time. Use the Bills page (by region) or Tag Editor with \"All regions\" to find it. You create a $10 monthly cost budget. What happens when spending reaches $10? Budgets notify you (at 85%, 100% and a forecast of 100%). They don't shut anything down unless you set up budget actions yourself. Which habit best prevents \"I deleted the VPC but forgot the NAT gateway\"? IaC tracks everything it created and deletes all of it in the correct dependency order."},{"h":"Key takeaways","id":"s8","t":"Use the Bills page and Cost Explorer to see costs by service and region. The month-to-date total alone doesn't tell you much. Tag Editor with All regions + All supported resource types lists your resources in one place. From the CLI, use aws resourcegroupstaggingapi get-resources. Learners: stick to one region. Many items (IAM users, security groups, a plain VPC) are free. NAT gateways, EKS, EC2, load balancers and IPs are not. Create a monthly cost budget to get emails at 85%, 100% and when the forecast passes 100%. Budgets alert you but don't stop anything. Team habits: IaC, least privilege, tagging, and automated cleanup of stale resources."}],"n":36,"slug":"36-reduce-aws-bill"},{"title":"Amazon CodeCatalyst","lead":"CodeCatalyst puts source code, CI/CD workflows and developer tools together in one place. In this lesson you'll learn how it's organized (spaces, projects, blueprints, workflows, dev environments) and build a pipeline that uses the AWS CDK to create an S3 bucket from Python code, with no manual clicks after the first setup.","sections":[{"h":"First: finding the right AWS services for DevOps","id":"s1","t":"AWS has hundreds of services, so a common problem for teams adopting DevOps is choosing which ones to use. AWS's DevOps on AWS hub (part of the AWS Developer Center) helps with that. It has three sections: Learn Beginner blogs and videos, plus a \"choose a solution\" tool. Pick an area and it suggests services. For example, monitoring & logging gives CloudWatch and X-Ray, and platform as a service gives Elastic Beanstalk. Build Problem → solution guides with architecture diagrams and step-by-step instructions, such as \"deploy your first web app\" or \"infrastructure pipelines with CDK and CodeCatalyst\". That second guide is the project on this page. Explore Newer topics, such as how AI fits into DevOps (Bedrock, SageMaker, Amazon Q Developer). Why it helps It's a good place to bookmark when you need a proof of concept quickly: there's a ready-made guide for many common problems."},{"h":"What is CodeCatalyst?","id":"s2","t":"Amazon CodeCatalyst is a unified software development service. Instead of wiring together CodeCommit, CodeBuild and CodePipeline yourself, you get one web app at codecatalyst.aws with repositories, CI/CD, issues and cloud dev environments. It can also connect to code that already lives on GitHub. Need a refresher on CI/CD? See Lesson 14. For what \"infrastructure as code\" means, see Lesson 13: CloudFormation. The building blocks Term What it is Space The top-level container for your company or team. You connect an AWS account to the space (for billing and for deploying), and people join the space. Project One piece of work inside a space, such as an app or a pipeline. It holds repos, workflows, issues and environments. Blueprint A project template. It generates starter code, a workflow and settings for common app types. You can also start from scratch or bring your own code. Workflow The CI/CD pipeline: triggers (when to run) + actions (what to run), saved as YAML in the repo. Environment A deployment target: which AWS account and which IAM role a workflow action uses (e.g. \"non-production\"). Dev Environment A cloud-hosted coding workspace you can open in a browser IDE or connect to from your local IDE, already set up with the project's code. Think of it like…A space is an office building, each project is a room, a blueprint is a furnished-room template, and a workflow is the robot in the room that builds and ships things whenever new code arrives."},{"h":"The AWS CDK in 60 seconds","id":"s3","t":"To create an S3 bucket, you can click buttons in the console. That's fine once, but tiresome if you have to do it many times a week. The AWS Cloud Development Kit (CDK) lets you describe infrastructure in a normal programming language such as Python or TypeScript. Want 10 buckets, or a change to an existing one? Edit the code and run a CDK command. Under the hood, the CDK converts your code into a CloudFormation template and deploys it as a stack. Two commands matter: cdk bootstrap: run once per account and region. It creates a helper stack called CDKToolkit with what the CDK needs to work, such as a staging bucket and IAM roles. cdk deploy: builds your app into a template and creates or updates your stack. # install the CDK CLI (needs Node.js), or: brew install aws-cdk npm install -g aws-cdk cdk --version mkdir cdk-s3-bucket && cd cdk-s3-bucket cdk init app --language python # creates app.py, the stack file, requirements.txt, a venv and a git repo cdk init does most of the setup for you. app.py is the entry point that loads your stack class. You only write the resource code inside the stack class: from aws_cdk import Stack, aws_s3 as s3 from constructs import Construct class CdkS3BucketStack(Stack): def __init__(self, scope: Construct, construct_id: str, **kwargs) -> None: super().__init__(scope, construct_id, **kwargs) s3.Bucket(self, \"DemoBucket\", bucket_name=\"demo-cdk-codecatalyst-12345\") # must be globally unique TipYou don't need to remember this code. The CDK API reference has a ready example for each resource and language (search \"s3 Bucket\", pick Python). A code assistant such as Amazon Q in VS Code can also write it from a prompt like \"generate code for S3 bucket creation with bucket name …\". Push this project to a GitHub repository. Next, CodeCatalyst will run the CDK commands for you."},{"h":"The pipeline we're building","id":"s4","t":"A push to main triggers the workflow. Both actions use the environment's IAM role to work in your AWS account through CloudFormation."},{"h":"Building it, step by step","id":"s5","t":"Connect CodeCatalyst to AWS. Sign in at codecatalyst.aws, create (or open) a space and connect your AWS account. It then appears under the space's AWS accounts. You can reach this from the console too: search \"CodeCatalyst\" → Spaces. Create a project. Choose Bring your own code, authorize GitHub, and pick the repo (e.g. aws-infra-pipeline). Name the project, e.g. s3-bucket-creation. The other choices are Start from scratch or a Blueprint. Create a workflow. Go to CI/CD → Workflows → Create workflow and pick the repo and branch. You can edit it visually, which is easiest for beginners, or as YAML. Add a trigger: on push to the main branch. Add the \"AWS CDK bootstrap\" action. Configure the compute (EC2 runner), create an environment (e.g. bucket-creation, non-production) linked to your AWS account and an IAM role (CodeCatalyst can create one), the region (us-east-1), and the CDK CLI version (match your local one). Add the \"AWS CDK deploy\" action. Make it depend on the bootstrap action, use the same environment and region, and set the stack name to the one in app.py (e.g. CdkS3BucketStack). If the name doesn't match, the deploy fails. Commit the workflow. It's saved to the repo as YAML, and the first run starts. Here is roughly what the YAML view shows (simplified. The visual editor fills in exact action versions and account details): Name: s3-bucket-workflow SchemaVersion: \"1.0\" Triggers: - Type: PUSH Branches: - main Actions: CDKBootstrap: Identifier: aws/cdk-bootstrap@v2 Inputs: Sources: [WorkflowSource] Compute: Type: EC2 Environment: Name: bucket-creation Connections: - Name: \"111122223333\" # your AWS account Role: CodeCatalystWorkflowDevelopmentRole Configuration: Region: us-east-1 CdkCliVersion: \"2.150.0\" CDKDeploy: Identifier: aws/cdk-deploy@v2 DependsOn: [CDKBootstrap] Inputs: Sources: [WorkflowSource] Compute: Type: EC2 Environment: Name: bucket-creation Connections: - Name: \"111122223333\" Role: CodeCatalystWorkflowDevelopmentRole Configuration: StackName: CdkS3BucketStack Region: us-east-1 CdkCliVersion: \"2.150.0\" Try it: step through a workflow run Click Next to follow one run, from git push to a new S3 bucket."},{"h":"What you see after the run","id":"s6","t":"The first run takes a couple of minutes because CodeCatalyst first starts a runner, the temporary machine that executes the actions. In CloudFormation you now see two stacks: CDKToolkit (from bootstrap) and your bucket stack (from deploy). The bucket appears in that stack's Resources tab. In S3 you see the new bucket. The CDK used CloudFormation to create it. From now on, any change you push to main (another bucket, versioning, tags…) is deployed automatically. Nobody needs to touch the console. TipTo clean up, delete the bucket stack in CloudFormation (or run cdk destroy). Leftover stacks and buckets are exactly the kind of forgotten resources covered in Lesson 36. Check availabilityCodeCatalyst stopped accepting new customers on 7 November 2025. Existing users can keep using it, but AWS only plans security, availability and performance fixes, no new features. For a new project, AWS points to CodeBuild, CodePipeline and CodeDeploy instead. The ideas here (IaC in a repo, a pipeline that runs cdk bootstrap/cdk deploy, environments with IAM roles) work the same way in CodePipeline, GitHub Actions or Jenkins. Interview angle\"How would you make infrastructure changes repeatable and reviewable?\" Keep IaC (CDK, Terraform or CloudFormation) in Git, run it from a pipeline that has its own IAM role, and trigger it on merge to main. Then nobody needs broad console access."},{"h":"Check yourself","id":"s7","t":"What does cdk bootstrap do? Bootstrap prepares the account and region once. cdk deploy then creates your actual resources. In CodeCatalyst, what tells a workflow action which AWS account and IAM role to use? An environment links an AWS account connection and an IAM role, plus a type such as non-production. The CDK deploy action fails with \"stack not found\". What should you check first? The deploy action's stack name has to match the stack your CDK app defines."},{"h":"Key takeaways","id":"s8","t":"CodeCatalyst brings repos, CI/CD workflows, issues and dev environments together, organized as space → project. Projects can start from a blueprint, from scratch, or from your own GitHub code. A workflow = triggers + actions, stored as YAML. Environments give actions an AWS account and IAM role. The AWS CDK writes infrastructure in Python/TypeScript and deploys it through CloudFormation. Run cdk bootstrap once, then cdk deploy. The DevOps on AWS hub (Learn / Build / Explore) helps you choose services and find ready-made solution guides."}],"n":37,"slug":"37-codecatalyst"},{"title":"Amazon Q Developer CLI","lead":"An AI assistant that lives in your terminal, where DevOps work actually happens. You'll learn what makes it different from a chat window (agents, context, hooks, trusted tools), how to install it for free, and how it handled two real tasks: writing Terraform for a secure S3 bucket and fixing a broken Kubernetes pod.","sections":[{"h":"What it is, and why the terminal?","id":"s1","t":"Amazon Q Developer CLI is an agentic AI assistant for your command line. \"Agentic\" means it doesn't just answer. With your permission it can also do things: run shell commands, read and write files, call AWS, or run kubectl. Typical uses: Creating infrastructure (a Kubernetes cluster, Terraform for AWS resources). Getting unstuck: \"why is this deployment failing?\" Understanding large codebases (application code, IaC, maintenance scripts). This is useful when you have to troubleshoot a build or deployment for code you didn't write. Many AI assistants live inside an IDE (code editor). DevOps engineers, though, spend most of their day in the terminal: editing YAML manifests, running shell and Git commands, troubleshooting dev or production, and calling AWS with the CLI. Q Developer CLI brings the assistant to that terminal."},{"h":"The features that make it useful","id":"s2","t":"Multiple agents Create a specialist for each job, such as a Terraform agent, a Kubernetes agent or a Git agent. Each one has its own instructions, context and allowed tools. Context management Give the AI your rules so its output fits your setup instead of generic boilerplate. Context can be set at three levels (below). Agent hooks Run shell commands automatically at set moments (e.g. when the agent starts, or each time you send a prompt) and feed their output in as context. Strong models It runs on Anthropic Claude models. Claude Sonnet 4 was the default when this was written. Good reasoning matters for multi-step DevOps tasks. Three levels of context Level How long it lasts Example Agent resources Every session that uses that agent Files listed in the agent's config, e.g. AmazonQ.md, README.md, rule files under .amazonq/rules/ Session context Only the current chat /context add instructions.md Knowledge base Across sessions; built for big codebases Index a large repo so the agent can search it Rule files are also how you set guardrails, for example \"never touch these directories\" or \"only run these commands\". Your prompt goes to the model along with the agent's instructions and context. When the model wants to act, the agent asks your permission and then runs tools on your machine."},{"h":"Install and sign in (free)","id":"s3","t":"You don't need a paid AWS account. Sign in with a free AWS Builder ID (a personal AWS developer profile, separate from any AWS account). Go to the Amazon Q Developer \"Get started\" page → Command line. Download the installer for your OS (a .dmg on macOS; Linux packages are also available). Install it, then sign in with your Builder ID (q login, or follow the prompt on first launch). Run q chat. The banner tells you which model you're chatting with. # interactive chat q chat > generate linux command to list files # one-shot, non-interactive (handy in scripts) q chat --no-interactive \"generate linux command to list files\" NoteQ doesn't need your AWS keys separately. When it runs AWS or Terraform commands, they use whatever you already set up with aws configure (~/.aws/credentials). See Lesson 12: AWS CLI. Things change fastIn November 2025 Amazon Q Developer CLI was renamed Kiro CLI. Running q update upgrades you, your settings carry over, and the q command still works. The ideas in this lesson apply to Kiro CLI, but some command names and flags may differ, so check the Kiro docs."},{"h":"Working with agents and context","id":"s4","t":"# inside q chat /agent help # list, create, edit, set a default… /agent create --name demo-terraform-agent # starts from a default config /agent list # start a new chat using that agent q chat --agent demo-terraform-agent # add a file to this session's context /context add instructions.md The instructions.md in the example was short: - Always use the us-east-1 region. - Always add tags to resources, e.g. Name = <resource-name>. Hooks are set in the agent's JSON config. For example, a hook can run git status when the agent starts so it always knows what's changed: \"hooks\": { \"agentSpawn\": [ { \"command\": \"git status\" } ], \"userPromptSubmit\": [ { \"command\": \"kubectl get pods -A\" } ] }"},{"h":"Trust: the safety switch","id":"s5","t":"Whenever the agent wants to use a tool, such as writing a file or running a command, it asks first: y: allow it this one time. t: trust this tool for the rest of the session, so it won't ask again. n: deny. Watch outTrust carefully, especially when your terminal is pointed at a real AWS account or production cluster. Read what the agent is about to run. \"Trust always\" is convenient for reading files but risky for commands that delete or change things. Try it: a simulated Q CLI session Simulated, not a real AI. Pick a scenario and click Next to go through it. When the agent asks for permission, choose how to answer."},{"h":"Real task 1: Terraform for a secure S3 bucket","id":"s6","t":"With demo-terraform-agent and the instructions file in context, the prompt was: \"generate terraform code to create S3 bucket with all the best practices\". The agent asked to write a file (allowed with t) and produced something like this: provider \"aws\" { region = \"us-east-1\" # from instructions.md } resource \"random_id\" \"suffix\" { byte_length = 4 } resource \"aws_s3_bucket\" \"secure\" { bucket = \"my-secure-bucket-${random_id.suffix.hex}\" tags = { Name = \"my-secure-bucket\" } # tag format from instructions.md } resource \"aws_s3_bucket_versioning\" \"secure\" { bucket = aws_s3_bucket.secure.id versioning_configuration { status = \"Enabled\" } } resource \"aws_s3_bucket_server_side_encryption_configuration\" \"secure\" { bucket = aws_s3_bucket.secure.id rule { apply_server_side_encryption_by_default { sse_algorithm = \"AES256\" } } } resource \"aws_s3_bucket_public_access_block\" \"secure\" { bucket = aws_s3_bucket.secure.id block_public_acls = true block_public_policy = true ignore_public_acls = true restrict_public_buckets = true } Then the usual terraform init → plan → apply (see Lesson 26). In the S3 console, the bucket had the requested tag, was in us-east-1, and had versioning, encryption and Block Public Access turned on (these settings are explained in Lesson 11). The agent followed the context file instead of guessing."},{"h":"Real task 2: troubleshooting a broken pod","id":"s7","t":"A local kind cluster (Kubernetes running in Docker on your laptop) was given a deployment with an image tag that doesn't exist: kind create cluster --name demo-cluster-q kubectl create deployment nginx --image=nginx:doesnotexist kubectl get pods # STATUS: ErrImagePull / ImagePullBackOff A k8s-agent was created with session context saying \"act as a Kubernetes troubleshooting engineer and provide solutions\". Asked \"what is the issue with this pod?\", it ran kubectl (after a trust prompt), found the invalid image tag, and suggested a fix: kubectl set image deployment/nginx nginx=nginx:latest kubectl get pods # STATUS: Running New to pods and deployments? See Lesson 24: EKS. TipAI suggestions are a starting point. In the demo, the human ran the fix command. Keep it that way for production systems, and keep your IaC and cluster changes in Git."},{"h":"Check yourself","id":"s8","t":"You want every chat with your Terraform agent to remember \"always tag resources\". Which is the best place for that rule? Agent resources persist across sessions. /context add lasts only for the current session. The agent asks to run a command and you press t. What happens? y = once, t = trust for the session, n = deny. How does Q CLI get permission to create AWS resources through Terraform? Commands it runs use your existing local AWS credentials. The Builder ID only signs you in to Q itself."},{"h":"Key takeaways","id":"s9","t":"Amazon Q Developer CLI is an agentic AI assistant in the terminal. It can explain, generate, and (with permission) run commands. Create separate agents per job (Terraform, Kubernetes, Git), each with its own instructions and tools. Context comes in three levels: agent resources (persistent), session (/context add), and knowledge base (large codebases). Hooks add command output automatically. It's free to start with an AWS Builder ID. Run q chat, or q chat --no-interactive \"…\" for one-shot use. Use trust (y / t / n) carefully, and review what it generates before applying it to real environments."}],"n":38,"slug":"38-amazon-q-cli"},{"title":"ElastiCache: Redis vs Valkey","lead":"A cache can make a slow app fast by keeping frequently used data in memory. Here you'll learn how caching works, what Valkey is and why many teams are moving to it from Redis, how to create an ElastiCache Serverless cache for Valkey in a few clicks, and how to upgrade an existing Redis cache with no downtime.","sections":[{"h":"What is a cache?","id":"s1","t":"A cache is a small, very fast store that keeps copies of data you read often, so you don't have to fetch it from a slower place (usually a database) every time. Think of it like…A kitchen. The database is the supermarket: it has everything, but every trip takes time. The cache is your fridge: it's small, but grabbing milk takes a second. You keep what you use most in the fridge and restock it when it runs out. Caches like Valkey and Redis are in-memory key-value stores: In-memory: data is kept in RAM, not on disk, so reads take well under a millisecond inside AWS. Key-value: you store and fetch data by a name (the key), like product:42 → {\"name\":\"Shoes\",\"price\":59}. Cache hit, cache miss, and TTL The most common pattern is called cache-aside (or \"lazy loading\"): Cache-aside: check the cache first. On a miss, read the database and save the answer in the cache for next time. Cache hit: the key is in the cache, so the answer comes back fast. Cache miss: the key isn't there. The app reads the database (slow) and then stores the result in the cache. TTL (time to live): how long a cached item stays before it expires. A short TTL keeps data fresher. A long TTL gives more hits. When memory fills up, the cache evicts (removes) items, usually the least recently used ones. Try it: cold vs warm cache Request products and watch the latency. The first request for each product is a miss (cold cache). Repeat requests are hits until the TTL runs out. Times are simulated. TTL: 15 seconds No requests yet. Caches aren't only for speeding up database reads. The same engines are also used for session storage (who's logged in), message queues and pub/sub, leaderboards and counters, and sometimes as a primary database for fast, simple data."},{"h":"Redis vs Valkey: what happened?","id":"s2","t":"Redis has been the most popular in-memory key-value store for years. Then its license changed: 2024: Redis moved from the permissive open-source BSD license to \"source-available\" licenses. These are not open source in the traditional sense, and they restrict how cloud providers can offer it. Valkey was created as a fork (a copy that continues separately) of the last open-source Redis version. It is backed by the Linux Foundation and keeps the permissive BSD license. 2025: Redis added another license option, AGPL, an open-source license with strong \"share your changes\" rules. Two license changes in a short time created uncertainty. Companies don't want licensing surprises in their cache, queue or database layer. That is a big reason many have moved to Valkey. Redis Valkey License Changed twice recently (source-available, then also AGPL) BSD: very permissive, staying open source under the Linux Foundation Community Run by Redis Ltd. Fast-growing open community with many contributors Compatibility The original Compatible with Redis commands and APIs (from the fork point), so existing clients and code keep working Performance Fast Valkey 8 added better multi-threaded I/O. Benchmarks often show higher throughput On ElastiCache \"Redis OSS\" engine \"Valkey\" engine: AWS's recommended choice and lower priced"},{"h":"Amazon ElastiCache","id":"s3","t":"ElastiCache is AWS's managed caching service. AWS runs the servers, patching, failover and backups for you. It supports three engines: Valkey, Redis OSS and Memcached (a simpler cache without the extra data types). You can run it two ways: Serverless No servers or node sizes to choose. It scales automatically and you pay for data stored and requests made. AWS handles infrastructure and maintenance with no downtime. For Valkey it's priced about 33% lower than the other engines. Node-based (self-designed) You pick node types, the number of shards and replicas. This gives more control and predictable capacity. Valkey nodes are about 20% lower in price than the other engines. Create a serverless Valkey cache Console → ElastiCache → Get started → choose Valkey (the recommended option). Choose Serverless → New cache. Give it a name and description, e.g. valkey-demo. Engine version: pick the latest major version (8 at the time of writing). Review the defaults: encryption in transit and at rest, the VPC/subnets, and the security groups (they must allow your app on port 6379). Click Create. Wait 2–3 minutes for status Available. Copy the endpoint. Connect and try it The cache lives inside your VPC, so you connect from something in that VPC. The easiest way is the cache's Connectivity and security section → Connect to cache. It opens AWS CloudShell (a browser terminal) with the valkey-cli ready. Copy the command it shows. Serverless caches require TLS: valkey-cli -h valkey-demo-xxxxxx.serverless.use1.cache.amazonaws.com -p 6379 --tls > PING PONG > SET xkey \"hello elasticache\" OK > GET xkey \"hello elasticache\" > SET session:abc \"user42\" EX 60 # expires after 60 seconds (TTL) OK From application code, use a client library. Valkey GLIDE is the recommended official client (Python, Java, Node.js and more), and existing Redis client libraries also work."},{"h":"Benchmarking Redis OSS vs Valkey","id":"s4","t":"To compare the two fairly, run the same test against a Redis OSS serverless cache and a Valkey serverless cache. The test script ran about 10,000 SET, GET and DELETE operations from an EC2 instance in the same VPC, changing only the endpoint between runs. It measured: Throughput: operations per second. Higher is better. p50 latency: half of all requests were faster than this (the \"typical\" request). p90 / p99 latency: 90% / 99% of requests were faster than this. p99 shows the slow \"tail\" that your unluckiest users feel, which is why benchmarks focus on it. In that test (repeated over many runs), Valkey had higher SET/GET/DELETE throughput and a better p99 latency. Treat this as one example: always benchmark with your workload before deciding."},{"h":"Upgrading Redis OSS → Valkey with zero downtime","id":"s5","t":"Already have an ElastiCache Redis OSS cache? You don't need to rebuild it: Open the cache in the ElastiCache console and click Upgrade to Valkey. Pick the Valkey engine version (e.g. 8) and confirm. The cache keeps serving traffic during the upgrade. When the status returns to Available, it's running Valkey. Because Valkey is API-compatible with Redis, your app keeps using the same endpoint, commands and client code. TipDelete test caches when you're done. Serverless caches charge for stored data and requests, so a forgotten one will show up on your bill (Lesson 36). Interview angle\"Your product page is slow and the database CPU is high. What do you do?\" Put a cache (ElastiCache) in front of the database using cache-aside with a sensible TTL, and watch the hit ratio and p99 latency. Mention the trade-off: cached data can be slightly stale."},{"h":"Check yourself","id":"s6","t":"The first request for product:7 takes 90 ms, and the second takes 1 ms. Why? Cache-aside: miss → read DB → store in cache. The next read is served from memory. What's the main reason companies are moving from Redis to Valkey? Licensing stability is the main driver. A growing community and good performance help too. Valkey stays Redis-compatible. You upgrade an ElastiCache Redis OSS cache to Valkey. What must you change in your application? The in-place upgrade keeps the endpoint, and Valkey is API-compatible with Redis."},{"h":"Key takeaways","id":"s7","t":"A cache keeps hot data in memory. Hits are fast. Misses go to the database and then fill the cache. TTL controls how long data stays. Valkey is an open-source (BSD), Linux Foundation fork of Redis. It's Redis-compatible and growing fast. Redis's license changes pushed many teams toward it. ElastiCache offers Valkey, Redis OSS and Memcached, either serverless (no capacity planning) or node-based. Valkey is the recommended and cheaper engine. Connect from inside the VPC (e.g. CloudShell via \"Connect to cache\") using valkey-cli --tls. In code, use Valkey GLIDE or a Redis client. Compare with throughput and p50/p90/p99 latency. Upgrading Redis OSS → Valkey is a one-click, zero-downtime change."}],"n":39,"slug":"39-elasticache-valkey"},{"title":"EKS Auto Mode","lead":"Running Kubernetes is easy to start and hard to keep running well: sizing nodes, scaling, patching, upgrades and cost. EKS Auto Mode hands most of that work to AWS. You'll learn which problems it solves, how it uses Karpenter to add and remove nodes, and exactly what you still manage yourself.","sections":[{"h":"","id":"","t":"This lesson assumes you know what an EKS cluster, nodes and pods are. If not, start with Lesson 24: EKS, and for why orchestration exists, Lesson 23."},{"h":"The problems with running Kubernetes yourself","id":"s1","t":"Problems show up in two phases. Day 1 is setting the cluster up. Day 2 is everything after that, which never ends. Day 1: creating the cluster Capacity planning: how many worker nodes (the EC2 machines that run your pods): 3, 6, 10? And how big should each one be (16 CPU / 16 GB, or more)? Teams new to Kubernetes have no data to base this on. Instance type: EC2 has many families (general, memory-optimized, GPU…). The wrong choice hurts app performance. AI/ML workloads make this harder because you also have to pick GPU instances and use them efficiently. Setup effort: by hand, that means a VPC with subnets, route tables, internet and NAT gateways, and then EKS, with every step checked for mistakes. With IaC such as Terraform, you also have to manage state files, locking, and many .tf files. Day 2: keeping it healthy Node management: picture an online shop with 3 nodes on weekdays, 5 at weekends and 8 during a sale. Every scale-up means adding nodes, joining them to the cluster and spreading pods onto them. Every scale-down means cordoning and draining nodes (moving pods off safely) before removing them. Some teams do this every day and every night. Security fixes: the node operating systems need patches for vulnerabilities, all the time. Version upgrades: Kubernetes releases patch versions often (1.33.0 → 1.33.1 → …) and new minor versions regularly. You can't stay on one version forever. Add-on compatibility: after each upgrade, core components such as the CNI (pod networking), CoreDNS (in-cluster DNS) and CSI drivers (storage) must still be compatible. Cost: teams over-provision \"just in case\", and rarely go back to scale down or rebalance when usage drops."},{"h":"How EKS Auto Mode solves them","id":"s2","t":"One-click, ready-to-use cluster Choose Quick configuration and click create. Networking, storage drivers, a load-balancing controller, DNS and metrics come built in. Automatic nodes with Karpenter No node groups to size. Auto Mode launches the right number and type of nodes for your pods, and removes them when they're no longer needed. Patched, upgraded nodes Nodes run Bottlerocket-based AMIs (a minimal, container-focused OS) that AWS patches, tests and rolls out. Nodes are replaced regularly (never older than 21 days), so you never patch nodes by hand. Compatible core components Pod networking, DNS, storage (EBS) and load-balancer integration are built in and kept compatible by AWS when you upgrade. Karpenter in one minute Karpenter is an open-source node autoscaler. AWS built it and donated it to the Kubernetes/CNCF community. Instead of scaling fixed node groups, it looks at pending pods (pods that can't be scheduled because no node has room), works out what they need, and launches the cheapest EC2 instance that fits. It also keeps checking for consolidation: if pods could fit on fewer or cheaper nodes, it moves them and removes the extra nodes. In Auto Mode, Karpenter is already running and managed by AWS, so you don't install or upgrade it. Auto Mode adds a right-sized node when pods are waiting and removes it once it's empty, with no human involved."},{"h":"Who manages what?","id":"s3","t":"Compare the three ways to run EKS worker nodes. Self-managed nodes are EC2 instances you build and join to the cluster yourself. Managed node groups are groups of EC2 nodes that EKS creates for you, but you still choose and size them. Auto Mode is described above. Try it: who manages what? Pick a node option and see who's responsible for each job. NoteEven with Auto Mode, you still own your applications: deployments, sensible CPU/memory requests (Karpenter sizes nodes from these), IAM permissions, and choosing when to upgrade the cluster's Kubernetes version. Auto Mode also charges a management fee per node on top of the normal EC2 price."},{"h":"Hands-on: create a cluster and watch it scale","id":"s4","t":"1. Create the cluster Console → Elastic Kubernetes Service → Create cluster → Quick configuration (with EKS Auto Mode). First time only: create a cluster IAM role and a node IAM role using Create recommended role. The wizard pre-selects EKS / EKS Auto Cluster and the right policies. You can reuse both roles for future Auto Mode clusters. (Roles are explained in Lesson 3.) Click Create. After a few minutes the status is Active and the API server endpoint is shown. Look around in the console. The Resources tab shows system pods already running on a node that Auto Mode launched (its compute shows as auto mode). Default services such as kubernetes and the metrics service are present, and storage drivers for EBS/EFS are listed. The cluster is ready to use. 2. Point kubectl at it and watch the nodes aws eks update-kubeconfig --name <cluster-name> --region ap-south-1 # in a second terminal: live view of nodes, pods, CPU use and cost eks-node-viewer eks-node-viewer is a small open-source tool from AWS Labs. It shows each node with its instance type, price, CPU use, and running and pending pods. It's the easiest way to watch Karpenter work. 3. Deploy a lot of microservices The OpenTelemetry demo installs 20+ microservices, which is enough to make the cluster grow: helm repo add open-telemetry https://open-telemetry.github.io/opentelemetry-helm-charts helm install my-otel-demo open-telemetry/opentelemetry-demo kubectl get pods # most pods Pending at first… What happened: 24 pods were Pending (only the 2 existing system pods were running). Karpenter launched a new node and chose m6a.large as the best fit for those pods, with about 73% of its CPU used. It chose the instance type itself. A minute or two later, kubectl get pods showed them Running. 4. Remove them and watch the cost go away helm uninstall my-otel-demo CPU use dropped to 0 and no pods were left on that node. After a few minutes the node moved to Deleting, and shortly afterwards it was gone. That's what makes Auto Mode cost-effective: you don't keep paying for an empty node. TipDelete the cluster when you're done practicing. The EKS control plane is billed per hour even with no nodes (see Lesson 36). Interview angle\"Managed node groups vs Karpenter vs Auto Mode?\" Node groups: AWS runs the node lifecycle, but you size them and install autoscaling and add-ons. Karpenter (self-installed): flexible, cost-aware autoscaling, but you operate it. Auto Mode: AWS runs Karpenter, patched Bottlerocket nodes and core add-ons for you, at the cost of less low-level control and a per-node fee."},{"h":"Check yourself","id":"s5","t":"In EKS Auto Mode, what decides that a new m6a.large node should be launched? Karpenter watches for pods that can't be scheduled and launches the cheapest instance type that fits them. Who patches the worker node operating system in Auto Mode? AWS continuously patches, tests and rolls out the node AMIs, and nodes are replaced regularly. After helm uninstall, the node stays \"Ready\" for a minute and then shows \"Deleting\". What is this? Once a node is empty (or its pods fit elsewhere), Karpenter consolidates it away."},{"h":"Key takeaways","id":"s6","t":"Running Kubernetes means Day-1 work (sizing, instance types, setup) and Day-2 work (scaling, patching, upgrades, add-on compatibility, cost). EKS Auto Mode gives a ready-to-use cluster in one click, with networking, DNS, storage and load-balancer integration built in. It uses Karpenter to launch the cheapest instance that fits pending pods and to consolidate nodes that aren't needed. Nodes use AWS-patched Bottlerocket AMIs and are replaced regularly, so there's no manual node patching. You still own your apps, pod resource requests, IAM, and when to upgrade. Managed node groups leave sizing, autoscaling and add-ons to you."}],"n":40,"slug":"40-eks-auto-mode"}];
